+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 1 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l1">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L1</span><h2>Async &amp; the Event Loop</h2><span class="tag">how one worker serves thousands</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The core idea</h3>
+      <p>Your app spends most of its life <strong>waiting</strong> — for the database, the internet, the AI. <strong>Async</strong> is the trick of doing other useful work <em>during those waits</em>, using just <strong>one worker</strong>.</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍽️ Analogy — one waiter, many tables</span>
+        A restaurant with <strong>one waiter</strong>. The slow way: take Table 1's order, then <em>stand in the kitchen</em> until it's cooked before touching Table 2. The smart way: hand Table 1's order to the kitchen and <strong>immediately</strong> serve Table 2 while it cooks; deliver when the bell rings. One waiter, many tables — because the <em>cooking</em> (waiting) is the slow part, not the waiter.
+        <br><br>
+        <strong>Waiter</strong> = one thread · <strong>Tables</strong> = requests · <strong>Kitchen cooking</strong> = I/O wait · <strong>“serve another table while it cooks”</strong> = <code>await</code>.
+      </div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Sync vs Async — see the difference</h3>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>Synchronous — the worker sits idle during every wait (slow)</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Req A</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">wait (DB)</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span></div></div>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Req B</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg idle" style="flex:4">…B waits for A to fully finish…</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">wait</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time →</div>
+      </figure>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>Asynchronous — one thread overlaps the WAITS (fast)</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Req A</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">wait (DB)</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span></div></div>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Req B</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg idle" style="flex:1">·</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">wait</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">work</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time → &nbsp; (while A waits on the DB, the thread runs B)</div>
+        <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--work)"></i>work (using CPU)</span><span><i style="background:var(--wait)"></i>waiting on I/O</span><span><i style="border:1px dashed var(--line)"></i>idle / not started</span></div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Key insight</span>Async doesn't make one task faster — it stops the worker from <strong>sitting idle</strong>, so many tasks finish in the same wall-clock time.</div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>What is the “event loop”?</h3>
+      <p>The <strong>event loop</strong> is the waiter's brain: a <code>while True:</code> that runs forever asking one question — <em>“who's ready for their next step?”</em> — and running whoever is ready.</p>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>The event loop cycle</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode accent">EVENT LOOP · one thread, runs forever</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">pick a task that is <strong>READY</strong></div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">run it until it says <code>await</code></div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">is the awaited thing ready?</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">NO</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">park it · “wake me later”</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">YES</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">keep running it</div></div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">pick the next ready task &nbsp;↺ repeat</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">4</span>What happens at <code>await</code>?</h3>
+      <p><code>await</code> means: <strong>“this might take a while — pause me, go help others, wake me when my result is ready.”</strong></p>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>await = pause here, resume later</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Your task</div><div class="lb">runs → hits <code>await db.fetch()</code> → <strong>“not ready, park me”</strong> → &nbsp;💤&nbsp; → woken with result → continues</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Event loop (meanwhile)</div><div class="lb">runs <strong>other tasks</strong> while the DB works → DB result arrives → resumes your task where it paused</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The subtle part (bites people later)</span>Between two <code>await</code>s your code runs <strong>uninterrupted</strong> — nobody touches your variables. But <strong>across an <code>await</code>, the world can change</strong> (other tasks ran while you were paused). This is the seed of the “async race conditions” we'll handle in later stages.</div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">5</span>The #1 sin: blocking the loop</h3>
+      <p>Because there's <strong>one</strong> worker, if a task refuses to <code>await</code> and does something slow <em>synchronously</em>, the whole app <strong>freezes</strong>.</p>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>Polite waiting vs blocking the one thread</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">GOOD</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg work" style="flex:1">A work</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">A awaits →</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">B runs</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">C runs</span></div></div>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">BAD</span><div class="tl">
+          <span class="seg work" style="flex:1">A work</span><span class="seg blocked" style="flex:4">🔒 time.sleep(5) — whole loop FROZEN — B,C,D all stuck</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time →</div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">🚫 The rule</span>In async code, never call a <strong>blocking</strong> function. Use the async version:
+        <br><code>time.sleep()</code> ❌ → <code>await asyncio.sleep()</code> ✅ &nbsp;·&nbsp; <code>requests.get()</code> ❌ → <code>await httpx.get()</code> ✅ &nbsp;·&nbsp; sync DB driver ❌ → <strong>async</strong> driver ✅ &nbsp;·&nbsp; heavy CPU work → push to a separate worker pool.</div>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍽️ Analogy</span>Blocking = the waiter walks into the kitchen and <em>personally cooks</em> one dish while every other table sits ignored.</div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">6</span>Async is NOT parallelism</h3>
+      <p>Everything above happens on <strong>one worker, one CPU core</strong>. Async overlaps <em>waiting</em>, not <em>computing</em>.</p>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>Two different tools for two different problems</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">ASYNC = concurrency</div><div class="lb">1 waiter, many tables. Overlaps <strong>WAITS</strong>. → Use for <strong>I/O-bound</strong> work (DB, network, AI). <em>This is your whole platform.</em></div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--violet)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--violet)">PARALLEL = many cooks</div><div class="lb">Many workers/cores. Overlaps <strong>COMPUTING</strong>. → Use for <strong>CPU-bound</strong> work (heavy math). Needs multiple <strong>processes</strong>.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🛠️ Your DevOps bridge</span>This is exactly how <strong>nginx / Envoy</strong> serve tens of thousands of connections on a few threads with an epoll/kqueue event loop. And “blocking the loop” is the same “one bad call wedged the whole pod” incident you've debugged — now you know the mechanism.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet — the whole lesson in 8 lines</span>
+        <ul>
+          <li>App spends most time <strong>waiting</strong> → async fills those waits with other work.</li>
+          <li>1 thread + event loop = one waiter serving many tables.</li>
+          <li><code>await</code> = “pause me, help others, wake me when ready.”</li>
+          <li>Between awaits: uninterrupted. Across an await: the world may have changed.</li>
+          <li>A blocking call on the loop = the whole app freezes (cardinal sin).</li>
+          <li>Fix: always use async versions (<code>asyncio.sleep</code>, <code>httpx</code>, async DB driver).</li>
+          <li>async = concurrency (overlap <strong>waits</strong>), NOT parallelism (overlap <strong>CPU</strong>).</li>
+          <li>I/O-bound → async. &nbsp; CPU-bound → processes.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>In the waiter analogy, what is the “kitchen cooking,” and what is <code>await</code>?</li>
+          <li>Why does async help an app that talks to a DB and an API, but not one doing heavy math?</li>
+          <li>A teammate writes <code>time.sleep(3)</code> in an async function. What happens to the other 500 users, and what should they have written?</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="review">
+        <div class="review-h"><span class="ct">🧭 My answers &amp; feedback</span><span class="score">Understanding 3/3 · Precision ~9/10</span></div>
+        <div class="qa">
+          <div class="q"><span class="vb ok">✅</span><span>Q1 · What is “kitchen cooking,” and what is <code>await</code>?</span></div>
+          <p class="ans"><b>My answer:</b> Kitchen cooking = I/O wait (DB / network / AI calls). <code>await</code> = tell the waiter (thread) not to idle while food cooks — go take other orders.</p>
+          <p class="fb"><b>Correct.</b> Precision: <code>await</code> is the point where the task hands control <em>back to the event loop</em> — that's what frees it to serve others.</p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="qa">
+          <div class="q"><span class="vb warn">⚠️</span><span>Q2 · Why does async help I/O apps but not heavy math?</span></div>
+          <p class="ans"><b>My answer:</b> One thread never idles, handling several tasks at once; heavy math needs multiple processors (multiple cooks).</p>
+          <p class="fb"><b>Right conclusion — one word to fix:</b> async is <b>concurrent</b> (interleaved on ONE thread — one thing at a time, overlapping the <em>waits</em>), <b>not parallel</b>. Heavy math is CPU-bound: the CPU is busy the whole time so there are <em>no waits to overlap</em>, and Python's GIL runs one thread at a time anyway → you need multiple <b>processes</b>.</p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="qa">
+          <div class="q"><span class="vb ok">✅</span><span>Q3 · A teammate writes <code>time.sleep(3)</code> in an async function.</span></div>
+          <p class="ans"><b>My answer:</b> The one thread freezes for 3s; B, C, D… all wait. Use <code>asyncio.sleep(3)</code> so the request pauses without holding the thread.</p>
+          <p class="fb"><b>Correct.</b> Precision: <code>asyncio.sleep</code> <em>yields to the event loop</em>, which is why the other ~500 users keep being served during those 3s.</p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 2 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l2">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L2</span><h2>Types &amp; Pydantic</h2><span class="tag">labels for data + a guard at the door</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>How a Python variable really works</h3>
+      <p>A variable name is just a <strong>sticky tag you put on a box (an object)</strong>. The <em>type lives on the box, not the tag</em> — and you can peel the tag off and stick it on a different box anytime.</p>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>A variable is a tag you stick on an object</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode"><code>x</code> &nbsp;──tag──▶&nbsp; [ int · 5 ]</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ reassign — just move the tag</div>
+          <div class="fnode"><code>x</code> &nbsp;──tag──▶&nbsp; [ str · "hi" ]</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <p>This is <strong>dynamic typing</strong>. Breezy for scripts, but risky at scale: a wrong-type value rides silently through five functions and explodes far away. Application Python tames this with <strong>types</strong>.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Type hints are <em>labels</em> — ignored at runtime</h3>
+      <p>You label functions: <code>def add(a: int, b: int) -&gt; int</code>. But the Python interpreter <strong>does not enforce</strong> them — <code>add("x","y")</code> still runs.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📦 Analogy</span>A type hint is like writing <strong>“FRAGILE — GLASS”</strong> on a box. The label doesn't physically stop someone packing a brick — it's guidance for <strong>humans and tools</strong>. Two “guards” read those labels for you 👇</div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Guard #1 — <code>mypy</code> (the inspector, before the flight)</h3>
+      <p><code>mypy</code> reads your labels and checks your code <strong>before it runs</strong>: <code>add("x", 1)</code> → <span style="color:var(--blocked)">✗ expected int, got str</span>.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛫 Analogy</span><strong>mypy = the airport inspector who X-rays every labeled box at check-in</strong> — catching “you said glass but packed a brick” <em>before</em> the plane departs, not at 30,000 ft (3am in prod). Put it in CI and a whole class of bugs dies at dev time.</div>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">🔤 The typing words you'll use</span>
+        <code>list[int]</code>, <code>dict[str, float]</code> · <code>str | None</code> (optional) · <code>Literal["pending","running","done"]</code> (only these exact values) · <code>Protocol</code> (“anything with these methods fits” — for ports/adapters).</div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">4</span>Guard #2 — Pydantic (customs, at the border)</h3>
+      <p>Hints don't check <strong>incoming data</strong>. When a client sends <code>{"priority":"abc"}</code>, plain Python won't stop it landing in an int field. At a <strong>trust boundary</strong> (API request, config, third-party data) you need <strong>runtime</strong> checking — that's <strong>Pydantic v2</strong>: it parses, validates, and coerces, and rejects bad input loudly.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛃 Analogy</span><strong>Pydantic = the customs officer at the border</strong> — opens each incoming bag, checks it matches the declared contents, converts where sensible, and loudly rejects what's wrong. (v2's core is Rust → fast; <strong>FastAPI uses it automatically</strong> → clean <code>422</code> for free.)</div>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>How a request enters your app</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">Incoming JSON (untrusted) · <code>{"prompt":"hi","priority":"3"}</code></div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">PYDANTIC — parse · validate · coerce</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">VALID</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">clean typed object → your code</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">INVALID</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">ValidationError → 422 to client</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">5</span>The pattern: validate at the edges, trust the inside</h3>
+      <figure>
+        <figcaption>Two zones, two guards</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--violet)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--violet)">EDGE — untrusted</div><div class="lb">API requests, config files, third-party responses → <strong>Pydantic validates here, once.</strong></div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">CORE — trusted</div><div class="lb">Already-validated typed objects flow freely → <strong>mypy checks your code</strong>; no repeated runtime validation.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛂 Analogy</span>Airport security screens you <strong>once</strong> at the entrance; inside the secure zone you're trusted — nobody re-screens at every gate. Pydantic at the doors, plain typed objects inside.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🛠️ Your DevOps bridge</span>This is <strong>JSON Schema / OpenAPI request validation / Terraform variable type constraints</strong> — the same idea, now living inside your Python at its boundary.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span>
+        <ul>
+          <li>A variable is a tag; the type lives on the <strong>object</strong>, not the name (dynamic typing).</li>
+          <li>Type hints are labels — the interpreter <strong>ignores</strong> them at runtime.</li>
+          <li><strong>mypy</strong> = static inspector: catches type bugs <strong>before</strong> you run (put it in CI).</li>
+          <li><strong>Pydantic</strong> = runtime customs at the boundary: parse, coerce, reject bad input.</li>
+          <li>Use <strong>both</strong>: mypy for your code, Pydantic for untrusted input.</li>
+          <li>Pattern: <strong>validate at the edges, trust the typed core.</strong></li>
+          <li>Handy types: <code>list[int]</code>, <code>str | None</code>, <code>Literal[...]</code>, <code>Protocol</code>.</li>
+          <li>FastAPI uses Pydantic automatically → clean 422 on bad requests.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>Is a Python type attached to the <em>name</em> or the <em>object</em>? Why does that make hints “not enforced” at runtime?</li>
+          <li>You receive a webhook from Stripe. Which guard applies — mypy, Pydantic, or both — and <em>where exactly</em> does each one act?</li>
+          <li>Why is <code>status: Literal["pending","running","done"]</code> safer than <code>status: str</code>?</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 3 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l3">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L3</span><h2>Generators &amp; Coroutines</h2><span class="tag">the machinery under async</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The idea: a function that can pause</h3>
+      <p>Normal functions run start-to-finish and forget everything. A <strong>generator</strong> can <strong>pause in the middle, hand back a value, and later resume exactly where it left off</strong> — remembering all its variables.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍬 Analogy — a Pez dispenser</span>A normal function dumps the whole bag of candy at once. A generator is a <strong>Pez dispenser</strong>: press once → one candy (<code>yield</code>), it pauses; press again → the next one. It produces values <strong>one at a time, on demand (lazily)</strong>, so it never has to hold the whole bag in memory.</div>
+      <figure><figcaption>yield = pause &amp; hand back a value</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">call generator → runs to first <code>yield 1</code> → <strong>pauses</strong>, returns 1</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ ask for next</div>
+          <div class="fnode">resumes after the yield → runs to <code>yield 2</code> → pauses, returns 2</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ …until it finishes</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Coroutines = the same trick, for async</h3>
+      <p>An <code>async def</code> function is a <strong>coroutine</strong> — it uses the <em>exact same “pause &amp; resume” machinery</em> as a generator. The only difference: a generator pauses at <code>yield</code> to hand back <strong>data</strong>; a coroutine pauses at <code>await</code> to hand back <strong>control</strong> to the event loop (Lesson 1!).</p>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Key insight</span>Calling <code>async def f()</code> does <strong>nothing</strong> by itself — it just creates a coroutine object sitting there, inert. It only runs when the event loop <em>awaits/schedules</em> it. (Like a recipe card: writing it doesn't cook anything; a chef has to pick it up.)</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties back to L1</span>“<code>await</code> pauses my task and lets others run” works <em>because</em> coroutines are pausable functions. Now you know the gears inside the event loop.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Generator = a function that pauses at <code>yield</code>, produces values lazily (one at a time).</li>
+        <li>It remembers its place &amp; variables between resumes.</li>
+        <li>Coroutine (<code>async def</code>) = same pause/resume machinery, pauses at <code>await</code>.</li>
+        <li><code>yield</code> hands back <strong>data</strong>; <code>await</code> hands back <strong>control</strong>.</li>
+        <li>Calling a coroutine returns an inert object — it runs only when awaited/scheduled.</li>
+        <li>Generators save memory (lazy); great for streaming large data.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>What do a generator and a coroutine have in common under the hood?</li>
+        <li>You write <code>result = my_async_fn()</code> and nothing happens. Why — and what's missing?</li>
+        <li>When would a generator save you memory vs a normal function returning a list?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 4 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l4">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L4</span><h2>Context Managers</h2><span class="tag">borrow → use → always return</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: things you must clean up</h3>
+      <p>Files, database sessions, network connections, locks — you <strong>open/acquire</strong> them, use them, and <strong>must release</strong> them. If an error happens mid-use and you forget to release, you <strong>leak</strong> resources (connections pile up, the app dies).</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📚 Analogy — a library book</span>A context manager (<code>with</code>) is like borrowing a library book that <strong>returns itself automatically the moment you leave</strong> — even if you trip and fall on the way out (an exception). You literally cannot forget to return it.</div>
+      <figure><figcaption>the <code>with</code> block guarantees cleanup</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode accent"><code>with open(f) as file:</code> &nbsp;→&nbsp; ACQUIRE (<code>__enter__</code>)</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">use the resource…</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">SUCCESS</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">RELEASE (<code>__exit__</code>)</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">ERROR</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">RELEASE anyway (<code>__exit__</code>)</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span><code>async with</code> for async resources</h3>
+      <p>Async resources (a DB session, an HTTP client) use <code>async with</code> (backed by <code>__aenter__</code>/<code>__aexit__</code>). You'll use this constantly in Stage 0.5 for the SQLAlchemy session — open a session, do work, and it commits/closes safely even on error.</p>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Two ways to make one</span>A class with <code>__enter__</code>/<code>__exit__</code>, or the easy way: decorate a generator with <code>@contextlib.contextmanager</code> — the code before <code>yield</code> is “acquire,” after is “release.” (Notice: generators show up again!)</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li><code>with</code> guarantees cleanup runs — even if an exception is thrown.</li>
+        <li>Mechanism: <code>__enter__</code> (acquire) → body → <code>__exit__</code> (release, always).</li>
+        <li><code>async with</code> = the async version (<code>__aenter__</code>/<code>__aexit__</code>) for DB sessions, connections.</li>
+        <li>Build one via a class, or <code>@contextmanager</code> on a generator.</li>
+        <li>Use for: files, DB sessions, locks, connections — anything you must release.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why is <code>with open(...)</code> safer than opening a file and calling <code>.close()</code> yourself?</li>
+        <li>What runs in <code>__exit__</code>, and when is it guaranteed to run?</li>
+        <li>Why would a DB session use <code>async with</code> instead of plain <code>with</code>?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 5 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l5">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L5</span><h2>Decorators</h2><span class="tag">wrap a function to add behavior</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The idea: gift-wrapping a function</h3>
+      <p>In Python, functions are just objects you can pass around. A <strong>decorator</strong> takes a function, <strong>wraps it in an extra layer</strong> that runs code before/after, and returns the wrapped version — <em>without changing the original function's code</em>.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎁 Analogy — gift wrapping</span>The gift inside is unchanged; you add a layer around it. Decorators add <strong>cross-cutting behavior</strong> — logging, timing, auth checks, retries, caching — the same way, to many functions, without copy-pasting that logic into each.</div>
+      <figure><figcaption>a decorator wraps the call</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">call arrives</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">DECORATOR — “before” (e.g. start timer, check auth)</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">the original function runs</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">DECORATOR — “after” (e.g. log duration)</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>You'll see these everywhere</h3>
+      <p><code>@app.get("/jobs")</code> (FastAPI routes), <code>@pytest.fixture</code> (tests), <code>@property</code>, <code>@lru_cache</code> — all decorators. Tip: use <code>functools.wraps</code> inside your decorator so the wrapped function keeps its real name and docs (otherwise debugging gets confusing).</p>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🛠️ Your DevOps bridge</span>A decorator is basically <strong>middleware</strong> for a single function — the same “run something before/after” idea as nginx/Envoy filters or HTTP middleware, at function scope.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Functions are objects → you can wrap them.</li>
+        <li>A decorator takes a function, returns a wrapped one that adds before/after behavior.</li>
+        <li><code>@name</code> above a function applies the decorator.</li>
+        <li>Great for cross-cutting concerns: logging, timing, auth, retry, caching.</li>
+        <li>Use <code>functools.wraps</code> to preserve the original name/docs.</li>
+        <li>FastAPI &amp; pytest are built on decorators.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>In one sentence, what does a decorator do to a function?</li>
+        <li>Name two cross-cutting concerns that are perfect for a decorator, and why.</li>
+        <li>Why bother with <code>functools.wraps</code>?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 6 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l6">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L6</span><h2>Dataclasses vs Pydantic</h2><span class="tag">two kinds of “data box”</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Two containers for structured data</h3>
+      <p>Both hold structured data with named, typed fields. The difference is <strong>whether they validate</strong>:</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>trusted core vs untrusted edge (ties to L2)</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">@dataclass — plain box</div><div class="lb">Auto-generates <code>__init__</code>/<code>__repr__</code>/<code>__eq__</code>. <strong>No validation.</strong> Use for <strong>trusted, internal</strong> data you already checked. Fast, zero-dependency. <code>frozen=True</code> makes it immutable.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--violet)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--violet)">Pydantic model — box + inspector</div><div class="lb">Same fields, but <strong>validates &amp; coerces at construction</strong>, and serializes to/from JSON. Use at the <strong>boundary</strong> (API input, config, external data).</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📦 Analogy</span>A <strong>dataclass</strong> is a labeled box you pack yourself (you trust the contents). A <strong>Pydantic model</strong> is a box with a customs inspector who checks everything going in. Don't pay for the inspector on internal boxes you already trust.</div>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Rule of thumb</span>Untrusted input crosses a boundary → <strong>Pydantic</strong>. Internal, already-validated domain object → <strong>dataclass</strong>. (This is the “validate at the edges, trust the core” pattern from L2, made concrete.)</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Both = typed, named-field containers.</li>
+        <li><code>@dataclass</code>: no validation, fast, for trusted internal data; <code>frozen=True</code> = immutable.</li>
+        <li>Pydantic: validates + coerces + (de)serializes; for untrusted boundary data.</li>
+        <li>Pick by <em>where the data comes from</em>: edge → Pydantic, core → dataclass.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Your worker passes an already-validated job object between two internal functions. Dataclass or Pydantic?</li>
+        <li>Why not just use Pydantic for everything?</li>
+        <li>What does <code>frozen=True</code> buy you?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 7 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l7">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L7</span><h2>Testing — pytest &amp; Hypothesis</h2><span class="tag">your safety net</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why tests (the real reason)</h3>
+      <p>Tests aren't about proving code works today — they're a <strong>safety net</strong> that lets you change code tomorrow <em>without fear</em>. For a long-lived platform, that confidence is everything.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧯 Analogy</span>Tests are <strong>smoke alarms</strong> throughout the house. You don't notice them daily, but the moment you break something, one goes off — right next to the problem, not after the house burns down in production.</div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The pytest toolkit</h3>
+      <figure><figcaption>the four tools you'll use constantly</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">fixtures</div><div class="lb">Reusable <strong>setup/teardown</strong> run before/after tests (a DB, a client, sample data). The “prep kitchen” that lays out ingredients so each test starts clean.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">parametrize</div><div class="lb">Run the <strong>same test with many inputs</strong> automatically — one test, a table of cases (a spice rack of inputs).</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">mocking</div><div class="lb">Replace slow/external things (DB, Claude API) with a <strong>stunt double</strong> so tests are fast, deterministic, and free.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">async tests</div><div class="lb"><code>pytest-asyncio</code> lets you <code>await</code> inside tests — needed to test your async code.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Hypothesis — the robot that breaks your code</h3>
+      <p>Normal tests check examples <em>you</em> think of. <strong>Property-based testing</strong> (Hypothesis) flips it: you state a <strong>property that must always hold</strong> (“encoding then decoding returns the original”), and Hypothesis throws <strong>hundreds of weird inputs</strong> at it to find a counterexample — then shrinks it to the smallest failing case.</p>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Why it's powerful</span>It finds the edge cases you'd never think to write — empty strings, huge numbers, unicode, zero. Great for parsers, encoders, and pure logic.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Tests = a safety net for fearless change, not busywork.</li>
+        <li><strong>fixtures</strong> = setup/teardown &amp; dependency injection for tests.</li>
+        <li><strong>parametrize</strong> = same test, many inputs.</li>
+        <li><strong>mocking</strong> = fake the slow/external stuff (DB, APIs).</li>
+        <li><code>pytest-asyncio</code> = test async code.</li>
+        <li><strong>Hypothesis</strong> = state a property; it hunts counterexamples.</li>
+        <li>Test pyramid: many fast unit tests, fewer integration, fewest end-to-end.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>What's the real value of a test suite for a system you'll change for months?</li>
+        <li>When you test a function that calls the Claude API, what do you do to that call, and why?</li>
+        <li>Give one property (not an example) you could hand to Hypothesis for a “parse job id” function.</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 8 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l8">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L8</span><h2>Async SQLAlchemy 2.0</h2><span class="tag">talking to Postgres without blocking</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The three pieces</h3>
+      <figure><figcaption>engine · session · ORM</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Engine</div><div class="lb">The <strong>phone line</strong> to the database — a <strong>connection pool</strong> it reuses (opening a new DB connection per request is expensive).</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Session (unit of work)</div><div class="lb">Your <strong>shopping cart</strong>: add/change/delete objects, then <code>commit()</code> applies them all in one <strong>transaction</strong> (or <code>rollback()</code> undoes everything).</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">ORM</div><div class="lb">A <strong>translator</strong> between Python objects and SQL rows — you work with a <code>Job</code> object, it writes the SQL.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛒 Analogy</span>The session is a shopping cart: you toss items in as you shop, and <strong>nothing is actually bought until checkout</strong> (<code>commit</code>). If you change your mind, empty the cart (<code>rollback</code>) — the store shelves (DB) are untouched.</div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why <em>async</em> here matters</h3>
+      <p>Use the <strong>async driver</strong> (<code>asyncpg</code>) with <code>AsyncSession</code> and <code>async with</code>. Reason: a DB query is I/O — with a <em>sync</em> driver it would <strong>block the event loop</strong> (Lesson 1's cardinal sin!) and freeze every other request. The async driver lets the loop serve others while the DB works.</p>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to Stage 0</span>You'll hide the session behind a <strong>repository</strong> (e.g., <code>JobRepository.get(id)</code>) so your business logic never touches SQL directly — that's the “ports &amp; adapters” idea you'll formalize in Stage 0.</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Watch out</span>The <strong>N+1 query problem</strong>: looping over 100 jobs and lazily loading each one's rows = 101 queries. Load them together (eager loading) instead. Classic performance trap.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Engine = connection pool (reused DB connections).</li>
+        <li>Session = unit of work / shopping cart; <code>commit</code> = checkout, <code>rollback</code> = undo.</li>
+        <li>ORM = maps Python objects ↔ SQL rows.</li>
+        <li>Use the <strong>async driver</strong> (<code>asyncpg</code>) + <code>async with</code> so DB calls don't block the loop.</li>
+        <li>Hide it behind a <strong>repository</strong> (keeps SQL out of business logic).</li>
+        <li>Beware the <strong>N+1</strong> query trap — load related rows together.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why must a DB call in an async app use an async driver? (tie it to Lesson 1)</li>
+        <li>What does <code>commit()</code> vs <code>rollback()</code> do, in the shopping-cart analogy?</li>
+        <li>What is the N+1 problem, and how do you avoid it?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 9 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l9">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L9</span><h2>Alembic Migrations</h2><span class="tag">version control for your database</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: the schema must change over time</h3>
+      <p>You'll add columns, tables, indexes as the app grows. But the database already has <strong>real data</strong> — you can't just drop and recreate it. You need <strong>ordered, repeatable, reversible</strong> changes that every environment (your laptop, staging, prod) applies identically.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🗂️ Analogy — git for your schema</span>Alembic is <strong>version control for the database structure</strong>. Each migration is like a commit: a numbered step with an <code>upgrade()</code> (go forward) and <code>downgrade()</code> (roll back). The chain of migrations = the schema's history.</div>
+      <figure><figcaption>a chain of ordered, reversible steps</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">v1 · create <code>jobs</code> table</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ upgrade &nbsp;/&nbsp; ▲ downgrade</div>
+          <div class="fnode">v2 · add <code>priority</code> column</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ / ▲</div>
+          <div class="fnode">v3 · add index on <code>status</code></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Autogenerate — with a warning</h3>
+      <p>Alembic can <strong>compare your ORM models to the actual DB</strong> and auto-write a candidate migration. Huge time-saver — but <strong>always read what it generated</strong> before applying (it can miss renames or generate destructive steps). Golden rule: <strong>never edit a migration that has already shipped</strong> — add a new one.</p>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Migration = a versioned, ordered, reversible schema change (like a git commit).</li>
+        <li><code>upgrade()</code> = apply; <code>downgrade()</code> = revert.</li>
+        <li>Autogenerate diffs models vs DB → candidate migration (always review it!).</li>
+        <li>Run migrations in deploy/CI so every environment matches.</li>
+        <li>Never edit an already-shipped migration — write a new one.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why can't you just recreate the database when the schema changes?</li>
+        <li>What are <code>upgrade()</code> and <code>downgrade()</code> for?</li>
+        <li>Why must you review an auto-generated migration before running it?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- ================= LESSON 10 ================= -->
+    <section class="lesson" id="l10">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">L10</span><h2>Project Hygiene — uv, venv, pyproject</h2><span class="tag">reproducible, isolated projects</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Virtual environments — isolation</h3>
+      <p>A <strong>virtual environment</strong> is a private, isolated set of installed packages <em>per project</em>, so Project A's libraries never clash with Project B's (or your system Python).</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧰 Analogy</span>A venv is a <strong>clean, dedicated workshop</strong> for one project — its own tools on the bench. You don't share one messy garage across every project and hope nothing conflicts.</div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>uv, lockfiles &amp; pyproject.toml</h3>
+      <p><strong>uv</strong> is a fast, modern tool that creates the venv, installs dependencies, and (crucially) writes a <strong>lockfile</strong> — the exact pinned versions of every package. That makes installs <strong>reproducible</strong>: your machine, a teammate's, and CI all build the <em>identical</em> environment. <code>pyproject.toml</code> is the project's spec sheet (name, deps, dev-deps, tooling config).</p>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Why the lockfile matters</span>“Works on my machine” bugs usually mean different package versions. A lockfile makes the environment <strong>exact and repeatable everywhere</strong> — the same reproducibility instinct you already have from Docker images and Terraform.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>venv = isolated packages per project (no cross-project clashes).</li>
+        <li><code>uv</code> = fast tool for venv + install + lockfile (modern pip/venv replacement).</li>
+        <li>Lockfile = exact pinned versions → reproducible installs everywhere.</li>
+        <li><code>pyproject.toml</code> = project metadata, dependencies, tool config.</li>
+        <li>Separate runtime deps from dev deps (pytest, mypy).</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>What problem does a virtual environment solve?</li>
+        <li>What does a lockfile guarantee that a loose dependency list doesn't?</li>
+        <li>Which of your DevOps habits does this map onto?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <div class="stage-banner"><span>▽ Stages 0 → 12 ▽</span></div><div class="stage-banner" id="s0"><span>◆ STAGE 0 — FASTAPI &amp; CLEAN ARCHITECTURE (full lessons) ◆</span></div>
+
+    <!-- S0·1 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·1</span><h2>FastAPI — the async front door</h2><span class="tag">accept · validate · document</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>What it is &amp; why we pick it</h3>
+      <p>FastAPI is a modern Python web framework that receives HTTP requests, <strong>validates the input</strong> (using Pydantic, from L2), runs your handler, and returns a response — and it's <strong>async-native</strong> (from L1).</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛎️ Analogy — a super-efficient receptionist</span>She (1) <strong>checks every form against a template</strong> before accepting it (rejects bad forms at the desk = Pydantic → 422), (2) <strong>juggles many visitors at once</strong> instead of finishing one before greeting the next (async), and (3) <strong>auto-writes the instruction manual</strong> for every service (OpenAPI docs at <code>/docs</code>).</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>the request path through FastAPI</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">HTTP request arrives</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">match the route (e.g. <code>POST /jobs</code>)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">validate body with Pydantic</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">VALID</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">your <code>async</code> handler runs → response</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">INVALID</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">auto <strong>422</strong> with clear errors (you write nothing)</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Two features you'll lean on</h3>
+      <p><strong>Typed params</strong>: path/query/body are declared with types and validated for free. <strong>Dependency Injection</strong> (<code>Depends</code>): shared things — a DB session, the current user — are declared once and injected into handlers, keeping them clean and testable.</p>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to L1 &amp; L2</span>Async (L1) is <em>why</em> FastAPI serves many slow LLM requests on few workers; Pydantic (L2) is the customs officer at the door. FastAPI simply wires them into the web layer.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>FastAPI = async + typed + auto-docs web framework (runs on ASGI / uvicorn).</li>
+        <li>Validates request bodies via Pydantic → clean <strong>422</strong> on bad input, before your code.</li>
+        <li>Typed path/query/body params; auto OpenAPI at <code>/docs</code>.</li>
+        <li><code>Depends</code> = dependency injection (DB session, auth) → clean, testable handlers.</li>
+        <li>It's the <em>adapter</em> at the edge; keep business logic out of the handler.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>How does FastAPI reject an invalid request body <em>before</em> your handler runs?</li>
+        <li>Why is an async framework the right fit for an LLM gateway? (tie to L1)</li>
+        <li>What problem does <code>Depends</code> solve?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- S0·2 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·2</span><h2>Hexagonal architecture (ports &amp; adapters)</h2><span class="tag">keep the core pure</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The idea: a pure core, swappable edges</h3>
+      <p>Put your <strong>business logic in the center</strong> (the domain). It never imports FastAPI or SQLAlchemy. It talks to the outside world only through <strong>ports</strong> (interfaces). The outside world (web, DB) plugs in as <strong>adapters</strong> that implement those ports.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔌 Analogy — a power socket</span>Your appliance (the domain) doesn't care if the electricity comes from solar or the grid — it just plugs into the <strong>socket (port)</strong>. You can swap the power source (Postgres → a fake DB for tests) without rewiring the appliance.</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>dependencies point INWARD</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode" style="border-color:var(--tier-deep)">ADAPTERS (outer) · FastAPI in · SQLAlchemy out</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ depends on</div>
+          <div class="fnode" style="border-color:var(--gold)">APPLICATION · use-cases / services (orchestrates)</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ depends on</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">DOMAIN CORE · pure rules · defines PORTS (interfaces) · imports nothing</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The one rule</span>Arrows only point <strong>inward</strong>. The core defines an interface like <code>JobRepository</code>; the SQLAlchemy adapter <em>implements</em> it. The core never knows SQLAlchemy exists.</div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why bother</h3>
+      <p><strong>Testability</strong> (swap the DB for an in-memory fake → fast unit tests). <strong>Framework independence</strong> (change web framework or DB without touching rules). <strong>Clarity</strong> (business logic isn't tangled with I/O).</p>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Domain core = pure business logic; imports no framework.</li>
+        <li><strong>Port</strong> = an interface the core defines (e.g. <code>JobRepository</code>).</li>
+        <li><strong>Adapter</strong> = an implementation of a port (FastAPI, SQLAlchemy).</li>
+        <li>Dependencies point inward; outer layers depend on inner, never the reverse.</li>
+        <li>Payoff: swap DB/framework &amp; test the core in isolation.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Which layer is allowed to import SQLAlchemy — and which is forbidden to?</li>
+        <li>What's a “port” vs an “adapter,” in your own words?</li>
+        <li>How does this make unit-testing the domain fast?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- S0·3 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·3</span><h2>Idempotency keys</h2><span class="tag">safe retries</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: everything retries</h3>
+      <p>A client sends <code>POST /jobs</code>, the network hiccups, the client retries — now you might create <strong>two</strong> jobs. An <strong>idempotency key</strong> (a unique id the client sends) makes the repeat safe: same key → same single result.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎟️ Analogy — a concert ticket</span>Scanning your ticket twice still gets you <strong>one seat</strong>, not two. Or an elevator button: pressing it 5 times doesn't summon 5 elevators.</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>the dedup check</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">request arrives with <code>idempotency_key</code></div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">look up the key in a store</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">SEEN BEFORE</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">return the <strong>stored</strong> result (no new job)</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">NEW</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">process once · store result under the key</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Why it's foundational</span>Once you add a queue (L/Stage 1), retries are everywhere. Idempotency is the seatbelt that makes “at-least-once” delivery safe.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Client sends a unique key per logical request.</li>
+        <li>Store key → result; a repeat returns the stored result (no duplicate).</li>
+        <li>Turns unsafe retries into safe ones.</li>
+        <li>Essential companion to at-least-once delivery &amp; queues.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why can't you rely on the client to “just not retry”?</li>
+        <li>What do you store, and what happens on a repeat key?</li>
+        <li>Which later feature makes idempotency non-optional?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- S0·4 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·4</span><h2>Optimistic concurrency control (OCC)</h2><span class="tag">no lost updates, no locks</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The lost-update problem</h3>
+      <p>Two requests read the same job (version 3), both change it, both save — the second silently overwrites the first. <strong>OCC</strong> prevents this with a <strong>version number</strong>: you save only if the version is still what you read; otherwise you re-read and retry.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📝 Analogy — Google Docs / Wikipedia</span>If someone saved an edit between your read and your write, you get “this changed — reload.” You don't silently clobber their work; you re-base on the latest and try again.</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>compare-and-set on version</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Writer A</div><div class="lb">reads version=3 → writes “WHERE version=3” → ✅ succeeds → version becomes 4</div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Writer B (concurrent)</div><div class="lb">also read version=3 → writes “WHERE version=3” → ❌ 0 rows (it's 4 now) → <strong>re-read &amp; retry</strong></div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Optimistic vs pessimistic</span><strong>Optimistic</strong> = assume conflicts are rare, detect &amp; retry (no locks, high concurrency). <strong>Pessimistic</strong> = lock the row first (safe but serializes writers, risks deadlocks). OCC fits web apps where conflicts are rare.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Add a <code>version</code> column; bump it on every write.</li>
+        <li>Update “<code>WHERE id=? AND version=?</code>”; 0 rows updated = conflict → retry.</li>
+        <li>Prevents the “lost update” bug without locking.</li>
+        <li>Optimistic (detect+retry) vs pessimistic (lock first).</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>What exactly is the “lost update” problem?</li>
+        <li>How does the version check catch a concurrent write?</li>
+        <li>When would you choose pessimistic locking instead?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- S0·5 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·5</span><h2>12-factor config</h2><span class="tag">one build, many environments</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Config lives in the environment</h3>
+      <p>Never hard-code URLs, credentials, or flags. Read them from <strong>environment variables</strong>, so the <em>same</em> built artifact runs unchanged in dev, staging, and prod — only the env differs.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔌 Analogy — a travel appliance</span>The same laptop charger works worldwide; you just change the <strong>plug adapter</strong> per country. The appliance (your build) is identical; the plug (config) changes per environment.</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>same image, different env</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">ONE build artifact</div><div class="lb">the exact same Docker image, unchanged</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">+ dev env vars</div><div class="lb">dev DB URL, debug on → runs as <strong>dev</strong></div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">+ prod env vars</div><div class="lb">prod DB URL, secrets from Vault → runs as <strong>prod</strong></div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🛠️ Your DevOps bridge</span>This is the same principle behind a single container image promoted across environments, and 12factor.net is the canonical checklist. You already live this with Helm values &amp; ConfigMaps.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Config in env vars, not in code.</li>
+        <li>No secrets in the repo — inject at runtime (later: Vault).</li>
+        <li>One build promoted across dev/staging/prod.</li>
+        <li>Backing services (DB, cache) are attached resources via config.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why must the same build run in every environment?</li>
+        <li>Where do secrets belong, if not in code?</li>
+        <li>Name one 12-factor habit you already practice in K8s.</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- S0·6 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">0·6</span><h2>Correlation IDs &amp; consistent errors</h2><span class="tag">trace one request everywhere</span></div>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Follow a single request across the system</h3>
+      <p>Attach a unique <strong>correlation id</strong> (e.g. <code>X-Request-ID</code>) to each request at the edge, propagate it to every service and log line, so you can reconstruct one request's whole journey by searching that id.</p>
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📦 Analogy — a courier tracking number</span>One number follows your parcel through every hub — pickup, sorting, transit, delivery. Search it and you see the entire journey. Without it, debugging is asking every hub “did you see a brown box?”</p></div>
+      <figure><figcaption>the id flows through everything</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode accent">edge assigns <code>X-Request-ID: abc123</code></div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">gateway logs it · passes it on</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">worker · DB calls · all log <code>abc123</code></div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">grep <code>abc123</code> → the request's full story (across services + traces)</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>One error shape</h3>
+      <p>Return errors in a single consistent structure — e.g. <code>{code, message, request_id}</code> — so clients handle failures predictably and every error is traceable back to its logs.</p>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Sets up Stage 12</span>Correlation ids are the seed of <strong>distributed tracing</strong> (OpenTelemetry). Add them now and observability later is almost free.</div>
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Assign a request id at the edge; propagate everywhere.</li>
+        <li>Log it on every line for that request.</li>
+        <li>Return one uniform error shape (code, message, request_id).</li>
+        <li>Foundation for tracing (OTel) in Stage 12.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>How does a correlation id turn a multi-service debug into a single search?</li>
+        <li>Why standardize the error response shape?</li>
+        <li>Which Stage-12 capability does this quietly enable?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section><div class="stage-banner" id="s1"><span>◆ STAGE 1 — ASYNC CORE: QUEUE · STATE MACHINE · EVENT SOURCING ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·1</span><h2>Accept vs Process (202 + id)</h2><span class="tag">respond fast, work later</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: slow work must not block the request</h3><p>An agent job runs for seconds to minutes. If the HTTP handler waited for it, every in-flight job would hold a connection open — and a burst of traffic would exhaust your connection pool and the server would stop accepting <em>anything</em>. You have to answer the client in milliseconds regardless of how long the work takes.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The handshake: 202 + a job id</h3><p>So you <strong>split accepting from processing</strong>. The gateway validates the request, writes a job row, immediately returns <strong>202 Accepted + a job id</strong>, and drops the job onto a queue. The heavy lifting happens later in a background worker. The id is the client's claim ticket.</p><figure><figcaption>the accept path (milliseconds)</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">validate body (Pydantic)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">write job row (status=pending)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">enqueue the job</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">return 202 + id</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>How the client gets the result</h3><p>With just an id, the client either <strong>polls</strong> <code>GET /jobs/{id}</code> until it's done, or <strong>streams</strong> live updates over SSE. Either way the request that submitted the work returned instantly — the system stays responsive no matter how slow the job is.</p><figure><figcaption>blocking vs 202</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Blocking (bad)</div><div class="lb">Handler waits for the whole job → connections pile up → server chokes under load.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">202 + id (good)</div><div class="lb">Handler returns instantly; work runs in the background; client checks back by id.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧺 Analogy — the dry-cleaner</span>They take your clothes and hand you a <strong>numbered ticket instantly</strong> — they don't clean them at the counter while you stand there. You return (or get a text) when it's ready.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The id is a contract</span>That job id is how the client tracks, polls, streams, and cancels the work later. It's also what makes the request safely retryable when combined with idempotency.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to S0·3</span>A retried submit must not create two jobs — so the 202 handshake pairs with the <strong>idempotency key</strong> from Stage 0. Same key → same job, even if the client sends it twice.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Split accept (fast) from process (slow).</li><li>Submit → validate → write job → 202 + id → enqueue.</li><li>Client polls GET /jobs/{id} or streams via SSE.</li><li>Keeps the API responsive under slow work.</li><li>Pair with idempotency so retried submits don't duplicate.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why would processing inline eventually crash the server under load?</li><li>What does the client receive immediately, and how does it get the result?</li><li>Which Stage-0 feature makes a retried submit safe?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·2</span><h2>Durable Queue</h2><span class="tag">work that survives a crash</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>A buffer between fast producers and slow consumers</h3><p>The gateway produces jobs quickly; workers consume them slowly. A <strong>durable queue</strong> sits between them, absorbing the mismatch. Producers never wait on consumers, and consumers pull at their own pace.</p><figure><figcaption>producer → durable queue → workers</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">gateway enqueues jobs fast</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">durable queue persists them</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">workers pull one at a time</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Durability &amp; acknowledgement</h3><p>“Durable” means a job isn't lost if a worker crashes. The worker <strong>acknowledges (acks)</strong> a message only <em>after</em> it finishes processing. If it dies mid-job before acking, the queue <strong>redelivers</strong> the message to another worker — nothing is dropped (this is why at-least-once + idempotency, 1·5, matter).</p><figure><figcaption>what happens on a crash</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Acked</div><div class="lb">Worker finished + acked → message removed. Done.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--wait)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--wait)">Un-acked</div><div class="lb">Worker died before acking → message redelivered to another worker.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Load leveling — spikes become queue depth</h3><p>A traffic spike doesn't overwhelm the workers; it just makes the queue <strong>longer</strong>. Workers drain it steadily. This “queue-based load leveling” is what keeps the system stable under bursts — the queue is a shock absorber.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏭 Analogy — a conveyor belt</span>Orders drop onto a belt; cooks take the next when free. If a cook steps away (worker restarts), the orders <strong>stay on the belt</strong>. A rush just makes the belt longer.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Ack timing is a design choice</span>Ack <em>after</em> success = at-least-once (safe, may duplicate). Ack <em>before</em> = at-most-once (may lose work). For jobs you almost always want ack-after + idempotent processing.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Redis Streams now, Kafka later</span>You start with Redis Streams for simplicity; Stage 5 swaps in Kafka when you need partitioned scale, replay, and multiple independent consumers.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Queue = persistent buffer decoupling producers from consumers.</li><li>Ack after processing → redelivery on crash (nothing lost).</li><li>Load leveling: spikes become queue depth, not overload.</li><li>Ack-after = at-least-once; ack-before = at-most-once.</li><li>Redis Streams to start; Kafka in Stage 5.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What two problems does the queue solve between gateway and workers?</li><li>What happens to an un-acked message if the worker crashes?</li><li>How does the queue absorb a traffic spike?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·3</span><h2>Job State Machine</h2><span class="tag">only legal transitions</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Define the states — and forbid the impossible ones</h3><p>A job lives in one of a fixed set of states, and can only move along <strong>defined transitions</strong>. Making the states and moves explicit means illegal states simply can't happen — the code won't let a job jump from <code>pending</code> straight to <code>done</code>.</p><figure><figcaption>the job lifecycle</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">pending</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">running</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">done / failed</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">(pending|running) → cancelled</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Guards reject illegal moves</h3><p>Every transition is guarded: attempting an undefined move (e.g. <code>done → running</code>) must <strong>raise</strong>, not silently corrupt state. The state machine is a hard guardrail that turns “update the status field” into “make a <em>valid</em> transition.”</p><figure><figcaption>legal vs illegal</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Legal</div><div class="lb">pending→running, running→done, running→failed, pending→cancelled.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Illegal → reject</div><div class="lb">done→running, failed→done, done→pending. Raise, don't mutate.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Each transition is an event</h3><p>Crucially, every legal transition <strong>emits an event</strong> (JobStarted, JobSucceeded…). That's the bridge to event sourcing (1·4): the state machine decides <em>what's allowed</em>; the event log records <em>what happened</em>. Terminal states (done/failed/cancelled) are final — no transitions out.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📦 Analogy — parcel tracking</span>“accepted → in transit → delivered.” A parcel can't teleport to “delivered” without “in transit.” The tracking page only ever shows a real, ordered stage.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Don't just set a status column</span>A bare status field lets any code write any value, from anywhere — the source of “how did this job end up running <em>and</em> done?” bugs. The state machine centralizes and validates every change.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Feeds the event log</span>Each valid transition produces the event that 1·4 stores. State machine = rules; event log = history.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Fixed states; only defined transitions allowed.</li><li>Guard &amp; reject illegal transitions (raise).</li><li>Every transition emits an event.</li><li>Terminal states (done/failed/cancelled) are final.</li><li>Centralizes state changes — no scattered status writes.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why forbid illegal transitions instead of just updating a status field?</li><li>List the legal transitions out of running.</li><li>What should happen if code attempts done → running?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·4</span><h2>Event Sourcing</h2><span class="tag">store the story, derive the state</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Store every change, not just the latest value</h3><p>Instead of overwriting a job's status, you <strong>append every change as an immutable event</strong> (JobCreated, JobStarted, JobSucceeded…). The event log is the source of truth; the “current status” is something you <em>compute</em>, not something you store.</p><figure><figcaption>events are truth; state is derived</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">append: JobCreated → JobStarted → JobSucceeded</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">fold / replay the events</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">current state = done (computed)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Deriving state by folding the log</h3><p>To know a job's current state, you <strong>replay</strong> its events from the start, applying each to build up the state — a “fold.” Because you kept every step, you can reconstruct the state at <em>any point in time</em>, not just now.</p><figure><figcaption>two ways to model state</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">State-oriented</div><div class="lb">Store only current status; overwrite on change → history is lost.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Event-sourced</div><div class="lb">Store every change; derive state by replay → full history kept.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The superpowers — and the cost</h3><p>You get a perfect <strong>audit trail</strong> (“what happened to job X and when?” is always answerable), <strong>time-travel debugging</strong>, and the ability to build brand-new read-models by replaying (this is the seed of CQRS in Stage 5). The cost: you derive state instead of reading it, so hot paths use periodic <strong>snapshots</strong> to avoid replaying millions of events.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏦 Analogy — a bank statement</span>Your bank stores every <strong>transaction</strong>, not just a balance. You can always see <em>how</em> you reached today's balance, audit any moment, and never lose history.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Snapshots keep it fast</span>Replaying the whole log every read is slow. Periodically save a snapshot of derived state, then replay only the events since the snapshot.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Sets up CQRS (Stage 5)</span>Because state is derived from events, you can project those same events into multiple read-optimized views — exactly what CQRS does.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Append-only event log is the source of truth.</li><li>Current state = fold/replay over events.</li><li>Full audit trail + time-travel + rebuildable read-models.</li><li>Use snapshots so hot reads don't replay everything.</li><li>Trade-off: more moving parts than a status column.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>In the bank analogy, what are the events and what is the balance?</li><li>How do you find a job's current status if you only store events?</li><li>Why do you need snapshots, and what do they store?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·5</span><h2>At-least-once &amp; Idempotent Consumers</h2><span class="tag">the exactly-once myth</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why the same message gets delivered twice</h3><p>Queues guarantee <strong>at-least-once</strong> delivery. The classic case: a worker processes a job successfully, then <strong>crashes before it can ack</strong>. The queue, never told it succeeded, redelivers the message — so the work runs a second time. Duplicates are not a bug to avoid; they're a reality to design for.</p><figure><figcaption>a duplicate is normal</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">worker processes the job ✓</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">crashes before acking ✗</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">queue redelivers → processed again</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Idempotent processing makes duplicates harmless</h3><p>An <strong>idempotent consumer</strong> keys its work by a stable id (event id / sequence). Before acting, it asks “have I already handled this id?” If yes, it's a no-op; if no, it processes once and records the id. Now delivering twice changes nothing.</p><figure><figcaption>dedup by a stable id</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">seen this event id before?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">YES</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">skip — no-op</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">NO</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">process once · record the id</div></div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The “exactly-once” myth</h3><p>Exactly-once <em>delivery</em> is impossible across an unreliable network — you can't both guarantee delivery and guarantee no duplicates. What you achieve is <strong>effectively-once</strong> = at-least-once delivery + idempotent processing. Saying this precisely is a senior signal.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔔 Analogy — a duplicate reminder</span>Your phone shows the same reminder twice. If the action is “mark done,” seeing it twice is harmless. If it were “charge the card,” duplication is a disaster — so you design it to be safe.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Ack only after success</span>If you ack before processing, a crash loses the work (at-most-once). Ack after, and make processing idempotent — that's the safe combination for jobs.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Same thread as S0·3</span>Idempotency keys (submit) and idempotent consumers (process) are the same principle at two layers: retries are everywhere, so make repeats harmless.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Queues deliver at-least-once → expect duplicates.</li><li>Idempotent consumer: key by id; reprocessing is a no-op.</li><li>Ack only after successful processing.</li><li>“Exactly-once delivery” is a myth → aim for effectively-once.</li><li>Effectively-once = at-least-once + idempotency.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Give a realistic reason the same message is delivered twice.</li><li>How does an idempotent consumer make that harmless?</li><li>What's the honest truth about exactly-once, and how do you approximate it?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">1·6</span><h2>Cancellation</h2><span class="tag">stopping work in flight</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why long jobs must be stoppable</h3><p>Agent jobs can run for minutes and cost real money. Users change their minds; runaway jobs must be stoppable. But you can't just kill a worker mid-operation — that risks half-written state and leaked resources.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Cooperative cancellation</h3><p>So cancellation is <strong>cooperative</strong>: a cancel request appends a <code>JobCancelled</code> signal, and the worker <strong>checks for it at safe checkpoints</strong> in the loop (e.g. between agent steps). It won't abandon a half-finished operation, but before starting the next step it sees the signal and stops.</p><figure><figcaption>cooperative cancellation</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">POST /jobs/{id}/cancel → append JobCancelled</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">worker checks the signal at each safe checkpoint</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">stop · clean up · record final state = cancelled</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Clean up, then record the ending</h3><p>On seeing the signal the worker releases resources (open connections, partial writes), then records the terminal <code>cancelled</code> state via the event log — so the job's history clearly ends in cancellation, not a mysterious stall.</p><figure><figcaption>two ways to stop</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Cooperative (good)</div><div class="lb">Signal + checkpoint checks → clean stop at a safe point.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Hard kill (bad)</div><div class="lb">Terminate mid-op → partial state, leaked resources.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍳 Analogy — a “cancel order” button</span>The kitchen checks the cancel board <strong>between steps</strong>. It won't abandon a half-cracked egg mid-motion, but before the next step it sees “cancelled,” stops, and clears the station.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Same idea as Go's context</span>Cooperative cancellation via a checked signal is exactly what Go's <code>context</code> does (Stage 7) — pass a cancel signal down and check it at boundaries.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Uses the state machine + event log</span>Cancellation is just another guarded transition ((pending|running) → cancelled) that emits an event — reusing 1·3 and 1·4.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Cancellation is cooperative: signal + checkpoint checks.</li><li>Never hard-kill mid-operation.</li><li>Clean up partial work before stopping.</li><li>Record the terminal cancelled state in the log.</li><li>Same pattern as Go's context cancellation.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is cancellation cooperative rather than an instant kill?</li><li>Where does the worker check for the cancel signal, and why there?</li><li>What must you do before recording the job as cancelled?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s2"><span>◆ STAGE 2 — POLYGLOT PERSISTENCE: MONGODB &amp; REDIS ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·1</span><h2>Why Polyglot Persistence</h2><span class="tag">the right store per data shape</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>No single database is good at everything</h3><p>Force everything into one database and you get either rigid schemas where you need flexibility, or weak guarantees where you need ACID. <strong>Polyglot persistence</strong> means deliberately using several stores, each matched to a kind of data.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Cosmos's data placement</h3><p>The mapping is driven by what each piece of data <em>is</em>: money and state need ACID; agent trajectories are nested and variable; counters need to be instant.</p><figure><figcaption>store per data shape</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">PostgreSQL</div><div class="lb">Jobs, tenants, quotas, ledger — the ACID source of truth.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">MongoDB</div><div class="lb">Agent trajectories &amp; variable-shape payloads (nested, evolving).</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Redis</div><div class="lb">Hot state, cache, rate-limit counters — microsecond reads.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The cost, and the interview framing</h3><p>The price of polyglot is a bigger operational surface (more systems to run and back up). The payoff is that each store does one thing excellently. Frame it as: “I put <em>money</em> in Postgres for ACID, <em>trajectories</em> in Mongo for flexibility, <em>counters</em> in Redis for speed.”</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧊 Analogy — fridge, toolbox, album</span>You don't keep milk, screwdrivers, and photos in one box. A fridge keeps things fresh (Postgres/ACID), a toolbox grabs the right tool fast (Redis), an album holds varied pages (Mongo).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Match on consistency vs flexibility vs speed</span>Ask of each dataset: does it need strong consistency (Postgres), flexible shape (Mongo), or raw speed (Redis)? That question picks the store.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The vector/object/OLAP stores too</span>Later you add pgvector/Qdrant (RAG, Stage 3), S3/MinIO (artifacts), and ClickHouse (analytics) — same principle, extended.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Polyglot = several DBs, each for its strength.</li><li>Postgres = ACID truth; Mongo = flexible docs; Redis = speed.</li><li>Choose by consistency vs flexibility vs speed.</li><li>Cost = more to operate; benefit = best guarantees + performance.</li><li>Write the “why polyglot” ADR.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Which store holds the billing ledger, and why?</li><li>Why not put everything in Postgres?</li><li>What single question helps you pick a store for a dataset?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·2</span><h2>Document Modeling — embed vs reference</h2><span class="tag">MongoDB's core decision</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Keep related data together, or link it?</h3><p>For each relationship in MongoDB you choose: put the related data <strong>inside</strong> one document (<em>embed</em>), or in a <strong>separate</strong> document you link to (<em>reference</em>). This single choice drives read speed, duplication, and update cost.</p><figure><figcaption>the two shapes for “a job and its steps”</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Embed</div><div class="lb">Steps inside the job doc → one read gets everything. Best when read together &amp; bounded.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--violet)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--violet)">Reference</div><div class="lb">Steps in their own collection, linked by job_id → best when large / shared / unbounded.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>When to embed</h3><p>Embed when the data is <strong>read together</strong>, <strong>owned</strong> by the parent, and <strong>bounded</strong> in size. One read returns the whole thing — fast and atomic. Downside: the document grows, and duplicated embedded data is painful to update in many places.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>When to reference (and the limit that forces it)</h3><p>Reference when the data is <strong>large, shared, or unbounded</strong> — e.g. an agent trajectory that can grow to thousands of steps. MongoDB caps a document at <strong>16MB</strong>, so unbounded lists must be referenced. The guiding question is always: <em>“what do I read together?”</em></p><figure><figcaption>the decision</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">is it read together &amp; bounded?</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">yes → embed</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">no (large/shared/unbounded) → reference</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍱 Analogy — combo meal vs menu number</span>Embed = a combo meal on one tray (everything you eat together, one grab). Reference = a menu number you look up separately (good when the item is huge or shared).</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Beware the giant document</span>Embedding an ever-growing list eventually hits the 16MB limit and makes every update rewrite a huge document. Unbounded → reference.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Model for queries, not for tidiness</span>Unlike relational normalization, Mongo modeling optimizes for your access patterns. Shape documents around how you'll read them.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Embed: read-together + bounded → one fast read.</li><li>Reference: large / shared / unbounded data.</li><li>16MB document limit forces unbounded lists to reference.</li><li>Model for query patterns, not normalization dogma.</li><li>Guiding question: what do I read together?</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>When is embedding the better choice?</li><li>Why does an ever-growing list push you toward a reference?</li><li>What hard limit can force a reference?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·3</span><h2>Aggregation Pipeline</h2><span class="tag">compute inside the database</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>An assembly line for your data</h3><p>The <strong>aggregation pipeline</strong> processes documents through ordered <strong>stages</strong>, each transforming the stream and passing it on — all inside MongoDB, so you don't drag raw data into your app to summarize it.</p><figure><figcaption>stages transform the stream</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">$match — filter</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">$group — aggregate (counts/sums)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">$sort — order</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">$project — shape output</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Common stages</h3><p><code>$match</code> filters, <code>$group</code> aggregates by a key (counts, sums, averages), <code>$sort</code> orders, and <code>$project</code> reshapes the final output. You chain them to turn raw documents into exactly the summary you need.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Performance: filter early, use indexes</h3><p>Put <code>$match</code> <strong>first</strong> so later stages process far fewer documents, and make sure that match can use an <strong>index</strong>. This is the same “push predicates down” instinct as SQL query planning — do the cheap filtering before the expensive grouping.</p><figure><figcaption>order matters</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Match early (good)</div><div class="lb">Filter to 1% of docs, then group → fast.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Match late (bad)</div><div class="lb">Group millions, then filter → wasteful.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏭 Analogy — an assembly line</span>Each station does one job (filter, group, reshape) and hands the item on. By the end, raw documents have become the exact summary you wanted.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Keep computation near the data</span>Aggregating in the DB moves far less data over the network than pulling everything into the app and looping — usually much faster.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Feeds dashboards</span>These pipelines produce the counts and rollups your ops console (Stage 9) and analytics need.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Ordered stages transform documents in the DB.</li><li>Common: $match → $group → $sort → $project.</li><li>Filter ($match) first, backed by an index.</li><li>Keeps heavy computation close to the data.</li><li>Push predicates down, like SQL planning.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why aggregate in the DB instead of the app?</li><li>Why put $match first?</li><li>What does $project do?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·4</span><h2>Change Streams</h2><span class="tag">react to data changes live</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>React, don't poll</h3><p>A <strong>change stream</strong> is a feed you subscribe to that emits an event whenever documents are inserted, updated, or deleted. Instead of repeatedly asking “anything new?” (polling), you're <strong>told</strong> the moment something changes.</p><figure><figcaption>subscribe once, react to every change</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">a doc is inserted/updated/deleted</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">change stream emits an event</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">subscriber reacts (cache, UI, trigger a job)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>What subscribers do with it</h3><p>Common reactions: invalidate/update a Redis cache, push a live update to the UI, or kick off downstream work. It turns the database into an event source other components can follow.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Resumability</h3><p>Change streams carry a <strong>resume token</strong> — if your subscriber disconnects, it can resume from where it left off instead of missing events. That makes them reliable enough to build features on, not just best-effort notifications.</p><figure><figcaption>poll vs stream</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Polling</div><div class="lb">Ask every N seconds → latency + wasted queries.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Change stream</div><div class="lb">Pushed on change → real-time, efficient, resumable.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔔 Analogy — a doorbell</span>Instead of walking to the door every 10 seconds to check (polling), you install a doorbell that rings only when someone actually arrives (a change event).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Resume tokens make it durable</span>Persist the last resume token so a restart continues from the right place — no missed or duplicated reactions.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Cousin of CDC (Stage 5)</span>This is the database-level version of Change-Data-Capture — react to changes without polling; Stage 5 does it at the Kafka/warehouse scale.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Subscribe to insert/update/delete events on a collection.</li><li>React in real time — no polling.</li><li>Uses: cache updates, live UI, triggering work.</li><li>Resume tokens make it recover after disconnects.</li><li>Conceptual cousin of CDC.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What problem do change streams solve vs polling?</li><li>Name two things a subscriber might do on a change.</li><li>What does a resume token give you?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·5</span><h2>TTL Indexes</h2><span class="tag">data that expires itself</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Auto-delete transient data</h3><p>A <strong>TTL (time-to-live) index</strong> tells MongoDB to automatically delete documents a set time after a timestamp field — no cron job, no cleanup code. The data cleans itself up.</p><figure><figcaption>set-and-forget cleanup</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">write doc with a timestamp + TTL index</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">time passes beyond the TTL</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">MongoDB background task deletes it</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>How it works (and its limits)</h3><p>A background task sweeps expired documents periodically — so deletion is <strong>approximate</strong>, not to-the-second. It's ideal for transient data but must not be relied on for exact timing or for data you actually need to keep.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>When to use it — and when not</h3><p>Great for sessions, caches, one-time tokens, and temp results. <strong>Don't</strong> use it for anything you must retain, and remember the timing is “soon after expiry,” not precise.</p><figure><figcaption>good vs bad fit</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Good fit</div><div class="lb">Sessions, caches, temp tokens, ephemeral results.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Bad fit</div><div class="lb">Anything durable, or where exact-second timing matters.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🥛 Analogy — milk with an expiry date</span>The carton effectively throws itself out once it's past date. TTL data cleans itself up so you never accumulate stale junk.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Approximate, background timing</span>Documents disappear <em>soon after</em> expiry, on the sweep — design around “roughly then,” not “exactly then.”</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Not a durability mechanism</span>TTL silently deletes. Never point it at data you might need later.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>TTL index auto-expires documents after a set time.</li><li>Set on a date field; a background task sweeps them.</li><li>Timing is approximate (near expiry, not exact).</li><li>Great for sessions/caches/temp data.</li><li>Never use it for data you must keep.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a TTL index save you from writing?</li><li>Is expiry exact to the second — why or why not?</li><li>Name two good use cases and one bad one.</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">2·6</span><h2>Redis Caching Patterns</h2><span class="tag">microsecond reads, safely</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why cache at all</h3><p>Some reads are expensive (a heavy query, a slow join) but requested often. <strong>Redis</strong> keeps hot/derived data in memory for <strong>microsecond</strong> reads, shielding the primary database and cutting latency.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Cache-aside — the default pattern</h3><p>The standard approach is <strong>cache-aside</strong>: check Redis first; on a miss, read the database, then store the result in Redis (with a TTL) for next time. Your code manages the cache alongside the DB.</p><figure><figcaption>cache-aside read path</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">is it in Redis?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">HIT</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">return instantly (µs)</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">MISS</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">read DB → store in Redis (with TTL) → return</div></div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The hard part: invalidation</h3><p>Stale caches cause subtle bugs. Always set a <strong>TTL</strong> so entries can't live forever, and <strong>invalidate or update</strong> the cache when the underlying data changes. Watch for the <em>cache stampede</em>: when a hot key expires, many requests miss at once and hammer the DB — mitigate with short locks or staggered TTLs.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🗒️ Analogy — a desk notepad</span>For answers you look up often, you jot them on a notepad instead of walking to the archive room (the DB) each time. If it's not on the pad, you fetch it once and write it down.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Cache invalidation &amp; stampedes</span>“Only two hard things: cache invalidation and naming.” Set TTLs, invalidate on writes, and guard against stampede when a popular key expires.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Redis does more than cache</span>The same Redis also holds rate-limit counters (Stage 4) and hot job state — one fast in-memory store, several jobs.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Redis = in-memory, microsecond reads for hot data.</li><li>Cache-aside: check cache → miss → DB → populate (with TTL).</li><li>Always set a TTL; invalidate on writes.</li><li>Beware cache stampede on hot-key expiry.</li><li>Also used for rate-limit counters &amp; hot state.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Walk through cache-aside on a hit and on a miss.</li><li>Why is a TTL non-negotiable?</li><li>What is a cache stampede, and how do you soften it?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s3"><span>◆ STAGE 3 — THE AGENT LAYER: LOOP · TOOLS · RAG ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·1</span><h2>The Agent Loop (plan → act → observe)</h2><span class="tag">the heart of the product</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>From answering to doing</h3><p>A single model call <em>answers</em> a question. An <strong>agent</strong> <em>does a task</em>: it plans a step, acts (calls a tool), observes the result, and loops — accumulating progress until the goal is met.</p><figure><figcaption>the loop</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">PLAN — model decides the next step</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">ACT — call a tool</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">OBSERVE — feed the result back</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">repeat until done</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>How a turn works</h3><p>Each turn, the model sees the goal + history and returns either a final answer or a <strong>tool request</strong>. If it's a tool request, you run the tool, append the result to the conversation, and loop. The trajectory (every step) is persisted as you go.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Terminating safely</h3><p>An unbounded loop can run forever and burn money, so you bound it three ways: a <strong>token budget</strong> (3·3), a <strong>max-iterations</strong> cap, and a <strong>no-progress</strong> check (stop if it's spinning). Termination logic is as important as the loop itself.</p><figure><figcaption>each turn</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">final answer or tool request?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">tool request</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">run tool → observe → loop</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">final / budget / max-steps</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">finalize &amp; return</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧑‍💼 Analogy — a junior assistant</span>You give a goal (“book the cheapest flight”). They think, search, look at results, decide the next step — repeating until done, then reporting back.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Always bound the loop</span>Token budget + max iterations + no-progress detection. Without these, one bad task loops forever and runs up the bill.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Persist the trajectory</span>Every step goes to MongoDB (Stage 2, referenced not embedded — it's unbounded) for debugging, evals (3·8), and the live SSE view (3·6).</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Agent = plan → act (tool) → observe → repeat.</li><li>Each turn: final answer or a tool request.</li><li>Terminate on done / token budget / max steps / no progress.</li><li>Persist every step (the trajectory).</li><li>Termination logic matters as much as the loop.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What's the difference between a single model call and an agent?</li><li>Name the three ways the loop can safely terminate.</li><li>Why persist the whole trajectory, and where does it go?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·2</span><h2>Tool / Function Calling</h2><span class="tag">letting the model act</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Give the model hands</h3><p>On its own the model can only produce text. <strong>Tool calling</strong> lets it request that <em>your code</em> run a function — search, fetch a URL, query the DB, compute — so it can act on the world, not just describe it.</p><figure><figcaption>the model asks, your code does</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">model returns a structured tool request (name + args)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">you validate the args, run the real function</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">feed the result back → model continues</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The contract: schemas</h3><p>You define each tool with a clear <strong>schema</strong> (name, description, argument types) the model can target. The model fills in the arguments; your code executes and returns a result. Good descriptions and typed args make the model call tools correctly.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Safety: validate and constrain</h3><p>The model's requested arguments are <strong>untrusted input</strong> — validate them with Pydantic before running anything, and give each tool the <strong>least privilege</strong> it needs. A tool that runs arbitrary shell or SQL is a security hole waiting to happen.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📞 Analogy — a phone and a calculator</span>Alone, the assistant can only talk. Give it a phone and a calculator (tools) and it can look things up and compute — it can act, not just narrate.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Untrusted arguments</span>Treat tool args like any external input: validate types/ranges, and scope what each tool can touch. Never hand the model a tool that can do anything.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to S0·2 &amp; 3·9</span>Tools are ports (S0·2 ports/adapters) with validated inputs (S0/L2), and their <em>outputs</em> are untrusted content you must guard against injection (3·9).</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Model → structured request; your code runs the function.</li><li>Define tools with clear schemas (name, desc, typed args).</li><li>Validate arguments (Pydantic) before executing.</li><li>Least-privilege tools; never “run anything.”</li><li>Bridges reasoning to real actions &amp; data.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Who actually runs the tool — the model or your code?</li><li>Why must tool arguments be validated?</li><li>What does least-privilege mean for a tool?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·3</span><h2>Context Window &amp; Token Budget</h2><span class="tag">finite memory, capped spend</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The model sees a limited amount</h3><p>The <strong>context window</strong> is the fixed amount of text the model can consider at once — the conversation, retrieved docs, tool results. It's finite, so as an agent runs and history grows, you must <strong>curate</strong> what stays in view.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Managing the window</h3><p>When history approaches the limit, you trim or <strong>summarize</strong> older turns, keep the most relevant retrieved docs, and drop stale tool output. Context management is an active job, not a one-time prompt.</p><figure><figcaption>two constraints to manage</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Context window (finite)</div><div class="lb">Only so much fits → include the relevant, summarize/trim the rest.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Token budget (per job)</div><div class="lb">Tokens = money + latency → cap them to stop runaways.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The token budget</h3><p>Every token costs money and adds latency, so each job gets a <strong>token budget</strong> — a hard cap on total tokens. It's a primary safety bound on the agent loop (3·1): when the budget is spent, the loop must stop and finalize.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧑‍🏫 Analogy — a fixed whiteboard + a spending cap</span>The model reasons on a whiteboard of fixed size (choose what to write), and there's a spending cap per task so no single job runs up a huge bill.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Context ≠ memory</span>The window is working memory that resets each call — anything the agent should “remember” long-term must be stored (trajectory, DB, RAG) and re-injected as needed.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Bounds the loop</span>The token budget is one of the three loop terminators from 3·1 — the accountant that stops a runaway agent.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Context = the model's finite working memory → curate it.</li><li>Trim/summarize old turns; keep relevant docs.</li><li>Token budget = per-job cap on cost/length.</li><li>Tokens are money and latency.</li><li>Budget is a key safety bound on the loop.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why can't you just stuff everything into the prompt?</li><li>What do you do as history approaches the context limit?</li><li>What does the token budget protect against?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·4</span><h2>Embeddings &amp; Vector Search</h2><span class="tag">finding things by meaning</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Turn meaning into numbers</h3><p>An <strong>embedding</strong> converts text into a vector (a list of numbers) that captures its <strong>meaning</strong>, so texts with similar meaning end up close together in a high-dimensional “meaning space.”</p><figure><figcaption>text → vector → nearest neighbors</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">query text</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">embed → a point in meaning space</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">nearest-neighbor search → top-k similar docs</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Searching by nearness</h3><p>To find relevant text, you embed the query and look for the <strong>nearest vectors</strong> (top-k). A vector database (pgvector / Qdrant) stores the vectors and does this nearest-neighbor search efficiently, even over millions of items.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Why it beats keyword search</h3><p>Keyword search misses “car” when you searched “automobile” — different words, same meaning. Vector search matches on <strong>meaning</strong>, so it finds semantically related content regardless of exact wording. This is the retrieval engine behind RAG.</p><figure><figcaption>keyword vs semantic</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Keyword</div><div class="lb">Matches exact words → misses synonyms &amp; paraphrases.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Vector (semantic)</div><div class="lb">Matches meaning → finds “automobile” for “car.”</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🗺️ Analogy — meaning as GPS coordinates</span>Embeddings place each text at a location on a map of meaning: “king” and “queen” sit near each other; “banana” is far away. To find relevant text, look for nearby points.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Chunking matters</span>You embed <em>chunks</em> of documents, not whole files — chunk size and boundaries strongly affect retrieval quality.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Feeds RAG (3·5)</span>Vector search is the “retrieve” step; 3·5 injects those results into the prompt.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Embedding = text → vector capturing meaning.</li><li>Similar meaning → nearby vectors.</li><li>Vector search = top-k nearest neighbors (pgvector/Qdrant).</li><li>Beats keyword search on synonyms/paraphrase.</li><li>Chunking quality drives retrieval quality.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does an embedding capture that keywords don't?</li><li>How do you find relevant docs once everything is a vector?</li><li>Give an example where semantic beats keyword search.</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·5</span><h2>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)</h2><span class="tag">ground answers in your data</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Look it up, then answer</h3><p><strong>RAG</strong> retrieves relevant documents (via vector search) and <strong>injects them into the prompt</strong>, so the model answers grounded in <em>your</em> data — not just what it learned in training.</p><figure><figcaption>retrieve → augment → generate</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">user question</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">embed + vector search → top-k docs</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">inject docs into prompt</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">model answers grounded in them</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why RAG instead of retraining</h3><p>It adds fresh or private knowledge <strong>without retraining</strong> the model — you just update the knowledge base. It also lets the model cite sources, and it sharply reduces hallucination on facts that live in your documents.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Its failure mode</h3><p>RAG is only as good as what you retrieve: bad chunks, poor embeddings, or the wrong top-k → the model is grounded in the <em>wrong</em> context and confidently wrong. Retrieval quality — not the model — is usually the bottleneck.</p><figure><figcaption>RAG vs alternatives</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">RAG</div><div class="lb">Fresh/private knowledge, no retraining, citable.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Retraining</div><div class="lb">Expensive, slow, hard to keep current.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📖 Analogy — an open-book exam</span>Instead of answering from memory (and maybe making things up), the student looks up the relevant page first, then writes the answer citing it — fewer mistakes, always current.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Garbage retrieval → garbage answer</span>If retrieval surfaces irrelevant chunks, RAG grounds the model in noise. Invest in chunking, embeddings, and re-ranking.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Uses 3·4, guarded by 3·9</span>Retrieval is vector search (3·4); and retrieved text is untrusted, so it's a prime prompt-injection vector (3·9).</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Retrieve (vector search) → augment prompt → generate.</li><li>Adds fresh/private knowledge without retraining.</li><li>Reduces hallucination; enables citations.</li><li>Quality depends entirely on retrieval quality.</li><li>Invest in chunking, embeddings, re-ranking.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>In the open-book analogy, what's the “book”?</li><li>Why does RAG reduce hallucination?</li><li>What's RAG's biggest failure mode?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·6</span><h2>SSE Streaming (live output)</h2><span class="tag">show progress as it happens</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Stream the agent's work token-by-token</h3><p><strong>Server-Sent Events (SSE)</strong> push the agent's output to the browser <em>as it's produced</em>, over plain HTTP — so users watch reasoning and results appear live instead of staring at a spinner for a minute.</p><figure><figcaption>one-way live stream</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">agent produces tokens/steps</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">SSE pushes each chunk (server → browser)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">browser appends live</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why SSE, not WebSockets</h3><p>You only need <strong>one-way</strong> server→client updates here. SSE is simpler than WebSockets, is plain HTTP (works through proxies), and <strong>auto-reconnects</strong>. WebSockets (two-way) would be overkill for streaming output.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Handling the stream</h3><p>On the server you yield chunks as they're generated; on the client you append each event and handle stream <strong>close</strong> and <strong>reconnect</strong>. Backpressure and client disconnects are the edge cases to get right.</p><figure><figcaption>SSE vs WebSockets</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">SSE</div><div class="lb">One-way, HTTP-native, auto-reconnect → perfect for output streaming.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">WebSockets</div><div class="lb">Two-way, heavier → needed for interactive/bidirectional apps.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📻 Analogy — live commentary</span>Rather than waiting for the final score, you hear the match as it happens. Streaming keeps the user engaged and reassured that things are moving.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Disconnects happen</span>Design for the client dropping mid-stream — the job keeps running server-side; the client can reconnect or fall back to polling by id.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Rendered in Stage 9</span>The frontend (Stage 9) consumes this exact stream to show the live trajectory in the dashboard.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>SSE = one-way server→client stream over HTTP.</li><li>Push agent output token-by-token; append live.</li><li>Simpler than WebSockets; auto-reconnect.</li><li>Handle stream close &amp; client reconnect.</li><li>Turns a long wait into visible progress.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What UX problem does streaming solve?</li><li>Why SSE rather than WebSockets here?</li><li>What happens to the job if the client disconnects mid-stream?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·7</span><h2>Multi-Agent Patterns</h2><span class="tag">divide, specialize, review</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Several agents beat one for complex tasks</h3><p>Hard tasks are more reliable when decomposed. Instead of one agent doing everything in one pass, you structure multiple agents to cooperate — each focused, and often checking each other.</p><figure><figcaption>three patterns</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Orchestrator-worker</div><div class="lb">A lead agent decomposes the goal and delegates subtasks to workers.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Evaluator-optimizer</div><div class="lb">One agent drafts; another critiques against criteria; loop until good.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Routing</div><div class="lb">A router sends each request to the best-suited agent/model.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Orchestrator-worker &amp; routing</h3><p><strong>Orchestrator-worker</strong>: a manager agent breaks a big goal into subtasks for specialist workers, then assembles results. <strong>Routing</strong>: a lightweight classifier picks the right specialist (or the right model tier) per request — cheap model for easy asks, strong model for hard ones.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Evaluator-optimizer (the quality lift)</h3><p>One agent produces a draft; a second <strong>critiques</strong> it against explicit criteria and sends it back for revision, looping until it passes. This “generate then critique” loop noticeably improves quality over a single one-shot attempt — at the cost of more tokens.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">👔 Analogy — a manager and a team</span>A manager splits a project among specialists, and a reviewer checks the work and sends it back for fixes. Specialization + review beats one person doing it all in one pass.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 More agents = more cost</span>Each extra agent/turn spends tokens. Use multi-agent where the reliability gain is worth it; keep simple tasks single-agent.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Bounded by 3·3</span>Multi-agent flows multiply token use, so the token budget and termination logic (3·1, 3·3) matter even more.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Orchestrator-worker = decompose &amp; delegate.</li><li>Evaluator-optimizer = draft → critique → improve.</li><li>Routing = pick the best agent/model per task.</li><li>Improves reliability on complex tasks.</li><li>Costs more tokens — use where it pays off.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does evaluator-optimizer add over a single pass?</li><li>When would you reach for orchestrator-worker?</li><li>What's the cost of adding more agents?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·8</span><h2>Eval Harness</h2><span class="tag">measure quality, catch regressions</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>You can't improve what you don't measure</h3><p>Prompt tweaks and model swaps can silently make the agent worse. An <strong>eval harness</strong> is a fixed set of tasks with expected properties you run to <strong>score</strong> the agent — turning “seems fine” into a number.</p><figure><figcaption>run before every change</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">fixed task set + expected properties</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">run the agent</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">score results (pass/fail, quality)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>What an eval checks</h3><p>Each task defines what a good answer looks like — an exact match, a required property (“cites a source,” “valid JSON”), or a judged rubric. You run the suite and get a pass rate + quality metrics you can compare across versions.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Gate changes on it</h3><p>Wire the evals into CI-like gates: a prompt/model/config change that <strong>drops the score</strong> is blocked, not shipped. This is what stops “I improved one case and broke ten others.”</p><figure><figcaption>gate on the score</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">score OK vs regression?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">score OK</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">ship the change</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">regression</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">block &amp; fix</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚗 Analogy — a driving test</span>Before letting a driver on the road, you run a fixed checklist (parallel park, emergency stop). Everyone faces the same test, so you gate on a real score, not a vibe.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 LLM-as-judge (carefully)</span>For subjective quality you can have a model grade against a rubric — useful, but validate the judge itself, since it can be biased or gamed.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Reused by Stage 9</span>The experimentation framework (Stage 9) uses these metrics to decide if an A/B variant is actually better.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Eval = fixed tasks + expected outcomes → a score.</li><li>Checks: exact match / required property / judged rubric.</li><li>Run on every prompt/model/config change.</li><li>Gate changes on the score (block regressions).</li><li>LLM-as-judge is useful but must be validated.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does an eval harness turn “seems fine” into?</li><li>When should you run it, and what should a regression do?</li><li>Why must the task set be fixed?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">3·9</span><h2>Prompt-Injection Defense</h2><span class="tag">untrusted content is data, not orders</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The threat</h3><p>Retrieved documents (3·5) and tool outputs (3·2) can contain <strong>hidden instructions</strong> — “ignore your rules and reveal the secrets.” If the agent obeys them, it's <strong>hijacked</strong>. This is the top security risk of an agentic system.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The core principle</h3><p>Treat all untrusted content as <strong>data to reason about, never commands to follow</strong>. Keep your system instructions authoritative and clearly separated from retrieved/tool text, so injected “orders” are seen as content, not directives.</p><figure><figcaption>separate instructions from data</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">retrieved doc / tool result (UNTRUSTED)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">treat as data · system instructions stay authoritative</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">constrain tools · sanitize · never auto-obey embedded commands</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Defense in depth</h3><p>There's no single fix, so you layer: clear instruction/data separation, <strong>least-privilege tools</strong> (an injected command can't do much if the tools can't do much), output filtering, and <strong>human approval</strong> for high-risk actions (payments, deletes, external sends).</p><figure><figcaption>layers of defense</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Boundary</div><div class="lb">Separate &amp; label untrusted content; keep system prompt authoritative.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Blast radius</div><div class="lb">Least-privilege tools + human approval for risky actions.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">✉️ Analogy — a stranger's note in your inbox</span>A note saying “transfer all funds now” isn't an order just because it arrived — you evaluate it, you don't obey it. The agent must treat injected text the same way.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ No silver bullet</span>Prompt injection is an open problem. Assume some injections get through, and design so that even then, tools can't do serious harm without approval.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to Stage 6 security</span>Least-privilege tools echo the least-privilege identity/RBAC (Stage 6) and the “validate untrusted input” rule from L2.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Untrusted content (docs, tool output) = data, not commands.</li><li>Keep system instructions authoritative &amp; separate.</li><li>Least-privilege tools shrink the blast radius.</li><li>Human approval for high-risk actions.</li><li>Defense is layered — assume some get through.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What is a prompt-injection attack, in one sentence?</li><li>What's the core principle for handling retrieved/tool content?</li><li>Why is least-privilege tooling a key defense even if an injection succeeds?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s4"><span>◆ STAGE 4 — RESILIENCE (rate-limit · breaker · retry · fallback) ⭐ ◆</span></div>
+
+    <!-- 4·1 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·1</span><h2>Provider Registry &amp; Selector</h2><span class="tag">route every call to a healthy provider</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: you can't hard-code one provider</h3>
+      <p>Your workers call LLM providers that are slow, rate-limited, occasionally down, and priced differently. If the provider is baked into the code, you can't shift traffic when one degrades, can't weight a cheaper one, and can't add a new one without a redeploy. You need to choose a provider <strong>at runtime, per request</strong> — based on who's healthy right now.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The registry: a live, updatable list</h3>
+      <p>The <strong>registry</strong> holds each provider plus its live metadata: is it healthy, what weight (share of traffic) should it get, how loaded is it. It's updatable while the system runs — mark a provider unhealthy, change a weight, add a new one — with no redeploy.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>what the registry tracks</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Provider entry</div><div class="lb">endpoint · API key ref · <strong>weight</strong> (traffic share) · cost/limits</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Live health</div><div class="lb"><strong>healthy?</strong> (from success rate + breaker state) · current in-flight load</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The selector: how it picks</h3>
+      <p>Given the healthy candidates, a <strong>selection strategy</strong> chooses one. The three you'll implement, and when each fits:</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>three strategies</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Round-robin</div><div class="lb">Rotate evenly. Simple; good when providers are equivalent.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Weighted</div><div class="lb">Send more to a preferred/cheaper provider (e.g. 70/30). Good for cost or capacity tilts.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Least-loaded</div><div class="lb">Pick the one with the fewest in-flight requests. Best under uneven latency.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <figure><figcaption>the request path through registry + selector</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">request needs a provider</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">filter to <strong>healthy</strong> candidates (registry)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">apply the strategy (RR / weighted / least-loaded)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">chosen provider → hand off to the resilience pipeline</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚕 Analogy — a taxi dispatcher</span>The dispatcher has a live board of drivers: who's on shift (healthy), who you prefer (weight), and who's free right now (load). Each ride goes to the best available driver — and if one goes offline mid-shift, the very next ride simply routes around them.</div>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Where “healthy” comes from</span>Health isn't guessed — it's <em>fed by the other Stage-4 components</em>: a provider whose <strong>circuit breaker</strong> (4·3) is OPEN is “unhealthy,” and its recent <strong>success rate</strong> and latency inform its weight. The registry is the scoreboard; the breaker and metrics are the referees.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Why this is lesson #1 of the stage</span>Everything else in Stage 4 <em>wraps the call to the provider the selector picked</em>: rate-limit it, breaker-guard it, retry it, fall back from it. Get selection right and the rest is a pipeline around it.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Registry = providers + weight + live health, updatable at runtime (no redeploy).</li>
+        <li>Selector strategies: round-robin (equal), weighted (cost/capacity tilt), least-loaded (uneven latency).</li>
+        <li>Only ever select among <strong>healthy</strong> candidates.</li>
+        <li>Health is driven by breaker state + success rate + load.</li>
+        <li>The selected provider is what the rest of Stage 4 wraps.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why choose the provider at runtime instead of in code?</li>
+        <li>Which strategy fits “provider B is cheaper, send it 70%”? Which fits “latencies are uneven”?</li>
+        <li>How does the selector know a provider is unhealthy?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·2 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·2</span><h2>Rate Limiting</h2><span class="tag">token bucket · sliding window · distributed</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: providers punish bursts</h3>
+      <p>Providers enforce hard limits (e.g. requests/min, tokens/min). Exceed them and you get <strong>429 Too Many Requests</strong> — or worse, a temporary ban. With many workers calling in parallel, you must <strong>self-regulate the whole fleet's rate</strong>, not just each worker's.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Token bucket — steady rate, controlled burst</h3>
+      <p>Picture a bucket that <strong>refills tokens at a fixed rate</strong> (say 100/sec) up to a <strong>capacity</strong> (say 200). Each call must take one token; if the bucket is empty, the call waits or is rejected. The refill rate sets your <em>sustained</em> rate; the capacity sets how big a <em>burst</em> you tolerate after a quiet period.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>token bucket mechanics</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">tokens refill at a fixed rate → up to capacity</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">a call needs 1 token</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">token available</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">take token → call proceeds</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">bucket empty</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">wait for refill (or reject / shed)</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Fixed vs sliding window — the boundary spike</h3>
+      <p>A naïve <strong>fixed window</strong> (“100 per minute, reset on the minute”) allows a nasty double-burst: 100 at 11:00:59 and 100 at 11:01:00 = 200 in one second. A <strong>sliding window</strong> counts the last 60 seconds continuously, smoothing that boundary. Token bucket already avoids this — which is why it's the usual default.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>why fixed windows leak</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Fixed</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg work" style="flex:2">window A (100 near the end)</span><span class="seg blocked" style="flex:1">💥 boundary: 200 in ~1s</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:2">window B (100 at the start)</span></div></div>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">Sliding</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg wait" style="flex:5">rolling 60s count — no boundary spike</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time →</div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">4</span>Distributed &amp; atomic (Redis + Lua)</h3>
+      <p>Because many workers share one limit, the counter must live in a shared store (<strong>Redis</strong>). And “read count → check → increment” must be <strong>atomic</strong>, or two workers both read “99,” both proceed, and you exceed the limit — a classic race. A <strong>Lua script</strong> runs the check-and-decrement as one indivisible operation on Redis.</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛣️ Analogy — a highway toll booth</span>Cars pass at a steady metered rate; a few can go through quickly if a gap built up (burst up to capacity), but the booth never lets a flood onto the road at once.</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ The race you must prevent</span>Without atomicity, N workers reading the counter simultaneously all think there's room and all proceed → you blow the limit. The Lua script (or an atomic Redis command) makes the check-and-take one step. This is the “distributed rate limiting” interview point.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties back to L1</span>Waiting for a token must be <code>await asyncio.sleep(...)</code>, never a blocking sleep — or you'd freeze the event loop while “rate limiting,” starving every other request.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Token bucket: refill rate = sustained rate; capacity = burst size.</li>
+        <li>Fixed windows allow a 2× boundary burst; sliding windows / token bucket smooth it.</li>
+        <li>Counter lives in Redis (shared across the fleet).</li>
+        <li>Check-and-take must be <strong>atomic</strong> (Lua) to avoid races.</li>
+        <li>Wait for tokens with a non-blocking sleep.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>In a token bucket, what do the refill rate and capacity each control?</li>
+        <li>Explain the fixed-window boundary burst in your own words.</li>
+        <li>Why must the counter be in Redis and updated atomically?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·3 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·3</span><h2>Circuit Breaker</h2><span class="tag">stop hammering a failing provider</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: piling onto a sick dependency</h3>
+      <p>When a provider starts failing or hanging, naïvely retrying makes it <strong>worse</strong> — you add load to something already struggling, tie up your own workers waiting on timeouts, and the failure <strong>cascades</strong> back into your system. The breaker's job: notice the trouble and <strong>stop calling for a while</strong>, so both sides can recover.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The three states</h3>
+      <p>A breaker wraps calls to one provider and tracks failures over a rolling window:</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>closed → open → half-open</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode accent">CLOSED — calls flow normally; count failures in a rolling window</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ failures cross the threshold</div>
+          <div class="fnode">OPEN — <strong>fail fast</strong>: reject immediately without calling (for a cooldown)</div>
+          <div class="farrow">▼ cooldown elapses</div>
+          <div class="fnode">HALF-OPEN — allow a <strong>few probe calls</strong> through</div>
+          <div class="fsplit">
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">probes succeed</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">back to CLOSED</div></div>
+            <div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">probe fails</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">back to OPEN (cooldown again)</div></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Why “fail fast” helps everyone</h3>
+      <p>In the OPEN state you return an error <strong>instantly</strong> instead of waiting on a doomed call. That frees your workers (no piling up on timeouts), removes load from the struggling provider so it can recover, and lets your <strong>fallback</strong> (4·5) kick in immediately. Half-open is the careful “is it back yet?” probe — you risk <em>one or two</em> calls, not a flood.</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">⚡ Analogy — an electrical fuse</span>A fuse trips to stop a fire (OPEN). You don't just keep shoving power into a fault — you wait, then <strong>test the wire once</strong> (half-open) before restoring full power (CLOSED).</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Tuning is the hard part</span>Too sensitive → it <em>flaps</em> open/closed on normal blips. Too loose → it lets failures through too long. Tune the failure threshold, the rolling window, and the cooldown to the provider's real behavior — and use a <em>rate</em> (e.g. &gt;50% of the last 20 calls) rather than a raw count.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Breaker vs retry — they compose</span>Retry (4·4) handles a <em>single</em> transient blip; the breaker handles a <em>sustained</em> outage. Retry inside, breaker around: retry a couple of times, but if the breaker is OPEN, don't even try — fail fast and fall back.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>States: CLOSED (normal), OPEN (fail fast), HALF-OPEN (probe).</li>
+        <li>Trip on a failure <em>rate</em> over a rolling window, not a raw count.</li>
+        <li>OPEN frees your workers + lets the provider recover.</li>
+        <li>Half-open risks a few probes to test recovery.</li>
+        <li>Retry = single blip; breaker = sustained outage; they compose.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Walk the three states and what triggers each transition.</li>
+        <li>Why does failing fast help the <em>provider</em>, not just you?</li>
+        <li>How do a retry and a circuit breaker divide the work between them?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·4 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·4</span><h2>Retry + Backoff + Jitter</h2><span class="tag">recover blips without a stampede</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Only retry the right things</h3>
+      <p>Retry <strong>transient</strong> failures (a timeout, a 503, a dropped connection) — not <strong>permanent</strong> ones (a 400 bad request, a 401 auth error): retrying those just wastes calls. And only retry operations that are <strong>safe to repeat</strong> (idempotent) — which is exactly why you built idempotency keys in S0·3.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Exponential backoff — wait longer each time</h3>
+      <p>Don't retry instantly and repeatedly — that hammers a recovering service. Wait an <strong>exponentially growing</strong> delay: ~1s, 2s, 4s, 8s… giving the dependency room to recover, and capping total attempts so you don't retry forever.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>backoff schedule</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">try</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">attempt 1</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">~1s</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">2</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">~2s</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">3</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:4">~4s</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">4</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time → (delay doubles each attempt, up to a cap)</div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Jitter — the part everyone forgets</h3>
+      <p>If 500 workers all fail at the same instant and all back off by exactly 1s, 2s, 4s… they <strong>retry in perfect sync</strong> — a “thundering herd” that spikes the provider the moment it recovers and knocks it down again. <strong>Jitter</strong> adds randomness to each delay so retries <strong>spread out</strong> instead of clumping.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>same failure, with vs without jitter</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">no jitter</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg idle" style="flex:3">…</span><span class="seg blocked" style="flex:1">💥 all retry at once</span><span class="seg idle" style="flex:3">…</span></div></div>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">jitter</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">r</span><span class="seg idle" style="flex:1"></span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">r</span><span class="seg idle" style="flex:1"></span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">r</span><span class="seg idle" style="flex:1"></span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">r</span></div></div>
+        <div class="axis">time → (jitter spreads the retries)</div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📞 Analogy — a busy phone line</span>If the line's busy you wait longer each redial (backoff). But if <em>everyone</em> redials at the exact same second, the line jams forever — so you each wait a <em>slightly random</em> amount (jitter) and get through.</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Retries amplify load</span>Each retry multiplies traffic to a struggling service. Combine with a <strong>retry budget</strong> (cap the % of traffic that is retries) and the circuit breaker (don't retry when it's OPEN) — otherwise your “recovery” logic becomes the outage.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to S0·3 &amp; S1·5</span>Safe retries require idempotency. This is the same “retries are everywhere → make repeats harmless” thread running through the whole platform.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Retry only transient + idempotent operations.</li>
+        <li>Exponential backoff: delay doubles each attempt, with a cap.</li>
+        <li><strong>Jitter</strong> = randomness → prevents the thundering herd.</li>
+        <li>Use a retry budget; don't retry when the breaker is OPEN.</li>
+        <li>Naïve retries can turn a blip into an outage.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Which errors should you <em>not</em> retry, and why?</li>
+        <li>What exactly goes wrong without jitter?</li>
+        <li>How do a retry budget and the breaker keep retries from amplifying an outage?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·5 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·5</span><h2>Fallback Chain</h2><span class="tag">degrade gracefully — carefully</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Keep serving when the primary fails</h3>
+      <p>A <strong>fallback chain</strong> is an ordered list of alternatives: if the primary provider fails (or its breaker is OPEN), try the next, then the next — so the user still gets an answer instead of an error. It's the “still eat something” safety net.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>try alternatives in order</figcaption>
+        <div class="flow">
+          <div class="fnode">primary fails / breaker OPEN</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode">backup #1 → (fails) → backup #2 …</div><div class="farrow">▼</div>
+          <div class="fnode accent">serve the first success · else fail cleanly with a clear error</div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why fallback is a double-edged sword</h3>
+      <p>Amazon's Builders' Library literally has an article titled “Avoiding fallback,” and it's worth internalizing. Fallbacks are dangerous because: (1) the fallback path is <strong>rarely exercised</strong>, so it's often the <em>least</em>-tested code and breaks exactly when you need it; (2) failures are often <strong>correlated</strong> — if the primary is down due to a regional outage, the backup may be too; and (3) shifting all traffic to the backup can <strong>overload</strong> it (it wasn't provisioned for 100%), turning one outage into two.</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍽️ Analogy — a restaurant list</span>Your favorite is full, so you try the next on your list. You still eat — but if you never <em>notice</em> your favorite keeps being full, you miss a real problem; and if everyone in town has the same backup list, that backup is now slammed too.</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Use deliberately, and alert</span>Fallback that fires silently hides the real failure. Always <strong>emit a metric/alert</strong> when a fallback triggers, make sure the backup has capacity for the shifted load, and <em>test the fallback path</em> regularly (chaos, Stage 12) so it works when it matters.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Composes with 4·1 &amp; 4·3</span>The “next provider” often <em>is</em> the selector (4·1) picking another healthy candidate; the breaker (4·3) is what says “skip the primary, it's OPEN.” Fallback is the policy that ties them together.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Ordered alternatives; serve the first success.</li>
+        <li>Risk 1: fallback path is under-tested (breaks when needed).</li>
+        <li>Risk 2: correlated failures (backup down too).</li>
+        <li>Risk 3: capacity — shifting 100% can overload the backup.</li>
+        <li>Always alert when a fallback fires; test it regularly.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>What does a fallback chain give the user during a partial outage?</li>
+        <li>Give two concrete reasons fallback can make things <em>worse</em>.</li>
+        <li>What must you always do when a fallback fires?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·6 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·6</span><h2>Load Shedding vs Backpressure</h2><span class="tag">survive overload without collapsing</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why overload causes <em>collapse</em>, not just slowdown</h3>
+      <p>Past a certain load, systems don't just get slower — <strong>goodput</strong> (useful work completed) actually <em>drops</em>. Queues grow, latency explodes, timeouts fire, clients retry (adding <em>more</em> load), memory balloons — a death spiral. The fix is to <strong>deliberately do less</strong> so the system stays alive for most users.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>goodput collapses under overload</figcaption>
+        <div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">load↑</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg work" style="flex:2">healthy: goodput rises</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">saturation</span><span class="seg blocked" style="flex:2">overload: goodput collapses 📉</span></div></div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Two responses — from different ends</h3>
+      <figure><figcaption>shed at the door vs push back on the source</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Load shedding</div><div class="lb"><strong>Reject excess requests now</strong> (fast 429/503) to protect the core. Shed <em>low-priority</em> traffic first; always let health-checks through.</div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Backpressure</div><div class="lb"><strong>Signal upstream to slow down</strong> — bounded queues that, when full, make producers wait. The pressure flows back to the source.</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+      <p>Shedding is what a <em>server</em> does to protect itself from clients it can't control. Backpressure works when you <em>own</em> both ends (e.g. the gateway → worker pipeline): a full, bounded queue naturally slows the producer.</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚪 Analogy — a nightclub</span>Shedding = the <strong>bouncer turns people away</strong> when it's full (protect the room). Backpressure = a <strong>slow-moving queue</strong> outside that paces arrivals so the room never overfills in the first place.</div>
+      <div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Shed the <em>right</em> things</span>Not all requests are equal. Shed low-priority/background work before paying-customer requests; never shed liveness/health probes (or the orchestrator will kill a pod that was actually fine). Prioritization is what makes shedding graceful instead of random.</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Ties to your SRE world</span>This is the same instinct behind Envoy's overload manager and pod resource limits you already operate — now you're <em>implementing</em> the policy, not just configuring it.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Overload collapses goodput — do less to stay alive.</li>
+        <li>Shedding = reject excess now (server-side, clients you don't control).</li>
+        <li>Backpressure = slow the source (when you own both ends; bounded queues).</li>
+        <li>Shed by priority; never shed health checks.</li>
+        <li>Partial service &gt; total collapse.</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>Why does goodput <em>fall</em> under overload instead of just leveling off?</li>
+        <li>When would you use backpressure rather than shedding?</li>
+        <li>What must you never shed, and why?</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- 4·7 -->
+    <section class="lesson">
+      <div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">4·7</span><h2>Bulkhead</h2><span class="tag">contain failure to one compartment</span></div>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The noisy-neighbor problem</h3>
+      <p>If all your calls share one pool of resources (threads, connections, concurrency slots), a <strong>single slow dependency</strong> can grab and hold all of them while its calls hang — starving everything else. One sick provider takes down features that had nothing to do with it.</p>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Isolate resources per dependency</h3>
+      <p>Give each dependency its <strong>own bounded pool</strong> (a connection pool, or a concurrency limit / semaphore). Now if provider A hangs, it can only exhaust <em>A's</em> pool — calls to B and C keep flowing. You've drawn watertight compartments.</p>
+      <figure><figcaption>separate pools contain the damage</figcaption>
+        <div class="lanes">
+          <div class="lane"><div class="lh">Pool A (provider A)</div><div class="lb">A hangs → only Pool A saturates</div></div>
+          <div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Pool B / C</div><div class="lb">unaffected → keep serving normally</div></div>
+        </div>
+      </figure>
+
+      <h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Sizing &amp; the resilience trio</h3>
+      <p>Size each pool for its dependency's normal concurrency plus headroom — too small throttles healthy traffic, too big defeats the isolation. Bulkheads rarely work alone: pair with a <strong>timeout</strong> (so a hung call releases its slot) and a <strong>circuit breaker</strong> (so you stop filling a doomed pool at all).</p>
+
+      <div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚢 Analogy — a ship's watertight compartments</span>If one compartment floods, the bulkheads keep the water contained so the whole vessel doesn't sink. Each dependency gets its own sealed compartment.</div>
+      <div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The resilience trio</span><strong>Timeout + Bulkhead + Circuit Breaker</strong> are the classic combination: the timeout frees a stuck resource, the bulkhead limits how many can get stuck, and the breaker stops sending work into the failure. Together they turn “one dependency down” into “one feature degraded.”</div>
+      <div class="bridge callout"><span class="ct">🔗 Where it lives</span>In Cosmos, each LLM provider gets its own concurrency limit + connection pool — so a hung provider can't consume the workers that should be serving the healthy ones.</div>
+
+      <div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul>
+        <li>Separate resource pools per dependency (connections / concurrency).</li>
+        <li>One dependency's failure can't exhaust shared resources.</li>
+        <li>Size for normal concurrency + headroom.</li>
+        <li>Always pair with timeouts + circuit breakers (the trio).</li>
+        <li>Turns “dependency down” into “one feature degraded.”</li>
+      </ul></div>
+      <div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol>
+        <li>How can one slow provider take down unrelated features without bulkheads?</li>
+        <li>What does isolating pools per dependency prevent?</li>
+        <li>Name the “resilience trio” and what each part does.</li>
+      </ol></div>
+    </section>
+
+    <div class="stage-banner" id="s5"><span>◆ STAGE 5 — EVENT-DRIVEN BACKBONE: KAFKA · CQRS · SAGA · CDC ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·1</span><h2>Topics, Partitions &amp; Offsets</h2><span class="tag">how Kafka organizes events</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Kafka is a durable, append-only log</h3><p>Kafka isn't a queue that deletes messages when read — it's a <strong>durable log</strong>. Events are appended to <strong>topics</strong> and kept for a retention period, so many independent consumers can read the same stream, and you can <strong>replay</strong> history.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Partitions = parallelism + ordering</h3><p>Each topic is split into <strong>partitions</strong>. Partitions are the unit of parallelism (more partitions = more consumers can work in parallel) <em>and</em> the unit of ordering: messages are ordered <strong>within</strong> a partition, but <strong>not across</strong> partitions.</p><figure><figcaption>topic → partitions → offsets</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">topic (e.g. jobs)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">split into partitions (parallelism + ordering)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">each message has an offset (its position)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Offsets and the partition key</h3><p>Every message has an <strong>offset</strong> — its position in the partition — which consumers use as a bookmark to track progress and to replay from any point. Which partition a message lands in is decided by the <strong>partition key</strong> (e.g. <code>job_id</code>): same key → same partition → guaranteed order for that job.</p><figure><figcaption>ordering guarantees</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Within a partition</div><div class="lb">Strictly ordered by offset.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Across partitions</div><div class="lb">No global order — parallel, independent.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📚 Analogy — library shelves &amp; slot numbers</span>Shelves = topics, split into sections = partitions; each book has a slot number = offset. You always know where you are and can go back to any slot.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The partition key is a design decision</span>Key by <code>job_id</code> and all of a job's events stay ordered on one partition. A bad key (e.g. everything to one partition) destroys parallelism or creates a hot partition.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Sets up consumer groups (5·2)</span>Partitions are what a consumer group divides among its members — the basis of scaling workers.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Kafka = durable, replayable append-only log (not delete-on-read).</li><li>Partition = unit of parallelism AND ordering.</li><li>Order guaranteed within a partition, not across.</li><li>Offset = bookmark → track progress &amp; replay.</li><li>Partition key (e.g. job_id) decides ordering &amp; balance.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is Kafka called a log rather than a queue?</li><li>Where is ordering guaranteed — within or across partitions?</li><li>What does the partition key control?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·2</span><h2>Consumer Groups</h2><span class="tag">scale workers, keep order</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Share a topic's partitions across a team</h3><p>A <strong>consumer group</strong> is a set of consumers that <strong>split a topic's partitions</strong> among themselves, so each message is processed once <em>per group</em>. Add consumers to the group to process more in parallel.</p><figure><figcaption>partitions divided across the group</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">topic partitions</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">assigned across group members</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">add consumers → more parallelism</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>One partition → one consumer (the cap)</h3><p>Within a group, each partition is read by <strong>exactly one</strong> consumer. So your maximum parallelism equals the <strong>number of partitions</strong> — 4 partitions can be shared by at most 4 active consumers; a 5th sits idle. You size partitions for future scale.</p><figure><figcaption>scaling</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Add consumers</div><div class="lb">Up to #partitions → linear scale-out.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Beyond #partitions</div><div class="lb">Extra consumers idle → repartition to grow.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Rebalancing when membership changes</h3><p>When a consumer joins or dies, the group <strong>rebalances</strong> — partitions are reassigned. Rebalancing briefly pauses consumption, so you want it to be rare and fast; sticky assignment strategies minimize churn.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">👥 Analogy — a team sharing one pile</span>Each task is handled by exactly one teammate; add teammates to clear the pile faster — but you can't have more active workers than there are stacks (partitions).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Multiple groups read independently</span>Different consumer groups each get the <em>full</em> stream — so your worker group and your analytics group can both consume the same topic at their own offsets.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Rebalances pause work</span>Frequent joins/leaves cause repeated rebalances (stop-the-world pauses). Keep consumers stable; use cooperative/sticky rebalancing.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Group splits partitions → each message processed once per group.</li><li>One partition → one consumer in the group.</li><li>Max parallelism = number of partitions.</li><li>Different groups each read the whole stream independently.</li><li>Rebalances (on join/leave) briefly pause consumption.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>How does a consumer group scale processing?</li><li>Can two consumers in the same group read one partition at once?</li><li>What is a rebalance, and why keep them rare?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·3</span><h2>Delivery &amp; the Exactly-Once Myth</h2><span class="tag">effectively-once, not magic</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>At-least-once is the default</h3><p>By default Kafka gives <strong>at-least-once</strong>: a consumer may process a message, crash before committing its offset, and get it again on restart. Duplicates are normal — the same reality you met in S1·5, now at the backbone level.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>How offsets create duplicates</h3><p>The offset is your “I've read up to here” bookmark. If you commit the offset <em>after</em> processing (correct) but crash in between, the message is re-read. If you commit <em>before</em> processing, a crash <strong>loses</strong> it. Offset-commit timing is the whole game.</p><figure><figcaption>offset commit timing</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">commit before or after processing?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">after (safe)</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">crash → reprocess (at-least-once)</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">before (unsafe)</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">crash → message lost (at-most-once)</div></div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Reaching effectively-once</h3><p>True exactly-once <em>delivery</em> is impossible over a network. You reach <strong>effectively-once</strong> by combining at-least-once delivery with <strong>idempotent processing</strong> (dedup by key) — or, within Kafka-to-Kafka flows, its transactional producer that ties writes and offset commits into one atomic step.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">✉️ Analogy — a letter delivered twice</span>You can't guarantee both “always delivered” and “never twice,” so you make opening it twice harmless instead of praying the post is perfect.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Don't chase exactly-once delivery</span>It doesn't exist across systems. Design idempotent consumers and commit offsets after success — that's the honest, robust answer.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Same principle as S1·5</span>Idempotent consumers keyed by a stable id turn duplicates into no-ops — the thread running through the whole platform.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Default = at-least-once → expect duplicates.</li><li>Commit offsets AFTER processing (before = data loss).</li><li>Exactly-once delivery is impossible across systems.</li><li>Effectively-once = at-least-once + idempotency.</li><li>Kafka transactions give exactly-once within Kafka-to-Kafka.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What's Kafka's default delivery guarantee?</li><li>Why does committing offsets before processing risk data loss?</li><li>How do you achieve effectively-once?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·4</span><h2>Schema Registry &amp; Evolution</h2><span class="tag">evolve messages without breaking consumers</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>A shared, versioned contract</h3><p>Producers and consumers must agree on message shape. A <strong>schema registry</strong> stores <strong>versioned schemas</strong> and checks that every new version is <strong>compatible</strong> — so formats can change over time without breaking anyone.</p><figure><figcaption>registry gates changes</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">producer registers a new schema version</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">registry checks the compatibility rule</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">compatible → accept · incompatible → reject</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Compatibility modes</h3><p>You pick a rule: <strong>backward</strong> (new consumers can read old data), <strong>forward</strong> (old consumers can read new data), or <strong>full</strong> (both). This decides which changes are allowed and in what order you deploy producers vs consumers.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Safe changes vs breaking ones</h3><p>Adding an <strong>optional</strong> field with a default is usually safe. <strong>Removing a required field</strong>, renaming, or changing a type is breaking — it must be a new major version handled deliberately, not a silent change.</p><figure><figcaption>change types</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Usually safe</div><div class="lb">Add optional field (with default).</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Breaking</div><div class="lb">Remove/rename required field · change type.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📄 Analogy — a shared, versioned contract</span>Both offices agree on a form. You can add an optional box, but you can't rip out a box the other side depends on without a new agreed version.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Compatibility mode drives deploy order</span>Backward-compatible? Deploy consumers first. Forward-compatible? Producers first. The mode tells you the safe rollout sequence.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Silent breaking changes are outages</span>A dropped required field can crash every consumer downstream. The registry exists precisely to make that impossible by accident.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Versioned schemas with enforced compatibility.</li><li>Modes: backward / forward / full.</li><li>Add optional fields = usually safe.</li><li>Remove/rename/type-change = breaking → new version.</li><li>Compatibility mode dictates producer/consumer deploy order.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a schema registry enforce?</li><li>What's the difference between backward and forward compatibility?</li><li>Which kind of change is usually safe, and which is breaking?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·5</span><h2>Outbox Pattern</h2><span class="tag">no lost or duplicated events</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The dual-write problem</h3><p>You often need to do two things: update the database <em>and</em> publish an event. Do them as two separate writes and a crash between them leaves you inconsistent — DB updated but no event, or event sent but DB rolled back. There's no transaction spanning both systems.</p><figure><figcaption>without vs with outbox</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Dual write (broken)</div><div class="lb">DB write + separate publish → crash in between = inconsistent.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Outbox (safe)</div><div class="lb">DB write + outbox row in ONE txn → always consistent.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Write the event intent atomically</h3><p>The <strong>outbox pattern</strong> writes the business change <em>and</em> an <strong>outbox row</strong> (the event to publish) in the <strong>same database transaction</strong>. They commit together or not at all — so the DB and the “to-publish” list can never disagree.</p><figure><figcaption>atomic write + relay publish</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">one DB txn: business change + outbox row</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">commit (both or neither)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">relay reads outbox → publishes to Kafka → marks sent</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The relay (and idempotency)</h3><p>A separate <strong>relay</strong> process reads unsent outbox rows and publishes them to Kafka, then marks them sent. If the relay crashes after publishing but before marking, it republishes — which is fine because consumers are idempotent (5·3).</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🖋️ Analogy — one pen stroke</span>You write the diary entry and the “to-mail” note in the same stroke — the record and the intent to notify are created together, atomically.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Turns a distributed problem into a local one</span>The only atomic thing you needed was a single-DB transaction — which you already have. The relay handles the rest, idempotently.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 CDC is an alternative (5·9)</span>Instead of a relay reading an outbox table, CDC can stream the outbox (or the change itself) straight from the DB log.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Dual-write (DB + publish separately) can go inconsistent on crash.</li><li>Outbox: business change + outbox row in ONE txn.</li><li>A relay publishes outbox rows to Kafka, then marks sent.</li><li>Republish-on-crash is safe because consumers are idempotent.</li><li>Solves the dual-write problem with a local transaction.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What exactly is the dual-write problem?</li><li>Why put the outbox row in the same transaction as the business change?</li><li>Who publishes the event, and why is a republish harmless?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·6</span><h2>Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ)</h2><span class="tag">quarantine poison messages</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Poison messages block the line</h3><p>Some messages fail no matter how many times you retry — malformed data, a bug, a permanently missing dependency. If you keep retrying one at the head of a partition, it <strong>blocks everything behind it</strong>. That's a poison message.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Move it aside after N attempts</h3><p>A <strong>dead-letter queue</strong> is a side topic. After a message fails a set number of times, you move it to the DLQ and <strong>advance past it</strong>, so the main pipeline keeps flowing. The failure is preserved, not lost.</p><figure><figcaption>isolate failures</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">message fails repeatedly (after N retries)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">move it to the DLQ; advance the main stream</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">alert · inspect · fix · replay</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The DLQ is a workflow, not a graveyard</h3><p>A DLQ only helps if you <strong>watch it</strong>: alert on new entries, inspect why they failed, fix the cause, and <strong>replay</strong> them back into the pipeline. An ignored DLQ is just silent data loss with extra steps.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📮 Analogy — a “return to sender” bin</span>At the sorting office, one un-scannable parcel goes in a side bin so it doesn't stop the whole conveyor — but someone must actually work that bin.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ An unmonitored DLQ = silent loss</span>Messages pile up unseen and the “fix” never happens. Always alert on DLQ growth (ties to Stage 12).</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Pairs with retries (S4·4)</span>Retry transient failures a few times first; only truly stuck messages go to the DLQ — retry then quarantine.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Poison message = fails forever, blocks the partition.</li><li>DLQ = side topic for repeatedly-failing messages.</li><li>Move after N attempts; keep the main stream flowing.</li><li>Alert, inspect, fix, and REPLAY from the DLQ.</li><li>An ignored DLQ is silent data loss.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why not just keep retrying a poison message forever?</li><li>What must you do with the DLQ besides moving messages to it?</li><li>How do retries and the DLQ divide the work?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·7</span><h2>CQRS</h2><span class="tag">separate writes from reads</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>One model can't be great at both</h3><p>A data model optimized for <strong>writing</strong> (normalized, event-based, consistent) is often bad for <strong>reading</strong> (needs joins, slow queries). <strong>CQRS</strong> — Command-Query Responsibility Segregation — splits them: a clean write model, and one or more read models shaped for queries.</p><figure><figcaption>write model vs read models</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">commands → events (write model, source of truth)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">project events → read model(s)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">queries hit the fast, query-shaped read model</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Read models are projections</h3><p>Read models are built by <strong>projecting</strong> the event stream (5·1) into query-optimized shapes — a table per dashboard, a denormalized view, a search index. Because they're derived, you can add new read models anytime and <strong>rebuild</strong> them by replaying events.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Eventual consistency</h3><p>The read model updates <em>shortly after</em> the write, not instantly — so a query right after a command may briefly show stale data. You design the UX around this <strong>eventual consistency</strong> (e.g. optimistic UI, “processing…” states).</p><figure><figcaption>two sides</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Write model</div><div class="lb">Events, normalized, strongly consistent.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Read model</div><div class="lb">Denormalized, fast queries, eventually consistent.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🗄️ Analogy — writing desk &amp; reading library</span>A desk for recording changes cleanly, and a library organized for fast lookup — each shaped for its own job.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Read models are disposable</span>Since they're just projections of events, you can drop and rebuild them, or add a new one for a new feature, without touching the write side.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Eventual-consistency lag</span>Reads can trail writes by moments. Never assume a read immediately reflects the write that just happened.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Split write model (events) from read model(s).</li><li>Read models = projections of the event stream.</li><li>Add/rebuild read models freely by replaying events.</li><li>Reads are fast &amp; query-shaped; writes stay clean.</li><li>Trade-off: eventual consistency (read lags write).</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does CQRS separate, and why?</li><li>Where do read models come from, and how do you rebuild one?</li><li>What consistency trade-off does CQRS introduce?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·8</span><h2>Saga</h2><span class="tag">multi-step transactions without a global lock</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>You can't hold one transaction across services</h3><p>A workflow that spans several services (reserve credits → run job → charge) can't be wrapped in one ACID transaction — there's no lock across systems. A <strong>saga</strong> coordinates it as a series of <strong>local</strong> transactions with <strong>compensations</strong> to undo on failure.</p><figure><figcaption>steps + compensations</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">step 1 ✓ → step 2 ✓ → step 3 ✗</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">run compensations for step 2, then step 1</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">system ends in a consistent state</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Compensating actions</h3><p>Each step has a <strong>compensating action</strong> that undoes its effect (un-reserve the credits, refund the charge). If a later step fails, you run the compensations for the completed steps in reverse — a business-level rollback, since a technical rollback isn't possible.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Orchestration vs choreography</h3><p><strong>Orchestration</strong>: a central coordinator tells each service what to do and triggers compensations (explicit, easier to reason about). <strong>Choreography</strong>: services react to each other's events with no central brain (decoupled, but harder to follow). Pick per complexity.</p><figure><figcaption>two styles</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Orchestration</div><div class="lb">A coordinator drives steps &amp; compensations — clear, centralized.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Choreography</div><div class="lb">Services react to events — decoupled, but harder to trace.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧳 Analogy — a group booking</span>If the hotel step fails, you auto-cancel the flight you already booked — undoing earlier commitments cleanly, one by one.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Compensations must be idempotent too</span>A compensation might run more than once (retries). “Refund” must be safe to attempt twice — same idempotency discipline everywhere.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Compensations can fail</span>What if the refund itself fails? Real sagas need retries, alerting, and sometimes manual intervention for stuck compensations.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Coordinates multi-step distributed work (no global ACID).</li><li>Each step has a compensating action to undo it.</li><li>On failure, compensate completed steps in reverse.</li><li>Orchestration (coordinator) vs choreography (events).</li><li>Compensations must be idempotent &amp; can themselves fail.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why can't you use one ACID transaction across services?</li><li>What is a compensating action?</li><li>Name the two saga styles and a trade-off of each.</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">5·9</span><h2>CDC (Debezium)</h2><span class="tag">stream DB changes to the warehouse</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Read the database's own change log</h3><p><strong>Change-Data-Capture</strong> taps the database's transaction log (the WAL) and emits every insert/update/delete as an event. Debezium reads what the DB already writes internally — so you capture changes <strong>without touching the app</strong>.</p><figure><figcaption>tail the log</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">DB commits a change (written to its log)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Debezium reads the transaction log</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">streams the change event → Kafka → warehouse</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why not dual-write from the app</h3><p>Having the app write to both the DB and the warehouse is the dual-write problem again (5·5) — fragile and inconsistent. CDC avoids it entirely: the DB log is the single source, and analytics is a <strong>consumer</strong> of it, fully decoupled.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Real-time, low overhead</h3><p>Because it reads the log rather than polling tables, CDC is <strong>real-time and low-impact</strong>. It's how you keep a ClickHouse warehouse or a search index continuously in sync with Postgres for analytics and the CQRS read models.</p><figure><figcaption>CDC vs dual-write</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Dual-write</div><div class="lb">App writes twice → inconsistent, coupled.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">CDC</div><div class="lb">DB log → events → warehouse: decoupled, consistent.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">✍️ Analogy — a stenographer</span>Someone copies every change in the main ledger into a second book, live, as it's written — no one has to remember to duplicate anything.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Log-based beats poll-based</span>Polling tables for changes is slow and misses deletes; reading the log is real-time and complete.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Cousin of Mongo change streams (2·4)</span>Same idea — react to DB changes — at the cross-system, warehouse scale.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>CDC reads the DB transaction log (WAL), not the app.</li><li>Streams inserts/updates/deletes as events.</li><li>Avoids the dual-write problem; analytics is a consumer.</li><li>Real-time &amp; low-overhead (log-based, not polling).</li><li>Keeps warehouse / CQRS read models in sync.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does CDC read to capture changes?</li><li>Why is CDC safer than the app dual-writing to the warehouse?</li><li>How does log-based CDC beat polling for changes?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s6"><span>◆ STAGE 6 — MULTI-TENANCY &amp; IDENTITY ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·1</span><h2>Multi-Tenancy &amp; Isolation</h2><span class="tag">many customers, one platform</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Shared infrastructure, private data</h3><p>Multi-tenancy means many customers (tenants) run on the <em>same</em> infrastructure. That's efficient, but it puts the burden on you to guarantee <strong>isolation</strong>: one tenant must never read another's data, and must never starve another's capacity.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Isolation levels — a spectrum of strength vs cost</h3><p>You choose how deeply to separate tenants: <strong>row-level</strong> (one shared DB, every row tagged with <code>tenant_id</code>), <strong>schema-level</strong> (a schema per tenant), or <strong>database-level</strong> (a DB per tenant). Stronger isolation costs more to run.</p><figure><figcaption>isolation levels</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Row-level</div><div class="lb">Shared tables + tenant_id filter. Cheap, dense; leaks if a filter is forgotten.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Schema / DB</div><div class="lb">Separate schema or database per tenant. Stronger isolation, more overhead.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Fair scheduling — the noisy-neighbor problem</h3><p>Isolation isn't only about data — it's about <strong>resources</strong>. Without fair scheduling, one heavy tenant can consume all the workers and starve everyone else (the noisy neighbor). Quotas (6·2) and per-tenant fairness keep the platform balanced.</p><figure><figcaption>scope everything by tenant</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">request carries a tenant identity</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">every query/row scoped by tenant_id</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">fair scheduling so no tenant starves others</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏢 Analogy — an apartment block</span>Shared building and plumbing (infrastructure), but each family has its own locked unit (data) and its own meter (usage/quota). Neighbors can't enter your unit or drain your water.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ One missing filter = a cross-tenant leak</span>In row-level isolation, a single query that forgets <code>WHERE tenant_id = ?</code> exposes everyone's data. Enforce it centrally (a repository layer, row-level security) — never rely on every developer remembering.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Defense in depth</span>Combine scoping in the app, row-level security in the DB, and tests that assert tenant A can't read tenant B. Isolation you can prove beats isolation you hope for.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Multi-tenancy = shared infra, isolated tenants.</li><li>Isolation levels: row / schema / database (cost vs strength).</li><li>Scope EVERY query by tenant_id — enforce centrally.</li><li>Fair scheduling stops the noisy-neighbor starvation.</li><li>Prove isolation with tests, not hope.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What must one tenant never be able to do to another (two things)?</li><li>What's the risk of row-level isolation, and how do you mitigate it?</li><li>What is the noisy-neighbor problem?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·2</span><h2>API Keys &amp; Quotas</h2><span class="tag">access + fair, billable usage</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Identify the caller, then limit them</h3><p>An <strong>API key</strong> identifies which tenant is calling; a <strong>quota</strong> caps how much they may use in a window. Together they control access and enforce fair, billable consumption before any work is accepted.</p><figure><figcaption>check before serving</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">request presents an API key</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">identify tenant → check quota</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">under quota → serve &amp; record usage · over → 429</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Storing keys safely</h3><p>Treat API keys like passwords: store only a <strong>hash</strong>, never the plaintext. Give each key a visible <strong>prefix</strong> (so you can identify it in logs without exposing it), support <strong>rotation</strong> (issue a new key, retire the old), and allow instant revocation.</p><figure><figcaption>key handling</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Do</div><div class="lb">Hash at rest · short prefix for identification · rotate · revoke.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Don't</div><div class="lb">Store plaintext · log the full key · make keys immortal.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Quotas &amp; the usage ledger</h3><p>Every accepted call is recorded in an append-only <strong>usage ledger</strong> (tenant, tokens, cost, time). Quotas read from it to enforce limits, and billing reads from it to charge — the same record serves fairness and revenue.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎫 Analogy — a gym membership card</span>The card proves who you are (key) and caps your monthly visits (quota). Swipe it and the visit is logged (ledger) for billing.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Hash keys like passwords</span>If your key store leaks and you stored hashes, the keys aren't usable. Store plaintext and a leak is game over.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The ledger feeds billing (Stage 9/11)</span>The same usage records power quota enforcement now and cost/FinOps reporting later.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>API key = identity; quota = usage cap per window.</li><li>Store only a hash; keep a prefix for identification.</li><li>Support rotation &amp; instant revocation.</li><li>Record every call in an append-only usage ledger.</li><li>Ledger serves both fairness and billing.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>How should API keys be stored, and why?</li><li>When is the quota checked in the request path?</li><li>What does the usage ledger enable besides quotas?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·3</span><h2>OAuth2 / OIDC / JWT</h2><span class="tag">standard authentication &amp; authorization</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Two different questions: authN vs authZ</h3><p><strong>Authentication (authN)</strong> = <em>who are you?</em> <strong>Authorization (authZ)</strong> = <em>what may you do?</em> OAuth2 handles delegated authorization; <strong>OIDC</strong> adds an identity layer on top (authN); a <strong>JWT</strong> is the signed token that carries both.</p><figure><figcaption>two questions</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Authentication</div><div class="lb">Prove identity (login via the IdP / Keycloak).</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Authorization</div><div class="lb">Which scopes/permissions the token grants.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The flow</h3><p>The user authenticates with the Identity Provider (Keycloak). The IdP issues a <strong>signed JWT</strong> containing identity + scopes. The client sends that token with each request, and every service <strong>verifies the signature and checks the scopes</strong> — no round-trip to the IdP per call.</p><figure><figcaption>token-based access</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">user authenticates with the IdP (Keycloak)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">IdP issues a signed JWT (identity + scopes)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">each service verifies signature + scopes locally</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>What a JWT is (and its trade-off)</h3><p>A JWT is <code>header.payload.signature</code>. Any service can verify the signature with the IdP's public key and trust the claims — <strong>stateless</strong>, no DB lookup. The trade-off: a JWT is valid until it <strong>expires</strong>, so revocation is hard. You use <strong>short lifetimes + refresh tokens</strong> to limit exposure.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎪 Analogy — a festival wristband</span>Verified once at the entrance (login), then just flashed at each stage (services verify the signature) — no re-checking your ID everywhere.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ A JWT is signed, not encrypted</span>Its payload is readable by anyone who has the token (base64, not secret). Never put passwords or secrets in it — only identity + claims.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Stateless verification vs revocation</span>No DB hit to verify = fast &amp; scalable, but you can't instantly kill a token. Keep lifetimes short (minutes) and rotate via refresh tokens.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>authN = who you are; authZ = what you may do.</li><li>OIDC = identity on top of OAuth2; JWT carries both.</li><li>JWT = header.payload.signature; verify signature locally.</li><li>Stateless (no DB lookup) — but revocation is hard.</li><li>Short lifetimes + refresh tokens; never put secrets in a JWT.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What's the difference between authentication and authorization?</li><li>Why can any service trust a JWT without calling the IdP?</li><li>Why must you keep JWT lifetimes short?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·4</span><h2>Vault — Dynamic Secrets &amp; PKI</h2><span class="tag">short-lived, audited secrets</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Static secrets in config are the problem</h3><p>Long-lived passwords and keys sitting in env files or config get copied, leaked, and never rotated. If one leaks, you may not even know. The fix: don't hand out standing secrets at all.</p><figure><figcaption>static vs dynamic</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Static (bad)</div><div class="lb">Long-lived creds in config → copied, leaked, rarely rotated.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Dynamic (good)</div><div class="lb">Short-lived, unique creds issued on demand, auto-expiring.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Dynamic secrets on demand</h3><p><strong>Vault</strong> issues <strong>short-lived, unique</strong> credentials when a service asks — e.g. a DB username/password valid for an hour, tied to a <strong>lease</strong>. When the lease ends (or you revoke it), Vault deletes the credential at the source. Every issuance is audited.</p><figure><figcaption>issue on demand</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">service authenticates to Vault</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Vault issues a short-lived, unique credential (lease)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">lease expires / revoked → credential auto-deleted</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>PKI — certificates as a service</h3><p>Vault's <strong>PKI</strong> engine issues and rotates TLS certificates on demand — the machinery behind mTLS (7·4) between your services. Instead of manually minting and distributing certs, services request short-lived ones that auto-renew.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔑 Analogy — a vending machine of single-use keys</span>Instead of copying the master key for everyone, a machine hands out short-lived, single-purpose keys that stop working on their own.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Leases + audit change the game</span>Every secret has an owner, an expiry, and an audit trail. A leaked credential is useless in an hour, and you can see exactly who accessed what.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Powers mTLS (7·4) &amp; encryption keys (6·6)</span>Vault PKI issues the certs for the service mesh; its transit/KMS features manage encryption keys.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Static secrets in config = copied, leaked, un-rotated.</li><li>Vault issues short-lived, unique creds on demand (leases).</li><li>Auto-expire / revoke deletes the credential at the source.</li><li>Every access is audited.</li><li>PKI engine issues &amp; rotates TLS certs (for mTLS).</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why are dynamic secrets safer than static passwords?</li><li>What is a lease, and what happens when it ends?</li><li>What does Vault's PKI engine provide?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·5</span><h2>RBAC</h2><span class="tag">permissions via roles</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Grant permissions through roles, not to people</h3><p><strong>Role-Based Access Control</strong> assigns permissions to <strong>roles</strong>, and roles to users — instead of granting permissions to individuals directly. Change what a role can do once, and everyone in it updates.</p><figure><figcaption>roles gate access</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">user is assigned a role</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">role carries a set of permissions</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">action allowed only if the role permits it</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Why roles scale</h3><p>Directly granting permissions to hundreds of users is unmanageable and unauditable. Roles (admin, editor, viewer) collapse that into a handful of well-understood buckets you can reason about and audit at a glance.</p><figure><figcaption>RBAC vs direct grants</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Direct grants</div><div class="lb">Permissions per user → sprawls, impossible to audit.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">RBAC</div><div class="lb">Permissions per role, users get roles → clean &amp; auditable.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Least privilege (and when you need ABAC)</h3><p>Roles should follow <strong>least privilege</strong> — the minimum needed. When access depends on <em>attributes</em> beyond role (e.g. “only your own tenant's data,” “only during business hours”), you layer <strong>ABAC</strong> (attribute-based) on top — which is exactly your tenant-scoping from 6·1.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🪪 Analogy — job titles &amp; doors</span>Your job title decides which doors your badge opens. Promote someone to “editor” and they get the editor doors — no per-door paperwork.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 You already operate this</span>Kubernetes RBAC and AWS IAM roles are the same model. Here you're applying it to your <em>application's</em> users and tenants.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Combines with tenant scoping (6·1)</span>RBAC says “editors can edit jobs”; ABAC/tenant-scoping adds “…but only their own tenant's jobs.”</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Assign permissions to roles; assign roles to users.</li><li>Roles scale &amp; stay auditable vs per-user grants.</li><li>Follow least privilege by default.</li><li>Layer ABAC for attribute-based rules (tenant, time).</li><li>Same model as K8s/AWS RBAC — applied to your app.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What do you assign users, instead of individual permissions?</li><li>Why does RBAC scale better than direct grants?</li><li>When do you need ABAC on top of RBAC?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">6·6</span><h2>Encryption in Transit &amp; at Rest</h2><span class="tag">protect data moving and stored</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Two threats, two defenses</h3><p>Data faces two risks: being <strong>intercepted</strong> as it travels the network, and being read from a <strong>stolen disk or backup</strong>. You defend both: encrypt <strong>in transit</strong> (TLS) and <strong>at rest</strong> (KMS-managed keys).</p><figure><figcaption>two places to encrypt</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">In transit</div><div class="lb">TLS between clients &amp; services; mTLS internally (7·4).</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">At rest</div><div class="lb">KMS-managed keys on disk, backups, and object storage.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>In transit: TLS everywhere</h3><p>Every hop uses TLS so no one on the network can read or tamper with the data. Internally, the service mesh adds <strong>mTLS</strong> (both ends authenticated) for zero-trust — traffic is encrypted even inside your own network.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>At rest: envelope encryption</h3><p>Encrypting big data directly with a master key is slow and risky. <strong>Envelope encryption</strong> generates a per-object <strong>data key</strong> to encrypt the data, then encrypts that data key with a <strong>master key</strong> in KMS. To rotate, you re-encrypt the small data keys, not the terabytes.</p><figure><figcaption>envelope encryption</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">generate a data key → encrypt the data with it</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">encrypt the data key with the KMS master key</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">store both; rotate by re-encrypting the small data key</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚚 Analogy — armored truck + a safe</span>An armored truck for money on the move (in transit) and a locked safe for money stored in the vault (at rest). You need both — a strong safe doesn't help while the cash is being driven across town.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Why envelope encryption</span>Rotating or revoking access means re-wrapping tiny data keys, not re-encrypting all your data. It also lets KMS never touch the bulk data itself.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Key management is the hard part</span>Encryption is easy; safely storing, rotating, and controlling access to keys is the real work — which is why you use a KMS/Vault, not homegrown key handling.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Two threats: interception (transit) &amp; stolen disk (rest).</li><li>In transit: TLS everywhere; mTLS internally.</li><li>At rest: KMS-managed keys on disk/backups/object store.</li><li>Envelope encryption: data key encrypts data; master key encrypts data key.</li><li>The hard part is key management — use KMS/Vault.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What two threats do in-transit vs at-rest encryption each address?</li><li>How does envelope encryption make key rotation cheap?</li><li>What's the genuinely hard part of encryption?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s7"><span>◆ STAGE 7 — INTERNAL COMMS &amp; GO ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">7·1</span><h2>gRPC &amp; Protobuf</h2><span class="tag">fast, typed service-to-service calls</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>REST/JSON is costly for internal, high-volume calls</h3><p>REST + JSON is perfect at the public edge — human-readable, universal. But <em>between</em> your services, called millions of times, JSON is verbose to send and slow to parse, and there's no enforced contract, so a field rename silently breaks the caller.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Contract-first with Protobuf</h3><p>You define the service and its messages in a <code>.proto</code> file — the single source of truth. A code generator produces a typed <strong>client and server</strong> in each language, so both sides are always in sync and mismatches are caught at compile time.</p><figure><figcaption>contract-first</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">write the .proto contract</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">generate typed client + server code</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">call over gRPC (binary, HTTP/2)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Why gRPC is fast</h3><p>gRPC sends compact <strong>binary</strong> over <strong>HTTP/2</strong> — smaller payloads, multiplexed connections, and built-in <strong>streaming</strong> (client/server/bidirectional). For internal service calls that adds up to real latency and CPU savings versus REST/JSON.</p><figure><figcaption>REST vs gRPC (internal)</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">REST + JSON</div><div class="lb">Human-readable, universal → great at the edge; verbose &amp; loose internally.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">gRPC + Protobuf</div><div class="lb">Binary, typed contract, HTTP/2 streaming → fast &amp; strict internally.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📋 Analogy — a strict pre-agreed form</span>Two offices exchanging a fixed, pre-agreed form: no ambiguity, quick to fill and process — unlike mailing free-form polite letters back and forth.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Code-gen keeps both sides honest</span>Because client and server are generated from the same .proto, a breaking change won't even compile — the contract is enforced, not hoped for.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Not human-readable</span>You can't just curl a gRPC endpoint and read it. You need tooling (grpcurl, reflection) to debug — the price of binary speed.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>REST/JSON at the edge; gRPC/Protobuf internally.</li><li>Contract-first: .proto → generated typed client + server.</li><li>Binary over HTTP/2 → compact, multiplexed, streaming.</li><li>Breaking changes caught at compile time.</li><li>Trade-off: not human-readable; needs tooling.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is JSON a poor fit for high-volume internal calls?</li><li>What is the single source of truth in gRPC, and what does it generate?</li><li>Name one downside of gRPC vs REST.</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">7·2</span><h2>Go Concurrency</h2><span class="tag">cheap parallelism for the worker</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Why rewrite the worker in Go</h3><p>The worker fleet runs thousands of concurrent agent jobs — mostly waiting on I/O. Go makes that kind of concurrency <strong>cheap and simple</strong>: tiny memory per task, fast startup, and small static binaries that are ideal in containers.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Goroutines &amp; channels</h3><p>A <strong>goroutine</strong> is a function running concurrently — so lightweight you can have hundreds of thousands. <strong>Channels</strong> pass data between goroutines safely, so you coordinate by <em>communicating</em> rather than by sharing memory and locking.</p><figure><figcaption>goroutines + channels</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">spawn a goroutine per job (cheap)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">goroutines send/receive via channels (safe)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">no shared-memory locks needed</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>context — cancellation &amp; deadlines</h3><p><code>context</code> carries a <strong>cancel signal and deadline</strong> down through calls. Pass it into every operation; check it at boundaries; when it's cancelled, everything downstream stops cleanly. This is the cooperative cancellation from S1·6, made idiomatic.</p><figure><figcaption>goroutine vs OS thread</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Goroutine</div><div class="lb">~KBs of stack, scheduled by Go → hundreds of thousands feasible.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">OS thread</div><div class="lb">~MBs each, scheduled by the OS → thousands is already heavy.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">👨‍🍳 Analogy — a chef juggling pots</span>One chef effortlessly tends many simmering pots with cheap helpers — adding another pot costs almost nothing, because the work is mostly waiting for things to cook.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 “Share memory by communicating”</span>Go's motto: instead of many goroutines mutating shared state behind locks, pass ownership of data through channels. Fewer races, clearer code.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Goroutine leaks</span>A goroutine blocked forever on a channel never exits and leaks memory. Always give goroutines a way out — a closed channel or a cancelled context.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Go: cheap concurrency, fast, tiny container images.</li><li>Goroutines = ultra-light concurrent functions.</li><li>Channels pass data safely (communicate, don't lock).</li><li>context carries cancel + deadline (like S1·6).</li><li>Beware goroutine leaks — always provide an exit.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is Go a good fit for a high-concurrency worker fleet?</li><li>What do channels let you avoid?</li><li>What is context used for?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">7·3</span><h2>Service Mesh &amp; Sidecar</h2><span class="tag">security &amp; traffic control without app code</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Cross-cutting network concerns keep repeating</h3><p>Every service needs the same things between calls: encryption, retries, timeouts, traffic routing, metrics. Building all that into every service (in every language) is duplicated, inconsistent effort. A <strong>service mesh</strong> lifts it out of the app.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The sidecar proxy</h3><p>The mesh runs a <strong>proxy sidecar</strong> next to every service pod. All traffic in/out flows through the proxy, which handles mTLS, retries, timeouts, and routing — <strong>transparently</strong>, so the application code does none of it.</p><figure><figcaption>sidecar per service</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">each pod gets a proxy sidecar</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">all traffic flows through the proxies</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">proxies apply mTLS, retries, routing, metrics</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Control plane vs data plane</h3><p>The <strong>data plane</strong> is the fleet of sidecars carrying traffic; the <strong>control plane</strong> (Istio) is where <em>you</em> set policy — “retry these 3×,” “canary 5% to v2,” “mTLS everywhere.” You configure intent centrally; the sidecars enforce it.</p><figure><figcaption>in the app vs in the mesh</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Without mesh</div><div class="lb">Each service re-implements TLS/retries/routing — inconsistent.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">With mesh</div><div class="lb">Policy set once; sidecars enforce it uniformly.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🕴️ Analogy — a translator/bodyguard per employee</span>Each employee gets a personal translator/bodyguard (sidecar) who handles secure comms and routing, so the employee just focuses on their actual job.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Policy, not code</span>Traffic rules become <em>configuration</em> you change centrally — no redeploying services to add a retry or shift a canary.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Real cost: latency &amp; complexity</span>Every hop now passes through two proxies, and the mesh is another complex system to run. Adopt it when you have enough services to justify it.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Mesh moves cross-cutting network concerns out of app code.</li><li>Sidecar proxy beside each service handles traffic.</li><li>Control plane = policy you set; data plane = sidecars enforcing it.</li><li>mTLS, retries, timeouts, routing, metrics — as config.</li><li>Cost: extra latency + operational complexity.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What repeated concerns does a mesh remove from app code?</li><li>What runs beside each service, and what does it do?</li><li>What's the difference between the control plane and data plane?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">7·4</span><h2>mTLS</h2><span class="tag">both ends prove identity</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Zero-trust: the network isn't trusted</h3><p>Being “inside the network” is not proof of identity — an attacker who breaches one pod could otherwise call everything. <strong>Zero-trust</strong> says every connection must be authenticated. <strong>mTLS</strong> (mutual TLS) makes both client <em>and</em> server prove who they are.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Two-way certificate verification</h3><p>Regular TLS authenticates only the server (like a website). <strong>mTLS</strong> adds the reverse: the client also presents a certificate, so each side verifies the other before any data flows. The channel is both encrypted and mutually authenticated.</p><figure><figcaption>mutual verification</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">client presents its cert</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">server presents its cert</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">both verify → encrypted, authenticated channel</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The mesh automates the certs</h3><p>Managing certs by hand is painful; the mesh (with Vault PKI from 6·4) <strong>issues, distributes, and rotates</strong> short-lived certs automatically. Identity is the <strong>certificate</strong> — not the IP address — so a service is trusted for <em>who it is</em>, not where it sits.</p><figure><figcaption>TLS vs mTLS</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">TLS</div><div class="lb">Server proves identity (client stays anonymous).</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">mTLS</div><div class="lb">Both ends prove identity → zero-trust internal traffic.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🪪 Analogy — both show ID</span>Not one person flashing a badge while the other is assumed fine — <strong>both</strong> show ID to each other before talking. No one is trusted just for being in the building.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Identity = cert, not IP</span>In a world of ephemeral pods and changing IPs, the certificate is the stable identity. Authorize on identity, not network location.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Powered by Vault PKI (6·4)</span>Those short-lived, auto-rotated certs come from the PKI engine you set up in Stage 6.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Zero-trust: network location ≠ identity.</li><li>mTLS = both client &amp; server present certs.</li><li>Encrypted AND mutually authenticated channel.</li><li>Mesh + Vault PKI auto-issue &amp; rotate short-lived certs.</li><li>Authorize on certificate identity, not IP.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>How does mTLS differ from regular TLS?</li><li>What does “zero-trust” mean for internal traffic?</li><li>Where do the certificates come from, and why short-lived?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">7·5</span><h2>Traffic Shaping &amp; Canary</h2><span class="tag">safe, gradual releases</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Big-bang deploys are a gamble</h3><p>Flipping 100% of traffic to a new version at once means a bad release hits every user instantly. A <strong>canary</strong> release de-risks this: send a <em>small</em> slice of traffic to the new version first and watch how it behaves.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Route a percentage, then decide on data</h3><p>The mesh (or Argo Rollouts) routes, say, <strong>5%</strong> to v2 while 95% stays on v1. You watch v2's <strong>error rate and latency</strong>; if healthy, you <strong>ramp up</strong> (5→25→50→100); if not, you <strong>roll back</strong> instantly — most users never saw v2.</p><figure><figcaption>gradual, metric-gated ramp</figcaption><div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">v2 %</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">5%</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">25%</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:2">50%</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:2">100%</span></div></div><div class="axis">time →</div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The mesh also shapes traffic generally</h3><p>Beyond canaries, the mesh applies <strong>retries, timeouts, and mirroring</strong> at the network layer — e.g. mirror real traffic to a new version without affecting users, or fail over on timeout. Traffic shaping is release safety plus runtime resilience.</p><figure><figcaption>promote or abort</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">canary metrics healthy?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">healthy</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">ramp up traffic</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">degraded</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">auto roll back</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🌉 Analogy — testing a bridge with 1% of cars</span>Before opening all lanes, you let a few cars across and watch it hold. If it does, you allow more; if it groans, you close it — with almost no one at risk.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Metric-driven promotion</span>Good rollouts promote based on <em>metrics</em>, not a stopwatch — the SLOs from Stage 12 become the gate that decides ramp-up vs rollback.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Argo Rollouts (Stage 8/9)</span>The GitOps tooling automates this canary + analysis + auto-rollback as part of the deploy pipeline.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Canary = route a small % to the new version first.</li><li>Watch error rate + latency on the canary slice.</li><li>Healthy → ramp up; degraded → instant rollback.</li><li>Mesh also does retries, timeouts, traffic mirroring.</li><li>Promote on metrics (SLOs), not on time.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is a big-bang deploy risky?</li><li>What do you watch on the canary before ramping up?</li><li>What decides promotion vs rollback in a good rollout?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s8"><span>◆ STAGE 8 — KUBERNETES DEEP &amp; THE OPERATOR ⭐ ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·1</span><h2>K8s Core Objects</h2><span class="tag">the declarative building blocks</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>You declare desired state; K8s makes it real</h3><p>Kubernetes is <strong>declarative</strong>: you describe the end state you want (“3 replicas of this image”), and K8s continuously works to make reality match — starting, healing, and rescheduling containers as needed. You don't run commands step-by-step; you state intent.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The objects you compose</h3><p><strong>Pods</strong> run containers (the smallest unit). <strong>Deployments</strong> manage stateless replicas (roll updates, self-heal). <strong>StatefulSets</strong> give stable identity + storage for stateful things (databases, Kafka). <strong>Services</strong> give a stable network address in front of changing pods.</p><figure><figcaption>what runs what</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Pod / Deployment</div><div class="lb">Pod = smallest unit; Deployment = self-healing stateless replicas.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">StatefulSet / Service</div><div class="lb">StatefulSet = stable identity for DBs/queues; Service = stable address.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Self-healing &amp; scaling for free</h3><p>Because it's a control loop, K8s restarts crashed pods, reschedules them off dead nodes, and scales replicas up/down — all to keep actual state matching desired state. This continuous reconciliation is the same idea you'll <em>extend</em> with your own Operator (8·3).</p><figure><figcaption>desired vs actual</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">you declare desired state (YAML)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">K8s compares to actual state</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">it acts to close the gap — continuously</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏗️ Analogy — an automated shipping port</span>You post the manifest (“these containers, here”), and robotic cranes load, move, and replace containers to match it — you never drive a crane yourself.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Declarative beats imperative</span>You don't script “start container, then…”; you declare the goal and let the control loop get there and keep it there — including after failures.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The Operator extends this loop</span>Your custom Operator (8·3) is just another controller reconciling desired vs actual — for <em>your own</em> object types.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>K8s is declarative: you state desired end-state.</li><li>Pod = smallest unit; Deployment = stateless replicas.</li><li>StatefulSet = stable identity (DBs); Service = stable address.</li><li>A control loop self-heals &amp; scales to match desired state.</li><li>This same loop is what an Operator extends.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does “declarative” mean in Kubernetes?</li><li>Which object runs a stateless app with replicas, and which for a database?</li><li>What keeps actual state matching desired state?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·2</span><h2>RBAC / NetworkPolicies / PodSecurity</h2><span class="tag">lock the cluster down</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Least privilege &amp; blast radius</h3><p>A breached pod or a fat-fingered permission shouldn't be able to touch everything. You minimize the <strong>blast radius</strong> with three controls: who can act (RBAC), which pods can talk (NetworkPolicy), and how safely pods run (PodSecurity).</p><figure><figcaption>three guardrails</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">RBAC — who can act on which resources</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">NetworkPolicy — which pods may talk to which</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">PodSecurity — drop privileges (no root, no host access)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Default-deny is the posture</h3><p>By default, Kubernetes pods can talk to <em>any</em> other pod — a flat, open network. You flip that to <strong>default-deny</strong> with NetworkPolicies and only allow the specific flows you need (gateway→worker, worker→DB). RBAC likewise grants the minimum verbs on the minimum resources.</p><figure><figcaption>open vs locked-down</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Default (open)</div><div class="lb">Any pod can reach any pod; broad permissions.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Hardened</div><div class="lb">Default-deny network + least-privilege RBAC + restricted pods.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>PodSecurity: restrict what a pod can do</h3><p>PodSecurity standards stop pods from running as <strong>root</strong>, mounting the host filesystem, or escalating privileges. Even if an attacker lands inside a pod, these limits keep them boxed in — they can't break out to the node.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🛂 Analogy — badges, internal firewalls, safety rules</span>Badge access (RBAC), firewalls between departments (NetworkPolicy), and safety rules every worker follows (PodSecurity). Together they contain any single breach.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ The default network is wide open</span>Until you add NetworkPolicies, one compromised pod can scan and reach everything. Default-deny, then allow explicitly.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Defense in depth</span>No single control is enough — RBAC + NetworkPolicy + PodSecurity layer so that getting past one doesn't grant the keys to the cluster.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Minimize blast radius: least privilege everywhere.</li><li>RBAC = who can act on what.</li><li>NetworkPolicy = allowed pod-to-pod traffic (default-deny!).</li><li>PodSecurity = no root, no host access, no priv-escalation.</li><li>Layer all three — defense in depth.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a NetworkPolicy control, and what's the safe default?</li><li>What does PodSecurity restrict, and why does it matter after a breach?</li><li>Why layer all three controls?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·3</span><h2>CRD + Controller + Reconcile Loop</h2><span class="tag">extend K8s with your own automation</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Teach Kubernetes a new noun</h3><p>A <strong>Custom Resource Definition (CRD)</strong> adds your own object type to the cluster — e.g. a <code>Tenant</code> or <code>PreviewEnv</code>. Now <code>kubectl get tenants</code> works, and your tenant becomes a first-class, declarative Kubernetes object like Pods and Services.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The controller runs a reconcile loop</h3><p>A <strong>controller</strong> watches your custom resources and runs a <strong>reconcile loop</strong>: read desired state (the resource), read actual state (what exists), and <strong>act to close the gap</strong> — create the tenant's namespace, DB, secrets, quotas. It runs continuously, forever.</p><figure><figcaption>desired vs actual, forever</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">you create a Tenant resource (desired)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">controller compares desired vs actual</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">creates/updates resources to match · repeat forever</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Level-triggered = self-healing</h3><p>Crucially the loop is <strong>level-triggered</strong> (act on the current <em>state</em>), not edge-triggered (act on events). So if someone deletes a resource the Operator manages, the next reconcile simply <strong>recreates</strong> it — the system self-heals to the declared state. That's the magic of the pattern.</p><figure><figcaption>imperative script vs reconcile loop</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">One-shot script</div><div class="lb">Runs once; drift &amp; failures aren't fixed.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Reconcile loop</div><div class="lb">Continuously converges to desired state; self-heals.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🌡️ Analogy — a thermostat</span>You set “desired 22°C.” The thermostat constantly compares to the room and nudges the heater — forever, without you. Open a window and it just works harder to converge again.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Level-triggered, not event-triggered</span>Reconcile on the current state, not on “an event happened.” Missed events don't matter — the next loop still converges. This is why Operators are robust.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Powers preview environments (Stage 10)</span>Your Operator is what provisions and tears down per-tenant preview stacks — and “I wrote a Kubernetes Operator in Go” is your ⭐ interview differentiator.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>CRD = your own Kubernetes object kind.</li><li>Controller reconciles desired vs actual, continuously.</li><li>Level-triggered → self-heals (recreates deleted things).</li><li>Encodes your ops knowledge as native automation.</li><li>Top-tier skill to show (your ⭐ anchor #2).</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a CRD add to Kubernetes?</li><li>What does the reconcile loop do on every pass?</li><li>Why does level-triggered reconciliation make an Operator self-heal?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·4</span><h2>KEDA — Event-Driven Autoscaling</h2><span class="tag">scale on real backlog</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>CPU scaling misses the real signal</h3><p>The default autoscaler (HPA) scales on CPU/memory. But your workers are mostly <em>waiting</em> on LLM calls — low CPU even when a huge backlog of jobs is piling up in Kafka. Scaling on CPU would leave you under-provisioned exactly when you're busiest.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Scale on Kafka consumer lag</h3><p><strong>KEDA</strong> scales on <em>external</em> signals — here, <strong>Kafka consumer lag</strong> (how many jobs are waiting). Backlog grows → add worker pods; backlog drains → remove them. You scale on the metric that actually reflects demand.</p><figure><figcaption>scale on lag</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">measure Kafka consumer lag</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">lag high → add worker pods</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">lag low → remove pods</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Scale to zero</h3><p>When there's <em>no</em> work, KEDA can scale the workers to <strong>zero</strong> — you pay for nothing while idle — and spin the first pod back up when a message arrives. Great for cost, with a small cold-start trade-off.</p><figure><figcaption>workers track the backlog</figcaption><div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">lag</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">low</span><span class="seg blocked" style="flex:2">spike</span><span class="seg wait" style="flex:1">drained</span></div></div><div class="tl-row"><span class="tl-lab">pods</span><div class="tl"><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">2</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:2">8</span><span class="seg work" style="flex:1">0</span></div></div><div class="axis">time →</div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">👥 Analogy — call in staff when the queue is long</span>You don't hire based on how hard the current staff are sweating (CPU) — you hire based on how many orders are waiting (lag), and send people home when it's quiet.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Scale on the signal that means “demand”</span>For queue workers, backlog depth is the true demand signal — not CPU. Match your scaler to what actually predicts load.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Complements HPA</span>KEDA can drive the HPA under the hood — it just feeds it external metrics like Kafka lag instead of CPU.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>CPU scaling misses I/O-bound backlog.</li><li>KEDA scales on external signals (Kafka lag).</li><li>Backlog up → add pods; drained → remove.</li><li>Can scale to zero when idle (cost win, cold-start cost).</li><li>Scale on the metric that reflects real demand.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is CPU a poor scaling signal for these workers?</li><li>What signal does KEDA scale on here, and why is it better?</li><li>What does scale-to-zero trade off?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·5</span><h2>Admission Control (Kyverno / OPA)</h2><span class="tag">policy-as-code guardrails</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Enforce standards at the moment of creation</h3><p>You want cluster-wide rules — “no privileged pods,” “every workload must have a team label,” “images only from our registry.” <strong>Admission control</strong> checks every object as it's created or updated and <strong>rejects or fixes</strong> the ones that break the rules.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Validate and mutate webhooks</h3><p>Two kinds: <strong>validating</strong> (reject anything non-compliant) and <strong>mutating</strong> (auto-fix it, e.g. inject a default label or a sidecar). Tools like <strong>Kyverno</strong> or <strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong> let you write these as declarative policies.</p><figure><figcaption>check at admission</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">object satisfies policy?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">yes</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">admit it</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">no</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">reject (or mutate to comply)</div></div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Policy-as-code</h3><p>Policies live in Git as code — versioned, reviewed, and applied uniformly across the cluster. Instead of hoping everyone follows the rules, the cluster <strong>enforces</strong> them automatically at the door, every time.</p><figure><figcaption>hope vs enforce</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Guidelines in a wiki</div><div class="lb">People forget → drift &amp; risk.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Policy-as-code</div><div class="lb">Enforced at admission, every time, auditable.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🚪 Analogy — a doorman with a dress code</span>Anyone not meeting the code is turned away at the door (validate) — or handed the required jacket on the way in (mutate). Consistent, automatic, no exceptions.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Shift governance left &amp; automatic</span>Catch violations at create-time, not in a quarterly audit. The guardrail is code, so it's consistent and reviewable.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Extends to supply chain (Stage 12)</span>The same enforcement gate can require signed images (cosign/SLSA) before a pod is admitted.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Admission control checks objects at create/update.</li><li>Validating = reject; mutating = auto-fix.</li><li>Kyverno / OPA Gatekeeper = policies as code.</li><li>Enforced uniformly &amp; automatically, not by memory.</li><li>Can require signed images (supply-chain gate).</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>When are admission policies evaluated?</li><li>What's the difference between a validating and a mutating policy?</li><li>Why is policy-as-code better than written guidelines?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">8·6</span><h2>Helm &amp; Kustomize</h2><span class="tag">package &amp; customize manifests</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The same YAML, slightly different per environment</h3><p>Dev, staging, and prod need almost-identical manifests with small differences (replica counts, image tags, resource limits). Copy-pasting YAML per environment is a drift-and-error factory. Two tools solve it differently.</p><figure><figcaption>two approaches</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Helm</div><div class="lb">Templated, versioned charts + a values file per environment.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Kustomize</div><div class="lb">A base set of manifests + overlays that patch it per environment.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Helm — templated, versioned charts</h3><p><strong>Helm</strong> packages manifests as a <strong>chart</strong> with placeholders filled from a <code>values.yaml</code>. It's versioned and shareable (install/upgrade/rollback a whole app as a unit) — great for distributing reusable packages.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Kustomize — base + overlays</h3><p><strong>Kustomize</strong> takes a plain <strong>base</strong> and applies <strong>overlays</strong> that patch specific fields per environment — no templating language, just declarative patches. Great when you want to keep manifests readable and diff-friendly.</p><figure><figcaption>base + overlay</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">base manifests (common)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">overlay: patch replicas/image per env</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">kustomize merges → env-specific manifests</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📝 Analogy — a fill-in template vs a base recipe + tweaks</span>Helm = a fill-in-the-blanks template you supply values to. Kustomize = a base recipe plus small written tweaks per occasion. Both beat rewriting the whole dish each time.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Often used together</span>A common pattern: Helm to install third-party charts, Kustomize to layer your environment-specific patches on top.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Over-templating hurts</span>Helm charts full of nested conditionals become unreadable. Keep templates simple; push complexity into values or use Kustomize overlays.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Avoid copy-pasting YAML per environment.</li><li>Helm = templated, versioned, shareable charts (values.yaml).</li><li>Kustomize = base + overlays (declarative patches, no templating).</li><li>Often combined (Helm for deps, Kustomize for env patches).</li><li>Keep templates simple; avoid over-templating.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What problem do Helm/Kustomize both solve?</li><li>How does Helm's approach differ from Kustomize's?</li><li>When might you use both together?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s9"><span>◆ STAGE 9 — FRONTEND &amp; EXPERIMENTATION ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·1</span><h2>React / Next.js / TypeScript</h2><span class="tag">the dashboard stack</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Components: build UIs from reusable pieces</h3><p><strong>React</strong> builds a UI out of <strong>components</strong> — small, self-contained pieces (a JobCard, a StatusBadge) that manage their own state and re-render when data changes. You compose complex screens from simple, reusable parts.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Next.js: the framework around React</h3><p><strong>Next.js</strong> adds what a real app needs: routing, server-side rendering (fast first paint, SEO), API routes, and an optimized build. It turns React from a library into a full application framework.</p><figure><figcaption>the three pieces</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">React</div><div class="lb">Reusable components that re-render on state change.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Next.js</div><div class="lb">Routing, SSR, build tooling around React.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">TypeScript</div><div class="lb">Static types across the whole app.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>TypeScript: catch bugs before users do</h3><p><strong>TypeScript</strong> adds static types to JavaScript — the same discipline as Python's type hints (L2). It catches “undefined is not a function” style bugs at compile time and makes refactoring across a large dashboard safe.</p><figure><figcaption>compose the UI</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">build reusable components (React)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Next.js adds routing/SSR/build</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">TypeScript types catch bugs at compile time</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧱 Analogy — LEGO for UIs</span>React gives you labeled bricks (components); Next.js is the builder kit with instructions; TypeScript makes the bricks only fit where they should.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Same typing discipline as the backend</span>TypeScript on the frontend mirrors mypy/Pydantic on the backend — types are your safety net on both sides of the wire.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Frontend churn is real</span>The JS ecosystem moves fast. Pin versions, keep dependencies lean, and don't chase every new tool mid-project.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>React = reusable components, re-render on state change.</li><li>Next.js = routing + SSR + build tooling.</li><li>TypeScript = static types → fewer runtime bugs.</li><li>Same typing discipline as the backend.</li><li>Keep dependencies lean; pin versions.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What is a React component?</li><li>What does Next.js add on top of React?</li><li>How does TypeScript relate to the backend's mypy/Pydantic?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·2</span><h2>SSE UI (live trajectory)</h2><span class="tag">render the agent's stream</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Consume the server's SSE stream</h3><p>The dashboard subscribes to the SSE endpoint you built in Stage 3 (3·6) and receives the agent's steps as a live event stream — each token or step arriving as its own message over one long-lived HTTP connection.</p><figure><figcaption>consume &amp; render</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">subscribe to the SSE endpoint</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">receive events (tokens/steps) live</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">append each to the UI as it arrives</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Append live, keep the UI responsive</h3><p>As events arrive you append them to the view so the user watches the agent think in real time. You batch/throttle rendering so a fast token stream doesn't thrash the browser, and keep the UI scrolled and responsive.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Handle disconnects gracefully</h3><p>Connections drop. SSE <strong>auto-reconnects</strong>, but you also design for it: the job keeps running server-side, so on reconnect you resume the stream, and if streaming fails entirely you <strong>fall back to polling</strong> <code>GET /jobs/{id}</code> by id.</p><figure><figcaption>SSE vs polling in the UI</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">SSE (primary)</div><div class="lb">Live, low-latency updates over one connection.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Polling (fallback)</div><div class="lb">Ask by id every few seconds if the stream fails.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🗺️ Analogy — a live delivery map</span>Instead of a static “out for delivery,” you watch the driver move in real time — engaging, and reassuring that progress is happening.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Optimistic UI</span>Show “submitting…” and the job id immediately (from the 202), then stream results in — the interface feels instant even though work is async.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The other half of 3·6</span>Stage 3 produced the stream; this lesson consumes it. Same feature, two ends of the wire.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Subscribe to the Stage-3 SSE endpoint.</li><li>Append events live; throttle rendering for fast streams.</li><li>SSE auto-reconnects; the job runs server-side regardless.</li><li>Fall back to polling by id if streaming fails.</li><li>Optimistic UI: show id/“submitting” from the 202 instantly.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Where does the streamed data come from?</li><li>What happens to the job if the client disconnects mid-stream?</li><li>What's the fallback if streaming fails entirely?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·3</span><h2>Feature Flags</h2><span class="tag">toggle features at runtime</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Decouple deploy from release</h3><p>Normally shipping code = releasing a feature. <strong>Feature flags</strong> break that link: you deploy code with the feature <strong>behind a switch</strong> that's off, then turn it on later — for everyone, or a subset — <em>without a new deploy</em>.</p><figure><figcaption>deploy ≠ release</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">ship code with the feature behind a flag (off)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">turn it on for a %/user/tenant</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">problem → flip it off instantly (no redeploy)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Targeting: who sees it</h3><p>A flag can be on for <strong>10% of users</strong>, for a <strong>specific tenant</strong>, for internal staff only, or globally. This powers gradual rollout, <strong>dark launches</strong> (ship code inert, enable later), and per-tenant features.</p><figure><figcaption>evaluate the flag</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">is the flag on for this user?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">on</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">show new feature</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">off</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">show current behavior</div></div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The kill switch — and flag debt</h3><p>A flag is an instant <strong>kill switch</strong>: if the new feature misbehaves, flip it off in seconds — far faster than a rollback deploy. The cost: <strong>flag debt</strong> — stale flags accumulate and tangle the code, so you must retire them once a feature is fully shipped.</p><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">💡 Analogy — a light switch</span>You can flip a feature on or off without rewiring the house (redeploying). And you can wire the switch to just one room (a tenant/%).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Dark launch</span>Deploy risky code turned <em>off</em>, enable it for internal users first, then ramp — you separate the risk of <em>deploying</em> from the risk of <em>releasing</em>.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Flag debt</span>Every flag is a branch in your code. Stale flags multiply paths and confuse behavior — schedule their removal once the feature is permanent.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Flags decouple deploy from release.</li><li>Target by %/user/tenant; enables dark launches.</li><li>Instant kill switch — faster than a rollback.</li><li>Cost: flag debt — retire stale flags.</li><li>Foundation for the A/B experiments (9·4).</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>How do flags separate deploying from releasing?</li><li>What is a dark launch?</li><li>What's the maintenance cost of flags?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·4</span><h2>Traffic Splitting &amp; Assignment</h2><span class="tag">the mechanism behind A/B</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>A controlled comparison on real traffic</h3><p>To know if a new agent config or model is actually better, you run both at once: a <strong>control</strong> (current) and a <strong>variant</strong> (new), each serving a slice of real users, and compare outcomes. Traffic splitting is the plumbing that assigns who gets which.</p><figure><figcaption>controlled comparison</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">stable-hash the user id → a bucket</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">assign control or variant consistently</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">measure each group's outcomes</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Stable assignment (sticky buckets)</h3><p>Assignment must be <strong>deterministic and sticky</strong>: hash the user id so the <em>same</em> user always lands in the <em>same</em> group across requests. Otherwise a user flips between variants and your comparison is meaningless.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Isolate one change; validate the pipe</h3><p>Change <strong>one thing at a time</strong> so any difference is attributable. And run an <strong>A/A test</strong> occasionally (both groups identical) — if it shows a “difference,” your splitting/measurement is broken. Watch for <strong>sample-ratio mismatch</strong> (groups not the size you set) as a red flag.</p><figure><figcaption>control vs variant</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Control</div><div class="lb">Current behavior — the baseline.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Variant</div><div class="lb">One change — the thing you're testing.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍽️ Analogy — 10% get the new menu</span>Give a tenth of diners the new menu and compare against the old — same restaurant, same conditions, one difference: the menu.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Sticky assignment is essential</span>Consistent bucketing per user is what makes the comparison valid. Random-per-request assignment destroys the experiment.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Sample-ratio mismatch</span>If you set 50/50 but see 55/45, something's wrong with assignment or logging — don't trust the results until it's fixed.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Compare control vs variant on real traffic.</li><li>Stable (sticky) assignment: hash the user id.</li><li>Change one thing at a time → attributable results.</li><li>A/A test validates the splitting pipeline.</li><li>Watch for sample-ratio mismatch.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why must assignment be stable per user?</li><li>What's the point of a control group?</li><li>What does an A/A test check?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·5</span><h2>Statistical Significance</h2><span class="tag">is the result real or luck?</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Real effect vs random noise</h3><p>A variant looking 3% better might be a genuine improvement — or just noise from a small sample. <strong>Statistical significance</strong> is how you decide whether the observed difference is unlikely to be chance.</p><figure><figcaption>real vs noise</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Signal</div><div class="lb">Difference persists with enough data → likely real.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Noise</div><div class="lb">Difference vanishes as data grows → was luck.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Sample size &amp; confidence</h3><p>You need <strong>enough samples</strong> before trusting a result — small samples swing wildly. A significance test (p-value / confidence interval) tells you the probability the difference is chance; below a threshold, you call it significant.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The peeking problem</h3><p>If you keep checking and <strong>stop the moment it looks good</strong>, you'll fool yourself — repeatedly peeking inflates false positives. Fix the sample size (or use sequential/Bayesian methods designed for continuous monitoring) and don't stop early on a lucky reading.</p><figure><figcaption>decide honestly</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">collect a pre-planned sample size</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">test if the difference is beyond chance</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">significant → act · not → keep gathering (don't peek-and-stop)</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🪙 Analogy — a lucky coin</span>Three heads in a row doesn't mean the coin is rigged. You need many flips before betting the business that “heads” is really more likely.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Peeking &amp; early stopping</span>Watching the dashboard and stopping at the first “win” is the most common way teams ship noise. Commit to a sample size (or a proper sequential method).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Statistical ≠ practical significance</span>A change can be statistically significant but too tiny to matter. Ask “is this difference big enough to care about?” too.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Distinguish real effects from random noise.</li><li>Need adequate sample size before trusting a result.</li><li>Significance test = probability the difference is chance.</li><li>Don't peek-and-stop → inflates false positives.</li><li>Statistical significance ≠ practical significance.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does statistical significance protect you from?</li><li>Why is sample size important?</li><li>What's the danger of stopping the moment it looks good?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">9·6</span><h2>Guardrails &amp; Auto-Rollback</h2><span class="tag">experiment boldly, fail safe</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>Bold experiments need a safety net</h3><p>You want to try aggressive changes, but not at the cost of harming users. <strong>Guardrail metrics</strong> + <strong>auto-rollback</strong> let you push hard because a bad experiment is <em>caught and reverted automatically</em> before it does real damage.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>Guardrail metrics</h3><p>Alongside the metric you're <em>optimizing</em> (say, task success), you monitor <strong>guardrails</strong> you must not harm — latency, error rate, cost, safety. These are the “don't make anything worse” tripwires, distinct from the goal metric.</p><figure><figcaption>monitor &amp; protect</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">define guardrail metrics upfront</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">monitor them during the experiment</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">breach → auto-halt / roll back the variant</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>Automatic halt</h3><p>If a guardrail crosses its threshold, the system <strong>automatically halts</strong> the variant (or rolls it back to control) — no human in the loop at 3am. This is what makes fast experimentation safe: the blast radius is bounded and the reaction is instant.</p><figure><figcaption>auto-protect</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">guardrail breached?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">no</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">let the experiment run</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">yes</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">auto-halt / roll back</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔌 Analogy — a safety cutoff</span>Like a machine that cuts power the instant a limit is crossed — you can run it fast because the cutoff protects you and the equipment automatically.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Guardrails ≠ your goal metric</span>Optimizing task success while a guardrail watches latency/cost/errors keeps you from “winning” on one metric by quietly wrecking another.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Guardrails are SLO-shaped (Stage 12)</span>The same reliability targets and burn-rate thinking from Stage 12 make natural guardrails for experiments.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Guardrails + auto-rollback make bold experiments safe.</li><li>Guardrail metrics = “don't harm” tripwires (latency/error/cost).</li><li>Distinct from the goal metric you're optimizing.</li><li>Breach → automatic halt/rollback, no human needed.</li><li>Guardrails are SLO-shaped.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What triggers an auto-rollback?</li><li>How do guardrail metrics differ from the metric you're optimizing?</li><li>Why do guardrails let you experiment more boldly?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s10"><span>◆ STAGE 10 — PER-TENANT PREVIEW ENVIRONMENTS ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">10·1</span><h2>Ephemeral Environments</h2><span class="tag">on-demand, disposable stacks</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: one shared staging is a bottleneck</h3><p>A single long-lived staging environment is contended: many branches and tenants share it, so a broken change blocks <em>everyone</em>, state drifts as people poke at it, and after a few weeks “works on staging” stops meaning anything. You need an isolated, production-shaped stack for each change or tenant that exists only as long as it's needed.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: provision a full stack on a trigger</h3><p>On a trigger — a PR opened, a tenant requesting a preview — a controller provisions a <strong>complete stack</strong> (app + its datastores + routing) from the <em>same</em> manifests production uses, tags it with an <strong>owner</strong> and a <strong>TTL</strong>, and hands back a URL. Nothing is pre-allocated; the environment is created on demand and is meant to be thrown away.</p><figure><figcaption>create → use → destroy</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">trigger: PR opened / tenant requests preview</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">controller provisions an isolated stack from prod manifests</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">owner + TTL tagged, HTTPS URL returned</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">used, then auto-torn-down</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: ephemeral is not stateless, and teardown is the hard part</h3><p>Each env still needs <strong>seeded or throwaway data</strong> to be useful — ephemeral ≠ stateless. And the whole value collapses if environments <strong>leak</strong>: the genuinely hard engineering is disciplined teardown (10·5), not creation. They must also be <strong>cheap and fast to create</strong> — if spin-up takes ten minutes, people route around the platform and go back to sharing staging.</p><figure><figcaption>shared staging vs ephemeral previews</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Shared staging</div><div class="lb">One env, contended by all; a bad change blocks everyone; drifts over time.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Ephemeral previews</div><div class="lb">Many isolated envs, one per change/tenant; disposable; no lasting footprint.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎬 Analogy — a film set</span>A set is built for one shoot, used, then struck — nobody keeps a hundred half-standing sets around. Each preview is a set built for one change and struck when the scene's done.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 This is the Emergent preview model</span>A per-app/per-tenant preview that spins up on demand and sleeps or dies when idle is exactly the product pattern here — the same lifecycle you debug in support (wake, idle-sleep, teardown) is what this stage builds.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Built on the Stage-8 operator</span>The controller that provisions and reaps these stacks is your custom operator from Stage 8; teardown discipline is 10·5.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Created on demand, per tenant/branch — not pre-allocated.</li><li>Full prod-shaped stack (app + data + routing), not a mock.</li><li>Tagged with an owner + a TTL so it can be reaped.</li><li>Ephemeral ≠ stateless — still needs seeded/throwaway data.</li><li>Must be cheap+fast to create, or people won't use it.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What makes an environment “ephemeral”?</li><li>Why is a single shared staging a bottleneck?</li><li>What's the hardest part of a preview platform — creation or teardown?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">10·2</span><h2>vcluster vs Namespace Isolation</h2><span class="tag">how strong a wall?</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: sharing one cluster leaks between tenants</h3><p>Previews share one physical cluster to save money — but sharing leaks. A namespace boundary scopes <em>namespaced</em> objects, yet <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> resources (CRDs, ClusterRoles, PriorityClasses) are shared, and a noisy or misconfigured tenant can still affect neighbors. The question is: how strong must the wall between environments be?</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: two isolation levels</h3><p><strong>Namespace isolation</strong> draws a boundary inside a shared control plane — RBAC, <code>NetworkPolicy</code>, and <code>ResourceQuota</code> confine each env, but they all talk to the <em>same</em> API server and share cluster-scoped objects. <strong>vcluster</strong> runs a <strong>virtual cluster</strong>: its own API server and controller-manager as pods inside a host namespace, so each tenant sees what looks like a full cluster (own CRDs, own cluster-admin) while the actual workloads still schedule onto the shared host nodes.</p><figure><figcaption>a spectrum of isolation, priced by blast radius</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Namespace</div><div class="lb">Light &amp; dense; shared API server; cluster-scoped objects shared.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">vcluster</div><div class="lb">Own virtual API server + CRDs; workloads run on shared nodes.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Separate cluster</div><div class="lb">Hardest wall; own control plane + nodes; most cost.</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: pick by threat model, not by default</h3><p>Choose by what the tenant needs and who you're isolating <em>from</em>. A <strong>namespace</strong> is enough for trusted internal branches. Reach for a <strong>vcluster</strong> when a tenant must install their own operators/CRDs or wants cluster-admin without touching neighbors. Neither is a hard <em>security</em> boundary — both share the host kernel, so a kernel escape still crosses; only separate clusters/VMs or sandboxed runtimes give that. The cost of vcluster is a control plane per tenant (memory + a little API latency).</p><figure><figcaption>decide the isolation level</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">tenant needs own CRDs / cluster-admin?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">yes</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">give it a vcluster (own virtual control plane)</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">no</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">a namespace + RBAC/NetPol/Quota is enough</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏢 Analogy — floor vs building</span>A namespace is a <strong>locked floor</strong> in a shared building; a vcluster is a <strong>mini-building</strong> on the same lot; a separate cluster is a building across town. More isolation, more rent.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Isolation is a spectrum, not a switch</span>“Isolated” isn't binary — it's a dial from namespace → vcluster → cluster → VM, each buying more blast-radius protection at more cost. Architects name the level, not just “isolate it.”</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Neither survives a kernel escape</span>vcluster and namespaces share the host kernel. For hostile multi-tenancy you need separate nodes/clusters or a sandbox runtime (gVisor/Kata) — don't oversell soft isolation as a security boundary.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Namespace = light, shared control plane, cluster-scoped objects shared.</li><li>vcluster = own virtual API server/CRDs; workloads on shared nodes.</li><li>Separate cluster/VM = hardest wall, highest cost.</li><li>Pick by threat model + whether tenants need cluster-scoped resources.</li><li>Soft isolation shares the kernel — not a security hard-wall.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a namespace <em>not</em> isolate?</li><li>When is a vcluster worth its overhead?</li><li>Why isn't a vcluster a real security boundary against a hostile tenant?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">10·3</span><h2>external-dns &amp; cert-manager</h2><span class="tag">auto domains + TLS</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: every env needs a working HTTPS URL, hands-off</h3><p>Each ephemeral environment needs its own unique, reachable <strong>HTTPS URL</strong>. Wiring DNS records and TLS certificates by hand doesn't scale to hundreds of short-lived envs — and a preview that throws a certificate warning is worthless to whoever you asked to test it.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: two controllers reconciling desired → actual</h3><p><strong>external-dns</strong> watches <code>Ingress</code>/<code>Service</code> objects and writes the matching records into your DNS provider (Route 53, Cloudflare, …) automatically. <strong>cert-manager</strong> watches for <code>Certificate</code> resources / ingress annotations and obtains TLS certs from an <strong>issuer</strong> — Let's Encrypt over ACME, or an internal CA — then renews them before expiry. Both are just the reconciling-controller pattern again: declare the hostname, the records and cert appear.</p><figure><figcaption>auto-wire access when an env comes up</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">env up → Ingress created</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">external-dns writes the DNS record</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">cert-manager requests a cert from the issuer (ACME)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">TLS issued → working HTTPS URL live</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: wildcard vs per-host, and the latency it adds</h3><p>Hundreds of envs under <code>*.preview.example.com</code> are far better served by <strong>one wildcard cert</strong> (a DNS-01 ACME challenge) than by issuing a fresh per-host cert (HTTP-01) for every env — the latter hammers Let's Encrypt <strong>rate limits</strong> and fails. Remember that DNS propagation + ACME issuance add seconds-to-minutes to “env ready,” so a wildcard (or pre-provisioned) cert keeps spin-up fast (ties back to 10·1's “cheap to create”).</p><figure><figcaption>per-host vs wildcard</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">HTTP-01 per-host</div><div class="lb">A cert per env; simple, but burns ACME rate limits at scale.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">DNS-01 wildcard</div><div class="lb">One *.preview cert covers every subdomain; fast, rate-limit-safe.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🪧 Analogy — sign-writer + locksmith</span>external-dns is an automatic sign-writer that posts each pop-up's address; cert-manager is the locksmith that fits its locks — both show up the moment the shop opens, no call needed.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Same operator pattern, again</span>Neither tool is special magic — both are controllers reconciling a declared state (a hostname) into a real-world side effect (a DNS record, a cert). Once you see it, every k8s add-on looks the same.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Rate limits &amp; propagation are real</span>Let's Encrypt caps issuance per domain per week; a cert-per-env pattern hits it fast. Prefer wildcard/DNS-01 and pre-warm, and budget DNS-propagation time into “env ready.”</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>external-dns = auto DNS records from Ingress/Service objects.</li><li>cert-manager = auto TLS issuance + renewal from an issuer (ACME/CA).</li><li>Every preview gets a real HTTPS URL, zero manual wiring.</li><li>Prefer a DNS-01 wildcard cert at scale — avoids ACME rate limits.</li><li>Issuance/propagation add latency — count it in spin-up time.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What object does external-dns react to, and what does it produce?</li><li>Why is a wildcard/DNS-01 cert better than per-host at scale?</li><li>What two latencies do these add to “env ready”?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">10·4</span><h2>Argo CD ApplicationSets</h2><span class="tag">stamp out many instances</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: one template, N tenants, all kept in sync</h3><p>You have <em>one</em> preview-app template but <strong>N</strong> tenants/branches, each needing its own Argo <code>Application</code>, each kept in sync, each created and deleted as tenants come and go. Hand-writing and maintaining an <code>Application</code> per env doesn't scale and drifts the moment someone edits one by hand.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: generator + template = for-each</h3><p>An <strong>ApplicationSet</strong> is a controller with a <strong>template</strong> and one or more <strong>generators</strong>. A generator produces a <em>list of items</em> — one per PR (the PR generator), one per Git directory, one per registered cluster, or a static list — and the controller renders the <code>Application</code> template once per item, creating/updating/deleting Applications automatically as that list changes. It's a declarative <code>for-each</code> over your environments.</p><figure><figcaption>one definition → many Applications</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">generator lists items (per PR / dir / cluster)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">template rendered once per item</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">an Argo Application created for each</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">Argo CD keeps each env in sync with Git</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: this is what makes previews self-service</h3><p>Wire the <strong>PR generator</strong> and the platform becomes self-serve: open a PR → an item appears → an <code>Application</code> is created → Argo syncs the env; close the PR → the item disappears → the <code>Application</code> (and, via a teardown hook, the whole env) is removed. Git/PR state is the single source of truth and drift self-heals. Two cautions: a bad edit to the <em>template</em> fans out to every env at once, and whether deletion truly tears down workloads depends on <code>preserveResourcesOnDeletion</code> and finalizers (10·5).</p><figure><figcaption>PR drives the env in and out</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode accent">PR open?</div><div class="fsplit"><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel yes">yes</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">item present → Application created → env up</div></div><div class="fbranch"><span class="blabel no">no</span><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">item gone → Application removed → env torn down</div></div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🍪 Analogy — a cookie-cutter from Git</span>One master shape (the template) presses out an identical Application for each item the generator lists — all driven from one design in Git, not hand-cut one by one.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Generators are the “for-each” of GitOps</span>Plain Argo <code>Application</code> = one app. ApplicationSet adds iteration: “for each PR / cluster / directory, make one.” That single idea is what turns GitOps into a fleet manager.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The knot that ties the stage together</span>The PR generator is 10·1's on-demand <em>trigger</em>; its item disappearing is 10·5's teardown <em>signal</em>. ApplicationSet is the mechanism connecting create and destroy.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>ApplicationSet = controller + template + generator(s).</li><li>Generator lists items (per PR / dir / cluster / static).</li><li>Template rendered once per item → many Applications.</li><li>PR generator makes previews self-service (open=up, close=down).</li><li>Template edits fan out to all envs — change carefully.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What are the two moving parts of an ApplicationSet?</li><li>How does the PR generator make previews self-service?</li><li>What's the risk of editing the shared template?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">10·5</span><h2>Lifecycle &amp; Teardown</h2><span class="tag">clean up automatically</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: the envs that never die</h3><p>Creating environments is easy; the failure mode of <em>every</em> preview platform is envs that <strong>never die</strong> — orphaned namespaces, dangling PVCs, stale DNS records, forgotten cloud load balancers, spend accruing on stacks nobody's used in weeks. Beyond cost, each zombie env is a security liability: old secrets and old code still reachable.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: bind every env to a trigger and a TTL</h3><p>Give each env an explicit lifecycle tied to a <strong>trigger</strong> (PR merged/closed, tenant done) <em>and</em> a <strong>TTL</strong>, with a <strong>reaper</strong> controller that sweeps envs past expiry. Teardown must be <strong>complete</strong> — not just the Deployment, but PVCs, DNS records, certs, external load balancers, and secrets. <strong>Finalizers</strong> hold the namespace open until dependent cloud resources are actually released, so nothing is orphaned behind a deleted namespace.</p><figure><figcaption>the full lifecycle</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">create on demand (owner + TTL)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">active — in use</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">trigger: PR closed / TTL expiry (reaper)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">ordered, complete teardown → all resources released</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: order it, make it idempotent, and choose sleep vs destroy</h3><p>Teardown has an <strong>order</strong>: drain traffic → remove ingress/DNS → delete workloads → release volumes — and it must be <strong>idempotent</strong> so a half-torn-down env fully cleans on retry. Decide a <strong>data policy</strong> (snapshot before destroy if anything's worth keeping). And weigh <strong>destroy vs idle-sleep</strong>: fully destroying costs nothing but rebuilds cold; scaling to zero and waking on demand is fast but leaves a small residual footprint — exactly the idle-sleep/wake trade-off behind Emergent's preview pods.</p><figure><figcaption>destroy vs idle-sleep</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Destroy on expiry</div><div class="lb">Zero residual cost; full cold rebuild on next use.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Idle-sleep / scale-to-zero</div><div class="lb">Fast wake; small residual cost (storage/DNS kept warm).</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏨 Analogy — hotel checkout</span>Checkout cleans the room for the next guest — nothing lingers, nothing is billed forever. Idle-sleep is more like leaving the key active but the lights off between stays.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 A TTL + reaper is the only reliable teardown</span>Never rely on a human remembering to delete an env. A default TTL and a reaper that enforces it is what keeps the platform from silently rotting into a cost and security problem.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Incomplete teardown is the #1 leak</span>Deleting the Deployment but leaking its PVC, DNS record, or cloud load balancer is the most common (and most expensive) mistake. Use finalizers to release cloud resources <em>before</em> the namespace disappears.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Every env gets a trigger + a TTL; a reaper enforces expiry.</li><li>Teardown must be complete: workloads, PVCs, DNS, certs, LBs, secrets.</li><li>Finalizers release cloud resources before namespace deletion.</li><li>Order teardown (drain → ingress/DNS → workloads → volumes); make it idempotent.</li><li>Destroy vs idle-sleep = cost-to-zero vs fast-wake trade-off.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why is a TTL + reaper essential rather than manual deletion?</li><li>Name three resources beyond the Deployment that must be torn down.</li><li>What's the trade-off between destroy and idle-sleep?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s11"><span>◆ STAGE 11 — CLOUD, IaC &amp; MULTI-REGION ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·1</span><h2>VPC &amp; Networking (SG vs NACL)</h2><span class="tag">the private cloud network</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: a flat network means one breach reaches everything</h3><p>Everything you run in the cloud sits on <em>some</em> network. If that network is flat and public, a single compromised host can reach your database directly. You need a private, segmented network where reachability is <strong>deliberate</strong> — nothing talks to anything unless you said so.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: a VPC, subnets, and two firewall layers</h3><p>A <strong>VPC</strong> is your own isolated IP space, carved into <strong>subnets</strong> across Availability Zones: <em>public</em> subnets (routed to an internet gateway) for load balancers, <em>private</em> subnets (egress via NAT, no inbound from the internet) for app and data. On top sit two firewalls: <strong>Security Groups</strong> — attached to a resource, <strong>stateful</strong> (return traffic auto-allowed), allow-rules only — and <strong>NACLs</strong> — attached to a subnet, <strong>stateless</strong> (each direction evaluated), and able to <em>deny</em>.</p><figure><figcaption>layers of the network, outside → in</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">Internet → Internet Gateway</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">public subnet: load balancer (ALB)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">private subnet: app pods (NAT for egress)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">private subnet: database — no internet path</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: defense in depth, and where each layer earns its keep</h3><p><strong>Security Groups are your primary control</strong> and their superpower is referencing <em>other SGs</em> — “the app SG may reach the db SG on 5432” — an identity rule that survives autoscaling where IPs churn. <strong>NACLs are a coarse backstop</strong>: block a CIDR, quarantine a subnet. Because NACLs are stateless you must remember to open <strong>ephemeral return ports</strong>, or connections silently hang. Spanning subnets across AZs (with matching route tables) is what makes the network survive an AZ loss.</p><figure><figcaption>Security Group vs NACL</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Security Group</div><div class="lb">Per-resource, stateful, allow-only; can reference other SGs (identity-based).</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">NACL</div><div class="lb">Per-subnet, stateless, allow + deny by CIDR; a coarse subnet-wide backstop.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏘️ Analogy — a gated community</span>NACLs are the neighborhood's street-level rules (who may enter this block at all); SGs are the per-house door locks (who may enter <em>this</em> house). Two independent layers deciding who reaches what.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 SG-to-SG references beat IP rules</span>Referencing an SG instead of a CIDR means the rule keeps working as pods scale in/out and IPs change — you're allowing an <em>identity</em>, not an address. This is the cloud-native way to write network policy.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Stateless NACLs bite on return traffic</span>Forgetting to allow the ephemeral high-port range for return packets is the classic NACL mistake — the request goes out, the reply is dropped, and it looks like a mysterious timeout. Also: cross-AZ traffic is billable.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>VPC = your isolated cloud network; public vs private subnets across AZs.</li><li>Private subnets hold app + data; only LBs live public.</li><li>SG = stateful, per-resource, allow-only, references other SGs.</li><li>NACL = stateless, per-subnet, supports explicit deny by CIDR.</li><li>Span subnets across AZs for HA; watch cross-AZ egress cost.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why put databases in a private subnet?</li><li>Which layer is stateful, and why does that matter?</li><li>What's the advantage of referencing an SG instead of a CIDR?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·2</span><h2>IAM &amp; IRSA</h2><span class="tag">least-privilege cloud identity</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: static keys in workloads are the #1 breach vector</h3><p>Your app needs to call cloud APIs — read an S3 bucket, publish to a queue. The lazy answer is to bake an access key into the image or an env var. That key is <strong>long-lived, over-broad, and un-rotatable</strong>; it leaks into logs, Git history, and crash dumps. Leaked static credentials are the single most common cause of cloud breaches.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: IAM roles + IRSA give each pod its own short-lived identity</h3><p><strong>IAM</strong> models access as <em>principals</em> (users, roles) plus <em>policies</em> (JSON allow/deny over actions, resources, conditions). Prefer <strong>roles</strong> (assumed → temporary creds) over users (static keys). <strong>IRSA</strong> ties a Kubernetes <code>ServiceAccount</code> to an IAM role through an OIDC trust: the pod presents a signed SA token, STS exchanges it (<code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code>) for <strong>short-lived, scoped</strong> AWS credentials. No secret is ever stored.</p><figure><figcaption>how a pod gets credentials with IRSA</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">pod's ServiceAccount token (signed)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">cluster OIDC provider — trusted by the IAM role</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">STS AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">short-lived, scoped creds — nothing baked into the image</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: least privilege is a practice, and trust is the real perimeter</h3><p>Least privilege means scoping <strong>actions AND resources AND conditions</strong> — start from deny-all and add only what breaks. <strong>Explicit deny always wins</strong>, and <em>permission boundaries</em> cap the maximum a role can ever hold. The same pattern exists everywhere — GCP Workload Identity, Azure Workload Identity — all OIDC federation. The classic IRSA hole isn't the permissions but the <strong>trust policy</strong>: too broad a <code>sub</code> claim lets <em>any</em> pod assume the role.</p><figure><figcaption>static key vs IRSA role</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Static access key</div><div class="lb">Long-lived, often over-broad, leaks easily, manual rotation.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">IRSA role</div><div class="lb">Short-lived, tightly scoped, auto-rotated, fully auditable.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🪪 Analogy — job-specific keycards</span>Instead of copying the building master key into every worker's pocket (static key), each worker gets their own card that opens only their doors and expires each shift (IRSA).</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Federation replaces stored secrets</span>IRSA's real idea: trust a token <em>issuer</em> rather than store a credential. Once you see identity as “a claim someone else vouches for,” OIDC/SAML/workload-identity all become the same move.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ The trust policy is the hole, not the permissions</span>A wildcard or overly broad OIDC <code>sub</code> condition lets any ServiceAccount assume the role — scope the <em>trust</em> as tightly as the permissions, or least-privilege on the policy is moot.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>IAM = principals + policies (actions, resources, conditions).</li><li>Prefer roles (temporary creds) over users (static keys).</li><li>IRSA = per-pod AWS identity via OIDC → STS short-lived creds.</li><li>Least privilege: deny-all, add minimally; explicit deny wins.</li><li>Scope the OIDC trust policy tightly, not just the permissions.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What security problem does IRSA remove entirely?</li><li>What does a permission boundary do that a policy doesn't?</li><li>Where's the classic misconfiguration in an IRSA setup?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·3</span><h2>Managed Services (EKS/RDS/…)</h2><span class="tag">rent, don't self-host</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: self-hosting stateful infra is undifferentiated heavy lifting</h3><p>Running your own database, Kafka, or cache means <em>you</em> own patching, backups, failover, version upgrades, and the 3am page when a disk fills. None of that makes your product better than a competitor's — it's <strong>undifferentiated heavy lifting</strong>, and it quietly consumes the team.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: rent AWS-run building blocks</h3><p>Instead you rent managed primitives — <strong>RDS/Aurora</strong> (databases), <strong>ElastiCache</strong> (cache), <strong>MSK</strong> (Kafka), <strong>S3</strong> (objects), <strong>EKS</strong> (the k8s control plane). Each gives you built-in multi-AZ HA, automated backups, and patching behind an API. The <strong>shared-responsibility line</strong> moves: AWS runs and secures the service itself; you configure and secure <em>your use</em> of it.</p><figure><figcaption>offload the operations</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">need a database / cluster / cache</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">use the managed service</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">AWS handles patching, backups, multi-AZ failover</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">you configure + secure your usage</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: you trade cost, control, and lock-in for ops relief</h3><p>Managed costs more per unit and hides tuning knobs — you can't SSH in and you inherit their upgrade cadence. Choose <strong>self-host</strong> when you need a capability/version the managed one won't give, or at a scale where the premium dwarfs an ops hire. The architect's move is to <strong>name the shared-responsibility boundary explicitly</strong> and weigh portability: sticking to open protocols (the Postgres wire protocol, the S3 API) keeps an exit and limits lock-in.</p><figure><figcaption>self-host vs managed</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Self-host</div><div class="lb">Full control, cheapest unit cost — you own all the ops and the pager.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Managed</div><div class="lb">Less control + some lock-in, higher unit cost — AWS owns the ops.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏨 Analogy — a serviced apartment</span>You could build and maintain a whole house (self-host), or rent a serviced apartment where plumbing, cleaning, and repairs are handled — you pay a premium to never think about the boiler.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Pay to delete non-differentiating work</span>The core cloud value proposition is buying back engineering time from work that doesn't distinguish you. Reserve self-hosting for where control genuinely <em>is</em> your differentiator.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The bill is a design input</span>Managed convenience shows up on the invoice — autoscaling, storage, and egress add up fast. That's why FinOps (11·6) treats cost as an architecture property, not an afterthought.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Managed = built-in HA/backups/patching behind an API.</li><li>Shared responsibility: AWS runs the service, you secure your use.</li><li>Trade: higher unit cost + less control + lock-in for ops relief.</li><li>Self-host only where control is a real differentiator.</li><li>Prefer open protocols (Postgres/S3 API) to keep an exit.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What is “undifferentiated heavy lifting”?</li><li>What exactly do you trade for a managed service?</li><li>How do open protocols reduce lock-in?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·4</span><h2>Terraform (IaC)</h2><span class="tag">infrastructure as versioned code</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: console clicking makes snowflake infrastructure</h3><p>Building infra by clicking in the console produces <strong>snowflakes</strong> — undocumented, unreproducible, un-reviewable resources nobody can recreate in a second region or after a disaster. Over time reality <em>drifts</em> from anyone's mental model, and there's no history of who changed what or why.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: declare desired state; plan then apply</h3><p>You declare the <strong>desired</strong> infrastructure as code. Terraform compares it against a recorded <strong>state file</strong> (its record of what exists), shows a <code>plan</code> (the diff), and on <code>apply</code> reconciles the real cloud to match. <strong>Remote state</strong> (e.g. S3 + a lock table) shares that state safely across a team, and <strong>modules</strong> package reusable, composable infra so a “VPC” or “service” is defined once and stamped out.</p><figure><figcaption>declare → plan → apply</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">write infra as code (reusable modules)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">plan: diff desired vs recorded state</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">review the plan like a PR</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">apply: reconcile the cloud to the code</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: the state file is both crown jewel and footgun</h3><p>State holds the mapping to real resources <em>and</em> often secrets — so it must never be committed, must be <strong>locked</strong> (a concurrent apply corrupts it), and drifts must be reconciled (<code>import</code>, drift detection). Declarative + plan-review gives infra the same rigor as application code. But a plan is not a guarantee — provider races and eventual consistency happen — so <strong>pin provider versions</strong> and test modules (Terratest).</p><figure><figcaption>console vs IaC</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Console clicking</div><div class="lb">Snowflakes, silent drift, no history or review.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Infrastructure as code</div><div class="lb">Reproducible, peer-reviewed, versioned, diffable.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">📐 Analogy — a blueprint</span>IaC is a blueprint you can rebuild the entire building from, exactly, at any time — and you diff and review the blueprint before you move a single wall.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 It's the reconciliation loop again</span>Desired-state → observe actual → converge is the same engine behind Kubernetes controllers and Argo CD. Terraform is that loop applied to cloud infrastructure — one mental model covers all three.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Protect and lock the state</span>State is sensitive and single-writer: never commit it, always use locked remote state. Two unlocked applies racing on the same state can corrupt or destroy live infrastructure.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Declarative, versioned, reviewable infrastructure.</li><li>plan = the diff (desired vs state); apply = converge reality.</li><li>Remote, locked state shared across the team; modules for reuse.</li><li>State is sensitive + single-writer — never commit, always lock.</li><li>Pin providers; test modules (Terratest); reconcile drift.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does a Terraform plan actually show you?</li><li>Why must remote state be locked?</li><li>Why is a plan not an absolute guarantee of the result?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·5</span><h2>Multi-Region (active-passive → active-active)</h2><span class="tag">survive a region outage</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: one region is one fault domain</h3><p>Multi-AZ survives the loss of a data center, but an entire <strong>region</strong> can fail — including its control plane. Some workloads must survive that. Multi-region is expensive and genuinely hard, though, so you justify it with concrete <strong>RPO/RTO</strong> targets, not reflex — most systems don't need it, and paying for it blindly is over-engineering.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: replicate data, route around failure</h3><p>You replicate data to another region and steer traffic away from failure. <strong>Active-passive</strong>: one region serves while a warm standby holds replicated data and health-based routing (Route 53) fails over on outage. <strong>Active-active</strong>: both regions serve live. There's a maturity ladder — <em>backup/restore → pilot light → warm standby → active-active</em> — each rung lowering RTO/RPO at higher cost and complexity.</p><figure><figcaption>the DR maturity ladder (RTO/RPO ↓, cost ↑)</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">backup &amp; restore (hours)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">pilot light (core always on)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">warm standby (scaled-down live copy)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">active-active (both serve, near-zero RTO)</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: the hard part is data, not compute</h3><p>Spinning up compute elsewhere is easy; keeping <em>data</em> correct is the problem. <strong>Async</strong> replication → a non-zero <strong>RPO</strong> (you can lose the last few seconds on failover); <strong>sync</strong> replication removes that but taxes every write with cross-region latency. Active-active forces you to resolve <strong>write conflicts</strong> (last-writer-wins, CRDTs, or region-sharded ownership). And failover you never rehearse won't work — <strong>game-day</strong> it, and design against split-brain.</p><figure><figcaption>active-passive vs active-active</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Active-passive</div><div class="lb">One region serves; a standby holds replicated data and takes over on failover.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Active-active</div><div class="lb">Both regions serve live traffic; requires real data-conflict handling.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏢 Analogy — a backup HQ</span>Active-passive is a fully-staffed backup HQ in another city ready to take over; active-active is a second HQ that <em>also</em> serves customers every day. The second is far harder to keep in sync.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 RPO/RTO pick the architecture</span>Set how much data you can lose (RPO) and how fast you must recover (RTO) <em>first</em>, then buy the cheapest ladder rung that meets them. The targets drive the design — not the other way around.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Untested failover is theater</span>Replication lag (RPO), split-brain, and stale DNS TTLs all surface only during a real outage. If you haven't run a game day, assume your failover is broken.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>One region = one fault domain; justify multi-region by RPO/RTO.</li><li>Ladder: backup/restore → pilot light → warm standby → active-active.</li><li>Async replication → non-zero RPO; sync → latency tax.</li><li>Active-active must resolve write conflicts (LWW/CRDT/sharding).</li><li>Rehearse failover (game days); design against split-brain.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why justify multi-region with RPO/RTO rather than defaulting to it?</li><li>Why is data the hard part, not compute?</li><li>What must active-active solve that active-passive doesn't?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">11·6</span><h2>FinOps</h2><span class="tag">make cloud cost visible</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: cloud spend is emergent and silent</h3><p>In the cloud every engineer can provision resources, cost accrues by the second, and the bill only arrives at month-end — aggregated past the point of recognition. Waste (idle capacity, over-provisioned instances, forgotten Stage-10 preview envs) hides inside the total until someone gets a nasty surprise.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: make cost a first-class, visible signal</h3><p>FinOps pulls cost <em>left</em> and makes it observable: <strong>estimate the cost of a change in CI</strong> (Infracost on the PR), <strong>tag every resource</strong> (team / env / feature) so the bill can be allocated, set <strong>budgets and alerts</strong>, and right-size continuously. It's as much a culture as a tool — engineering, finance, and product jointly owning spend through <em>inform → optimize → operate</em>.</p><figure><figcaption>cost as a first-class concern</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">estimate cost of the change in CI (Infracost)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">tag every resource for allocation</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">budgets + alerts on overspend</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">right-size &amp; kill waste continuously</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: cost is an architecture property, measured in unit economics</h3><p>Cost is the Well-Architected <strong>Cost Optimization</strong> pillar, not an afterthought. Levers: right-sizing, autoscaling to demand, <strong>Savings Plans/Reserved</strong> for steady load + <strong>Spot</strong> for interruptible work, storage tiering, and killing zombies (Stage-10 teardown, cross-AZ/region egress from 11·1/11·5). The metric that matters is <strong>unit economics</strong> — $ per job or per tenant — because that, not total spend, tells you whether growth is profitable.</p><figure><figcaption>reactive billing vs FinOps</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Untagged / reactive</div><div class="lb">Month-end surprise; waste hidden; no attribution.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">FinOps</div><div class="lb">Attributable, forecasted, right-sized; unit economics tracked.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔌 Analogy — a smart meter</span>FinOps is a smart meter for the cloud bill, broken down per team and per feature in real time — so nobody is shocked at month-end and waste shows up while it's still cheap to fix.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Track $/job, not just the total</span>A falling total can still hide a rising cost <em>per unit of value</em>. Unit economics ($ per job/tenant) is the number that says whether you scale profitably — architects optimize that, not the invoice.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ The silent killers</span>Forgotten preview environments (Stage 10), cross-AZ/region egress, and “just-in-case” over-provisioning quietly dominate real bills. Default TTLs + mandatory tagging are what catch them early.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Estimate cost before merge (Infracost); shift cost left.</li><li>Tag everything for allocation; set budgets + alerts.</li><li>It's the Well-Architected Cost Optimization pillar.</li><li>Levers: right-size, autoscale, Savings Plans/Spot, tiering, kill zombies.</li><li>Optimize unit economics ($/job or $/tenant), not just total spend.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why estimate cost in CI instead of at month-end?</li><li>What does tagging enable that a raw bill can't?</li><li>Why are unit economics more useful than total spend?</li></ol></div></section><div class="stage-banner" id="s12"><span>◆ STAGE 12 — OBSERVABILITY, SRE &amp; CAPSTONE ⭐ ◆</span></div><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·1</span><h2>The Three Pillars</h2><span class="tag">metrics, logs, traces</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: no single signal answers both “is it broken?” and “why?”</h3><p>Metrics tell you something's wrong but not why; logs explain one moment but hide trends and cross-service flow; traces show a request's path but aren't where you set alarms. Lean on one alone and every incident stalls at the handoff between “detect” and “diagnose.” You need all three, and — crucially — <strong>correlated</strong>.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: three signals, each cheapest at one job</h3><p><strong>Metrics</strong> are cheap aggregated numeric time-series — detection, alerting, dashboards. <strong>Logs</strong> are high-detail discrete events — the exact error at a moment. <strong>Traces</strong> are a request's span tree across services — where the latency went. The glue is a shared <strong>request/trace id</strong> (your Stage-0 correlation ID): it stitches a metric spike to the trace to the log line.</p><figure><figcaption>what each pillar is best at</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Metrics</div><div class="lb">Trends &amp; alerting — “something is wrong, now.”</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Logs</div><div class="lb">The exact event at a moment — “here's the error.”</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Traces</div><div class="lb">One request across services — “here's where time went.”</div></div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: the design axis is cost × cardinality × retention</h3><p>Each signal is priced differently. Metrics are cheapest but <strong>explode with high-cardinality labels</strong> (never label by <code>user_id</code>/<code>job_id</code>). Logs are mid-cost — index vs raw-volume trade. Traces are expensive, so you <strong>sample</strong>. The mature move is to correlate rather than duplicate: an <em>exemplar</em> links a metric bucket to a representative trace, so you jump signal-to-signal instead of paying to store everything three ways.</p><figure><figcaption>the debugging path across pillars</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">alert fires (metric crosses SLO)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">open the trace — which service/span?</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">drill to logs for that request id</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">root cause — confirmed &amp; explained</div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏥 Analogy — a patient's chart</span>Metrics are the vitals monitor (trends, alarms), logs are the diary of what happened, traces are the map of the patient's journey through the hospital. A doctor needs all three.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Correlation is the whole game</span>Without a shared id tying them together you have three disconnected tools and a manual hunt. With it, one click walks alert → trace → log. Instrumenting the id (Stage 0) is what makes observability, not just monitoring.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Cardinality is the metrics killer</span>A single unbounded label (user, job, request id) multiplies your time-series into millions and wrecks TSDB cost/performance. Keep metric labels low-cardinality; push the high-cardinality detail into logs/traces.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Metrics = detect/alert; Logs = explain a moment; Traces = follow a request.</li><li>A shared request/trace id correlates all three (Stage 0).</li><li>Design by cost × cardinality × retention per signal.</li><li>Exemplars link a metric spike → a trace → logs.</li><li>Never put high-cardinality values in metric labels.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What is each pillar the cheapest answer for?</li><li>What single thing makes the three usable together?</li><li>Why is a <code>user_id</code> metric label dangerous?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·2</span><h2>OpenTelemetry</h2><span class="tag">vendor-neutral instrumentation</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: vendor SDKs lock you in and don't agree</h3><p>Instrument with a vendor's SDK and switching backends means re-instrumenting the entire codebase. Worse, if every language/agent propagates context its own way, a request's trace breaks at service boundaries — you lose the end-to-end view exactly where distributed systems need it most.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: one API, the OTLP protocol, and the Collector</h3><p><strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> is a vendor-neutral <em>API/SDK</em> + wire protocol (<strong>OTLP</strong>) + the <strong>Collector</strong>. You instrument once against the OTel API; the SDK emits traces/metrics/logs; <strong>context propagation</strong> (W3C <code>traceparent</code>) carries the trace across every service hop. The Collector receives, processes, and exports to any backend — so the app never knows or cares where signals land.</p><figure><figcaption>instrument once, export anywhere</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">app instrumented against the OTel API</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">SDK emits OTLP (traces/metrics/logs)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Collector: batch · sample · redact · route</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">export to any backend(s) — no lock-in</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: the Collector is where policy lives</h3><p>The Collector is the architectural leverage point: it does <strong>tail-based sampling</strong> (keep the interesting traces), <strong>PII redaction</strong>, and <strong>fan-out</strong> to several backends — centrally, so app teams never touch export config. <strong>Semantic conventions</strong> make a “http.status_code” mean the same thing everywhere, so signals are comparable across services and languages. Auto-instrumentation gets you coverage fast; manual spans add the business-meaningful detail.</p><figure><figcaption>vendor SDK vs OTel</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Vendor SDK</div><div class="lb">Re-instrument the whole codebase to switch backends.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">OpenTelemetry</div><div class="lb">Instrument once; change the backend in the Collector.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔌 Analogy — a universal adapter</span>One adapter that fits every monitoring socket: wire your code once, and plug into whichever backend you like — swap it without rewiring the house.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Centralize policy in the Collector</span>Sampling, redaction, and routing belong in the Collector, not scattered through app code. Move that policy once and every service inherits it — the same “decouple apps from infrastructure” instinct as a service mesh.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 Built on Stage-0 correlation IDs</span>Your hand-rolled request id becomes a standardized, propagated W3C trace context — the same idea, now portable across every service and vendor.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>OTel = vendor-neutral API/SDK + OTLP protocol + Collector.</li><li>Instrument once; swap backends without touching app code.</li><li>Context propagation (traceparent) keeps traces whole across hops.</li><li>Collector = policy point: tail-sampling, redaction, fan-out.</li><li>Semantic conventions make signals comparable everywhere.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does OTel free you from?</li><li>Why is the Collector the right place for sampling/redaction?</li><li>What keeps a trace intact across service boundaries?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·3</span><h2>Prometheus &amp; Alerting</h2><span class="tag">metrics + rules that page</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: queryable health + alerting, at scale, decoupled from apps</h3><p>You need numeric health you can query and alert on, for a fleet that's constantly scaling, without every app knowing where the monitoring system is or pushing to it reliably. And you need alerts that route to the right people without drowning them.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: pull, a labelled TSDB, PromQL, and Alertmanager</h3><p><strong>Prometheus</strong> <em>pulls</em> — it scrapes each service's <code>/metrics</code> on a schedule (targets found via service discovery). It stores <strong>time-series keyed by name + labels</strong>; <strong>PromQL</strong> queries them; recording rules precompute; alerting rules evaluate and hand firing alerts to <strong>Alertmanager</strong>, which dedupes, groups, silences, and routes to pager/Slack.</p><figure><figcaption>scrape → store → query → page</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">scrape /metrics from every target</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">store time-series (name + labels)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">PromQL recording + alerting rules</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">Alertmanager groups/routes → page or ticket</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: pull-vs-push, durability, and the cardinality ceiling</h3><p><strong>Pull</strong> gives you a free liveness signal (<code>up</code>) and easy dedup, but short-lived batch jobs can't be scraped — those <em>push</em> to a Pushgateway. Prometheus's local TSDB isn't HA or long-term, so <strong>remote-write</strong> to Thanos/Mimir/Cortex for durability + a global view. And the recurring failure mode is <strong>cardinality explosion</strong> — labels are a dimensional data model, powerful but the same footgun as 12·1. Alertmanager (grouping/inhibition) is where you kill noise — pair it with burn-rate (12·7).</p><figure><figcaption>pull vs push</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Pull (default)</div><div class="lb">Liveness (up), easy dedup, standard for long-lived services.</div></div><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Push (Pushgateway)</div><div class="lb">For short-lived batch jobs that can't be scraped.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🎛️ Analogy — a control room of gauges</span>Walls of gauges you can query, with an alarm wired to ring only when a reading crosses a genuine danger line — not for every flicker.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Labels are a dimensional model</span>name+labels is a mini query dimension — slice by service/route/code in PromQL. That power is exactly why an unbounded label detonates your series count.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Local storage isn't HA or long-term</span>A single Prometheus loses data on restart and can't see across clusters. Remote-write to Thanos/Mimir for durability, global query, and long retention.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Pull-based scraping of /metrics; service discovery finds targets.</li><li>Time-series = name + labels; PromQL queries + recording rules.</li><li>Alertmanager dedupes/groups/routes; where noise control lives.</li><li>Push (Pushgateway) only for short-lived jobs.</li><li>Remote-write (Thanos/Mimir) for HA + long-term + global view.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why does pull give you a liveness signal for free?</li><li>When must you push instead of scrape?</li><li>What does remote-write solve that local TSDB doesn't?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·4</span><h2>Grafana &amp; Loki</h2><span class="tag">dashboards + a single pane</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: signals in separate stores, and full-text logs cost a fortune</h3><p>Metrics, logs, and traces live in different systems; hopping tools mid-incident is slow and error-prone. And indexing every word of every log line (classic ELK) gets ruinously expensive at real volume — you pay to make searchable a haystack you rarely search.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: a query layer + a label-indexed log store</h3><p><strong>Grafana</strong> is a query + visualization layer over many datasources — one pane of glass, with <strong>correlation</strong>: click a metric exemplar → jump to the trace → jump to the logs for that label set. <strong>Loki</strong> indexes only <em>labels</em> and stores compressed log <em>chunks</em> in object storage; you narrow by labels then grep the stream with LogQL, instead of indexing content.</p><figure><figcaption>one pane of glass</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">Loki: label-index + chunks in object storage</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Prometheus metrics + traces alongside</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">Grafana queries all datasources</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">correlated dashboards — metric → trace → log</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: cheap because it indexes metadata, not content</h3><p>Loki's economy comes from indexing <em>labels</em>, not words — so it rewards disciplined labels and punishes high-cardinality ones and broad unfiltered greps (the 12·1/12·3 footgun again). Build dashboards <strong>as code</strong> (provisioned JSON) around the <strong>RED</strong> (rate/errors/duration) and <strong>USE</strong> (utilization/saturation/errors) methods — not a “wall of graphs” nobody reads. Shared labels + trace ids are what make the single pane actually connect.</p><figure><figcaption>full-text index vs Loki</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Full-text (ELK)</div><div class="lb">Fast arbitrary search — but indexes everything, costly at scale.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Loki</div><div class="lb">Indexes labels only, greps within a stream — far cheaper.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧱 Analogy — dashboards on the wall + a journal</span>Big status boards you read at a glance (Grafana) next to a searchable event journal (Loki) — and a thread tying an entry on the board to the exact page in the journal.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Index metadata, not content</span>Loki's whole trick — and its cost story — is indexing labels rather than every word. That single decision is what makes centralized logging affordable at fleet scale.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Same cardinality trap, and don't build graph walls</span>High-cardinality labels and broad greps kill Loki too; and a dashboard nobody reads is worse than none. Design to RED/USE and provision dashboards as code.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Grafana = query+viz over many datasources → one pane of glass.</li><li>Correlate: metric exemplar → trace → logs for that label set.</li><li>Loki indexes labels + stores chunks in object storage (cheap).</li><li>LogQL greps within a label-narrowed stream, not full text.</li><li>Dashboards as code; design to RED/USE, not graph walls.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What makes Loki cheaper than a full-text store?</li><li>How does Grafana connect the three pillars?</li><li>What do the RED and USE methods each cover?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·5</span><h2>Profiling (Pyroscope)</h2><span class="tag">find the hot code</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: traces name the slow span, not the slow line</h3><p>Metrics and traces tell you <em>which</em> service or endpoint is CPU-hungry or slow — but not <em>which function or line</em> is burning the cycles. Reproducing production performance on your laptop is unreliable, so you end up guessing at optimizations. You need line-level truth from prod itself.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: continuous statistical sampling → flame graphs</h3><p><strong>Continuous profiling</strong> samples the running app's call stacks at a low frequency in production, aggregates by function/line over time, and renders <strong>flame graphs</strong>. Because it's statistical sampling (not instrumenting every call), overhead is small enough to run always-on — so you can correlate a CPU or latency spike to the exact code path that caused it.</p><figure><figcaption>pinpoint the hot path</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">sample call stacks continuously (low overhead)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">aggregate by function / line over time</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">render a flame graph</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">the true CPU/memory hot-spot, in prod</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: the “fourth pillar” that explains a slow span</h3><p>Profiling closes the gap the other signals leave: link a <strong>trace span to its flame graph</strong> and you see <em>why</em> that span was slow, down to the line. Use it to prioritize optimization by <strong>measured prod cost</strong> (not intuition), to catch performance <strong>regressions</strong> across releases, and to find allocation hotspots driving GC pauses. The only real knob is sampling frequency — resolution vs overhead.</p><figure><figcaption>what each tool localizes</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane"><div class="lh">Metrics / traces</div><div class="lb">Which service or endpoint is slow.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Profiling</div><div class="lb">Which function / line burns CPU or memory.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🩻 Analogy — an X-ray of the code</span>Instead of “the patient is tired” (the endpoint is slow), an X-ray shows precisely which muscle is straining — which function is doing the work.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Often called the fourth pillar</span>Wired to traces, profiling answers the question traces raise: “this span took 400ms — doing <em>what</em>?” It turns a slow span into a specific line to fix.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Optimize by measurement, not intuition</span>The real hot path is rarely where you'd guess. Profile first; a day spent optimizing a cold path is a day wasted while the true bottleneck stands.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Continuous, low-overhead statistical sampling in production.</li><li>Aggregates by function/line → flame graphs.</li><li>Localizes hot-spots to the line — beyond metrics/traces.</li><li>Link a trace span to its profile to explain the slowness.</li><li>Finds regressions + GC-driving allocation hotspots.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does profiling localize that traces don't?</li><li>Why is it safe to run continuously in prod?</li><li>Why profile before optimizing?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·6</span><h2>SLO / SLI / Error Budgets</h2><span class="tag">define “good enough”</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: “make it reliable” is unmeasurable, and 100% is a trap</h3><p>“Reliable” with no number can't be managed, and <strong>100% is impossible and infinitely expensive</strong> — the last nines cost more than they're worth and users can't tell. Without an agreed target, every incident becomes a subjective argument and you can't decide when reliability work should beat features.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: measure, target, and budget the failure</h3><p>An <strong>SLI</strong> is a precise measurement of user-visible reliability (e.g. % of requests that are 2xx and &lt;300ms). An <strong>SLO</strong> is the target over a window (99.9% over 28 days). The <strong>error budget</strong> is <code>100% − SLO</code> — the failure you're <em>allowed</em>. Budget healthy → ship fast, take risks; budget spent → freeze features and invest in reliability.</p><figure><figcaption>turn reliability into a managed number</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">SLI — the measurement (success rate, latency)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">SLO — the target over a window (99.9% / 28d)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">error budget = 100% − SLO</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">budget policy gates how much risk you ship</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: measure from the user, and treat the budget as policy</h3><p>SLIs must be <strong>user-centric</strong> — measured at the edge, from the user's vantage, not internal CPU that can look green while users suffer. Choose the right SLI (availability, a latency <em>percentile</em>, freshness, correctness) and window (rolling vs calendar). The error budget's real power is as a <strong>policy tool</strong>: it turns reliability into a shared, spendable currency that depoliticizes “ship or stabilize?” — the same shape as your Stage-9 experiment guardrails.</p><figure><figcaption>aim-for-100% vs SLO + budget</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Aim for 100%</div><div class="lb">Infinite cost, still fails, no decision rule.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">SLO + error budget</div><div class="lb">Explicit target, spendable budget, aligns eng + product.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">⏱️ Analogy — an on-time promise with an allowance</span>“99.9% of trains on time” is the SLO; the small allowance of lateness is the error budget. Spend the allowance and you must slow down and fix the timetable.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 The budget is a currency</span>It converts “should we ship or stabilize?” from an argument into a data decision — spend left, ship; spend gone, stabilize. That alignment is the point, more than the number itself.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Server-side SLIs lie</span>A metric measured inside your service can read 100% while users hit CDN/LB/DNS failures. Measure the SLI from the user's edge or you're grading the wrong exam.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>SLI = measurement; SLO = target over a window; budget = 100−SLO.</li><li>100% is the wrong goal — the last nines aren't worth it.</li><li>Budget healthy → ship; budget spent → freeze &amp; stabilize.</li><li>Measure SLIs user-side (edge), not internal CPU.</li><li>The budget is a policy currency that aligns eng + product.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Define SLI, SLO, and error budget in one line each.</li><li>Why is 100% the wrong target?</li><li>Why must the SLI be measured from the user's side?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·7</span><h2>Burn-Rate Alerting</h2><span class="tag">alert on what matters</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: alert-on-everything trains people to ignore alerts</h3><p>Paging on every error spike or threshold breach floods humans with alerts for blips that never hurt users. The result is <strong>alert fatigue</strong> — and when everything pages, the one page that matters gets muted with the rest. You want to wake someone only when reliability is <em>genuinely</em> at risk.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: alert on how fast you burn the budget</h3><p><strong>Burn rate</strong> is how fast you're consuming the error budget relative to on-pace (1× spends it exactly over the window; 14.4× spends a month's budget in ~2 days). Alert on <em>burn rate</em>, not raw errors. <strong>Multi-window, multi-burn-rate</strong>: a fast-burn rule (high rate over a short window) pages <em>now</em>; a slow-burn rule (moderate rate over a long window) opens a ticket.</p><figure><figcaption>page on impact, not noise</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">measure error-budget burn rate</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">fast burn (short window) → page a human now</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">slow burn (long window) → ticket, not a 3am page</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">on pace → no alert at all</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: symptom-based, and thresholds derive from the budget</h3><p>Pick thresholds from <strong>how much budget you'll let an incident spend</strong> before engaging humans; the short+long window pair gives fast detection with few false positives (the long window confirms the short one isn't a blip). And prefer <strong>symptom-based</strong> alerting (user-visible SLO burn) over <strong>cause-based</strong> (“CPU high”) — page on impact, keep causes as <em>diagnostics</em>. This is 12·6's budget turned into a paging policy.</p><figure><figcaption>alert-on-everything vs burn-rate</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Alert on everything</div><div class="lb">Noisy, cause-based, breeds fatigue.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Multi-window burn-rate</div><div class="lb">Few, symptom-based, meaningful pages.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🔥 Analogy — spend-rate alarm</span>The alarm rings only when you're spending your allowance dangerously fast — not every time one light flickers. A slow drip gets a note; a gush gets a call.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Symptom over cause</span>Page on the user-visible SLO burn; make CPU, queue depth, and GC diagnostics you consult <em>after</em> the page. Cause-based pages are most of the noise and rarely map to real harm.</div><div class="callout bridge"><span class="ct">🔗 The budget, operationalized</span>12·6 defined the error budget; burn-rate alerting is how you actually watch it — the policy currency becomes a concrete paging rule.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Burn rate = budget-consumption speed vs on-pace.</li><li>Alert on burn rate, not raw error counts.</li><li>Multi-window (short+long) = fast detection, few false positives.</li><li>Fast burn → page; slow burn → ticket; on pace → nothing.</li><li>Symptom-based (SLO) over cause-based (CPU) alerting.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>What does burn rate actually measure?</li><li>Why combine a short and a long window?</li><li>Why prefer symptom-based over cause-based alerts?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·8</span><h2>Chaos Engineering</h2><span class="tag">prove resilience on purpose</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: unverified resilience is just hope</h3><p>You built retries, circuit breakers, and multi-AZ/region failover (Stages 4 and 11) — but you don't <em>know</em> they work until a real failure exercises them, and by then it's a live outage. A circuit breaker never tripped and a failover never triggered are unproven assumptions, not features.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: the scientific loop with a small blast radius</h3><p>Chaos engineering is an experiment: state a <strong>steady-state hypothesis</strong> (“kill a worker → no user-visible impact”), <strong>inject a controlled failure</strong> (kill pods, add latency/errors, sever a dependency), <strong>observe</strong> against the hypothesis, and either confirm resilience or discover a weakness. You start with a tiny <strong>blast radius</strong>, off-peak, with an abort switch — a rehearsal, not a gamble.</p><figure><figcaption>controlled failure</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">hypothesize steady state (“killing a worker → no impact”)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">inject a small, controlled failure</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">observe against the hypothesis</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">confirm resilience — or find the weakness first</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: GameDays, governance, and validating what you designed</h3><p>Mature practice runs as scheduled <strong>GameDays</strong> with governance: a capped blast radius, steady-state metrics wired to <strong>auto-abort</strong> (SLO-guarded), and a progression from staging → prod off-peak → continuous. It validates precisely the patterns you designed — breakers, retries with <em>backoff + jitter</em>, failover (11·5) — and the culture must be <strong>blameless</strong>: the goal is learning, not blame.</p><figure><figcaption>hope vs tested</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Hope it's resilient</div><div class="lb">Untested failure paths = unknown behavior in the real outage.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Chaos-tested</div><div class="lb">Weaknesses found on purpose, off-peak, before users hit them.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🧯 Analogy — a fire drill</span>You start a small, controlled fire to prove the sprinklers actually work — on your schedule, with the fire brigade standing by — rather than discovering they're broken during a real blaze.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 It validates the very patterns you built</span>Resilience engineering without chaos testing is a set of untested claims. The breaker, the retry, the failover only count once you've watched them catch a real injected failure.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Bound the blast radius or the drill becomes the outage</span>Always cap scope and wire an auto-abort to your SLOs (12·6). Chaos without a kill-switch and a small radius is just self-inflicted downtime.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>Inject failures deliberately to verify resilience claims.</li><li>Scientific loop: steady-state hypothesis → inject → observe.</li><li>Start tiny blast radius, off-peak, with an abort switch.</li><li>GameDays with SLO-guarded auto-abort; staging → prod progression.</li><li>Validates breakers/retries/failover; culture stays blameless.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why inject failures on purpose?</li><li>What governs a safe chaos experiment?</li><li>Which earlier-stage patterns does it validate?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·9</span><h2>Supply-Chain Security (SBOM/cosign/SLSA)</h2><span class="tag">trust what you ship</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: you can't trust an artifact just because your pipeline built it</h3><p>A modern build pulls hundreds of transitive dependencies and base images off the internet, and runs on a build server that's itself attackable. A compromised dependency or builder (SolarWinds, the xz backdoor) ships malware straight to prod under your own signature. “Our CI produced it” is no longer proof it's safe.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: know it, sign it, prove how it was built</h3><p>Three layers. <strong>SBOM</strong> — a machine-readable bill of materials listing every component + version, so you know what's inside and can scan it. <strong>cosign</strong> — cryptographically sign the artifact (ideally <em>keyless</em>, via OIDC identity) so you can verify authenticity + integrity. <strong>SLSA</strong> — a framework of <strong>provenance</strong> attestations proving <em>how</em> and <em>from what source</em> it was built, at graded maturity levels.</p><figure><figcaption>tamper-evident builds</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">build → generate SBOM (every component)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">cosign signs the image</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">SLSA records build provenance</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">admission gate verifies before it runs</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: verification must be an admission gate, not a report</h3><p>The point isn't the paperwork — it's <strong>enforcement</strong>. An admission controller (Stage 8) <em>rejects</em> unsigned images or ones failing policy, so verification is a gate on the way into the cluster, not a dashboard nobody reads. <strong>SLSA levels</strong> are a maturity ladder (source → build → provenance integrity). And SBOM + provenance together let you answer “are we affected by CVE-X?” across the whole fleet in minutes instead of days.</p><figure><figcaption>blind trust vs verified</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Trust the pipeline</div><div class="lb">Tamper-blind — a poisoned dep ships silently.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Signed + attested</div><div class="lb">Verify before run — tamper-evident, CVE-answerable.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🏷️ Analogy — ingredients, seal, certificate</span>An SBOM is the ingredients list, cosign is the tamper seal, and SLSA is the certificate of origin — together you can prove the package is exactly what the factory made.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Enforce at admission</span>A signature you never check is theatre. The value lands only when an admission gate refuses to run anything unsigned or non-compliant — verification as policy, not as a PDF.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ Transitive deps + base images are the real surface</span>Your own code is the small part; the risk is the hundreds of things it pulls in. An SBOM is what turns the next zero-day CVE from a week of grepping into a fleet-wide query.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>SBOM = every component/version → scannable inventory.</li><li>cosign = sign + verify artifacts (prefer keyless/OIDC).</li><li>SLSA = graded build provenance/attestation.</li><li>Enforce at an admission gate — reject unsigned/non-compliant.</li><li>SBOM + provenance = answer “affected by CVE-X?” fleet-wide fast.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why isn't “our pipeline built it” sufficient trust?</li><li>What does each of SBOM / cosign / SLSA give you?</li><li>Why must verification be an admission gate?</li></ol></div></section><section class="lesson"><div class="lesson-h"><span class="lnum">12·10</span><h2>Load Testing (k6)</h2><span class="tag">find the breaking point</span></div><h3 class="step"><span class="n">1</span>The problem: you don't know your ceiling until traffic finds it</h3><p>Without testing, your capacity limit and first bottleneck are guesses — and the worst possible time to discover them is during a launch or an organic traffic spike. “How many requests per second can we take, and what breaks first?” must be answered <em>deliberately</em>, before real users answer it for you.</p><h3 class="step"><span class="n">2</span>The mechanism: script realistic load and ramp until it breaks</h3><p><strong>k6</strong> scripts realistic scenarios (in JavaScript), models virtual users, and ramps load while you watch throughput, <strong>latency percentiles</strong>, and error rate. Test types map to questions: <em>load</em> (survive expected peak), <em>stress</em> (ramp past it to find the break), <em>soak</em> (sustained, to expose leaks), <em>spike</em> (sudden surge). The point where latency/errors turn upward is your <strong>capacity knee</strong>.</p><figure><figcaption>push until it breaks</figcaption><div class="flow"><div class="fnode">script realistic scenarios (k6)</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">ramp: load · stress · soak · spike</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode">watch latency-percentile + error vs throughput</div><div class="farrow">▼</div><div class="fnode accent">find the knee — capacity + first bottleneck</div></div></figure><h3 class="step"><span class="n">3</span>The nuance: model open arrivals, and find the FIRST bottleneck</h3><p>The subtle trap is the workload model. A <strong>closed</strong> model (fixed pool of virtual users that wait for a response) hides queue collapse, because slow responses throttle the load itself. Real users arrive independently — an <strong>open</strong> model (constant <em>arrival rate</em>, k6's arrival-rate executors) exposes the collapse a closed test masks. Test against a prod-like env; the first bottleneck is usually <strong>DB connections/locks</strong>, not CPU; feed results into capacity planning, autoscaling thresholds, and your SLOs (12·6).</p><figure><figcaption>closed vs open workload model</figcaption><div class="lanes"><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--blocked)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--blocked)">Closed model</div><div class="lb">Fixed VUs wait for replies — slow responses throttle load, hiding collapse.</div></div><div class="lane" style="border-color:var(--work)"><div class="lh" style="color:var(--work)">Open model</div><div class="lb">Constant arrival rate — realistic; exposes queue collapse.</div></div></div></figure><div class="callout analogy"><span class="ct">🌉 Analogy — stress-testing a bridge</span>Roll on more and more trucks before the bridge opens to the public — you learn the limit on purpose, safely, instead of finding it with a rush-hour jam.</div><div class="callout insight"><span class="ct">💡 Find the FIRST bottleneck</span>Systems break at their weakest link, and it's rarely CPU — it's usually a database connection pool, a lock, or a downstream quota. Load testing tells you which ceiling you actually hit first.</div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ct">⚠️ A closed-model test flatters you</span>Fixed virtual users that wait for each response quietly self-limit, so the test looks stable right up to a collapse real (open) traffic would trigger. Model arrival rate, not a VU count.</div><div class="cheat"><span class="ct">📝 Cheat-sheet</span><ul><li>k6 = scriptable load tests; VUs, ramps, thresholds.</li><li>Types: load (peak), stress (break), soak (leaks), spike (surge).</li><li>Watch latency percentiles + error rate vs throughput → the knee.</li><li>Open (arrival-rate) model exposes collapse a closed model hides.</li><li>First bottleneck is usually DB connections/locks, not CPU; feeds capacity + SLOs.</li></ul></div><div class="selfcheck"><span class="ct">✅ Self-check</span><ol><li>Why load-test before a launch instead of after?</li><li>What does an open (arrival-rate) model reveal that a closed one hides?</li><li>Where's the first bottleneck usually found?</li></ol></div></section><div class="foot">🔱 Cosmos — Learning Notes · COMPLETE · all 13 modules taught as full lessons (Foundations 0.5 + Stages 0–12) · analogies, diagrams, cheat-sheets &amp; self-checks throughout</div>
